(window.__DP=window.__DP||[])[5]="sverse incision usually causes more bleeding than a classical incision\",\"A lower-segment transverse incision is generally easier to repair and carries a lower future rupture risk than a classical incision\",\"A lower-segment transverse incision has a higher subsequent rupture risk than a classical incision\",\"A classical uterine incision is preferred routinely for term cephalic presentations\",\"A classical incision is the standard choice whenever a previous cesarean has been performed\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The lower uterine segment is relatively less vascular at term, so a transverse incision usually causes less hemorrhage than a classical vertical incision.\",\"A low transverse uterine incision is usually easier to repair and is associated with less bleeding and a substantially lower risk of rupture in a future pregnancy than a classical incision.\",\"Classical incisions traverse the contractile upper segment and therefore carry the greater risk of subsequent uterine rupture.\",\"A classical incision is reserved for selected situations such as an inaccessible lower segment, certain transverse lies, or some very preterm presentations rather than routine term cephalic birth.\",\"Most repeat cesareans can again use a low transverse uterine incision unless anatomy, placentation, adhesions, or another indication requires a different approach.\"],\"e\":[\"The low transverse uterine incision is preferred for most cesarean births because it heals well and generally causes less bleeding.\",\"Compared with a classical incision, it is easier to repair and carries a lower future uterine-rupture risk.\",\"The classical incision enters the contractile upper uterine segment, where subsequent labor produces greater tensile stress on the myometrial scar.\"],\"tk\":\"Low transverse cesarean incisions have lower bleeding and future rupture risk than classical incisions.\",\"pr\":\"A prior classical cesarean incision is generally a contraindication to labor because scar rupture risk is substantially higher than after a prior low transverse incision.\",\"tp\":\"Review the previous operative report rather than assuming the uterine incision type from the skin scar.\",\"cn\":\"A lower segment transverse caesarean incision bleeds less, is easier to repair and has lower future rupture risk than a classical incision.\",\"sl\":\"Uterine rupture incidence: unscarred uterus 0.5-2.0 per 10,000; after classical caesarean section (C/S) 5%; after lower uterine segment caesarean section (LUSCS) 0.5%.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[55]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F480\"},{\"id\":\"F481\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Intermenstrual bleeding differential\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition is LEAST likely to be a primary cause of intermenstrual bleeding?\",\"o\":[\"Thrombocytopenia\",\"Hormone replacement therapy\",\"Endometrial or cervical polyp\",\"Endometriosis\",\"Cervicitis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Thrombocytopenia can produce abnormal mucosal bleeding, including irregular uterine bleeding, particularly when the platelet count is markedly reduced.\",\"Systemic hormone replacement therapy (HRT) can cause unscheduled or breakthrough bleeding, especially during initiation or with an imbalanced estrogen-progestogen regimen.\",\"Endometrial and cervical polyps are classic structural causes of bleeding between otherwise expected menstrual periods.\",\"Endometriosis more typically causes cyclic pelvic pain, dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and subfertility rather than isolated intermenstrual bleeding.\",\"Cervicitis can make the cervix friable and cause contact or intermenstrual bleeding, often with discharge.\"],\"e\":[\"Intermenstrual bleeding should prompt consideration of cervical, endometrial, iatrogenic, and hematologic causes.\",\"Polyps, cervicitis, HRT-related breakthrough bleeding, and bleeding disorders are established causes; endometriosis usually presents with pain rather than isolated bleeding between periods.\",\"The evaluation is directed by pregnancy status, cervical findings, medication exposure, and risk factors for endometrial pathology.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate intermenstrual bleeding for structural, cervical, iatrogenic, and hematologic causes rather than attributing it automatically to endometriosis.\",\"pr\":\"Postcoital bleeding particularly raises concern for cervical ectropion, cervicitis, polyps, or cervical neoplasia.\",\"tp\":\"Perform a pregnancy test and inspect the cervix when intermenstrual bleeding is new, persistent, or associated with discharge or contact bleeding.\",\"cn\":\"Endometriosis rarely presents as abnormal bleeding; intermenstrual bleeding (IMB) is usually from polyps, cervicitis, iatrogenic hormones or coagulopathy.\",\"sl\":\"Intermenstrual bleeding (IMB) causes: infection (cervicitis), iatrogenic breakthrough bleeding, polyps and fibroids, malignancy. Coagulopathy includes thrombocytopenia. Endometriosis rarely presents as abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) and is identified in under 5% of cases.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[42,47,49]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F481\"},{\"id\":\"F482\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Placental abruption severity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with suspected placental abruption has vaginal bleeding and an abnormal fetal heart-rate tracing, but her blood pressure and pulse remain normal. Which clinical grade best fits this presentation?\",\"o\":[\"Grade 0\",\"Grade 1\",\"Grade 2\",\"Grade 3\",\"Placenta previa rather than abruption\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Grade 0 abruption is generally recognized retrospectively by a small retroplacental clot after delivery rather than by active bleeding with fetal compromise.\",\"Grade 1 abruption is mild, with maternal stability and no significant fetal distress.\",\"Grade 2 abruption classically includes fetal distress with maternal vital signs still relatively preserved, matching abnormal cardiotocography (CTG) without maternal shock.\",\"Grade 3 abruption is severe and is associated with maternal shock, fetal death, coagulopathy, or marked uterine tetany.\",\"Placenta previa typically causes painless bleeding and does not explain the grading pattern of abruption with fetal compromise.\"],\"e\":[\"Clinical grading of placental abruption reflects the severity of maternal and fetal compromise rather than the visible volume of bleeding alone.\",\"Abnormal fetal status with preserved maternal vital signs is characteristic of moderate, grade 2 abruption.\",\"Concealed retroplacental bleeding can reduce uteroplacental perfusion before maternal shock becomes obvious, so fetal heart-rate abnormalities may precede hemodynamic collapse.\"],\"tk\":\"Grade 2 placental abruption may cause fetal distress despite preserved maternal vital signs.\",\"pr\":\"The amount of visible vaginal bleeding can underestimate severity because a substantial abruption may be concealed behind the placenta.\",\"tp\":\"Stabilize the mother first, obtain continuous fetal monitoring when viable, and assess coagulation studies when moderate or severe abruption is suspected.\",\"cn\":\"Grade 2 placental abruption has fetal distress but no maternal shock; grade 3 adds maternal shock or fetal death.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F482\"},{\"id\":\"F483\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Timing of birth after PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"According to the current Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) approach, a clinically stable woman with preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) at 32 weeks and a reassuring fetal assessment has no infection or other indication for birth. If stability continues, until what gestation can expectant management be offered?\",\"o\":[\"Immediately\",\"34 weeks\",\"36 weeks\",\"37 weeks\",\"40 weeks\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Immediate delivery is indicated for chorioamnionitis, significant bleeding, fetal compromise, established labor, or maternal instability rather than uncomplicated stable PPROM at 32 weeks.\",\"Some protocols consider delivery from 34 weeks, but current Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists (RCOG) guidance supports continued expectant management beyond 34 weeks when no contraindication exists.\",\"Routine delivery at 36 weeks is not the stated RCOG endpoint for uncomplicated PPROM.\",\"RCOG recommends offering expectant management until 37+0 weeks when PPROM is uncomplicated and there is no contraindication to continuing pregnancy.\",\"Continuing to 40 weeks after PPROM would expose mother and fetus to prolonged infection-related risk and is not the recommended expectant limit.\"],\"e\":[\"Stable PPROM requires balancing complications of prematurity against infection, abruption, and cord-related risks.\",\"Under current RCOG guidance, women without contraindications can be offered expectant management until 37+0 weeks with surveillance and appropriate adjunctive therapy.\",\"Birth becomes indicated earlier if infection, labor, bleeding, maternal deterioration, or fetal compromise shifts the risk-benefit balance.\"],\"tk\":\"Uncomplicated PPROM can be managed expectantly to 37 weeks under the RCOG pathway.\",\"pr\":\"After PPROM, erythromycin is the preferred latency antibiotic in RCOG guidance, given for up to 10 days or until established labor, whichever occurs first.\",\"tp\":\"Avoid routine digital vaginal examination in PPROM unless labor is established because it can shorten latency and increase infection risk.\",\"cn\":\"Stable preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) after 24+0 weeks without contraindications can be managed expectantly until 37+0 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM) after 24+0 weeks' gestation with no contraindications to continuing the pregnancy should be offered expectant management until 37+0 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[14]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F483\"},{\"id\":\"F484\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Prognosis\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Spontaneous labor after term PROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"At term, approximately what proportion of women with prelabour rupture of membranes will go into labour spontaneously within 24 hours according to National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) guidance?\",\"o\":[\"60%\",\"95%\",\"20%\",\"10%\",\"80%\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) advises that about 60% of women with term prelabour rupture of membranes will enter labor spontaneously within 24 hours.\",\"A figure around 95% has been reported for spontaneous labor over a broader interval, whereas NICE gives about 60% for the strict 24-hour window.\",\"Twenty percent substantially underestimates the proportion who begin labor spontaneously during the first day after membrane rupture.\",\"Ten percent is far below the observed spontaneous-labor rate after term PROM.\",\"Eighty percent exceeds the NICE estimate for the strict 24-hour interval.\"],\"e\":[\"At term, membrane rupture often precedes spontaneous contractions, and about 60% begin labor within 24 hours in NICE guidance.\",\"The source wording said “give birth,” but the guideline statistic refers to onset of labor, so the stem has been clarified to avoid conflating labor onset with delivery.\",\"As the duration of ruptured membranes increases, the risk of ascending infection rises, which is why induction is offered if labor has not begun after approximately 24 hours.\"],\"tk\":\"About 60% of women with term PROM enter labor spontaneously within 24 hours according to NICE.\",\"pr\":\"At term PROM, NICE offers either immediate induction or expectant management for up to 24 hours, using shared decision-making.\",\"tp\":\"Check group B streptococcus (GBS) status because a positive current-pregnancy GBS test changes management toward immediate induction or planned cesarean if otherwise indicated.\",\"cn\":\"About 60% of women with term prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM) go into labour spontaneously within 24 hours.\",\"sl\":\"Spontaneous onset of labour after term prelabour rupture of membranes (PROM) usually follows within 24 hours; median latency after preterm PROM is 7 days.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[3]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F484\"},{\"id\":\"F485\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":37,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT a recognized risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse?\",\"o\":[\"Obesity\",\"Abdominal wall defects\",\"Multiparity\",\"Chronic constipation\",\"Repeated heavy lifting\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Obesity increases chronic downward pressure on pelvic support structures and is associated with pelvic organ prolapse.\",\"General abdominal wall defects are not an established primary risk factor for pelvic organ prolapse in the way childbirth trauma and chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure are.\",\"Multiparity, particularly repeated vaginal birth, can damage levator and connective-tissue support and is a major risk factor.\",\"Chronic constipation causes repetitive straining and sustained increases in intra-abdominal pressure that can worsen pelvic support failure.\",\"Repeated heavy lifting increases intra-abdominal pressure and is associated with prolapse symptoms in susceptible women.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic organ prolapse develops when levator, fascial, and apical supports fail under cumulative mechanical stress.\",\"Multiparity, obesity, chronic constipation, and repeated heavy lifting are recognized contributors; abdominal wall defects are not a standard primary risk factor.\",\"Childbirth-related neuromuscular injury and repeated elevations in intra-abdominal pressure progressively weaken pelvic support.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize parity and chronically increased intra-abdominal pressure as major prolapse risk factors.\",\"pr\":\"Operative vaginal birth and levator ani avulsion are particularly associated with later pelvic-floor dysfunction.\",\"tp\":\"Address constipation, weight, chronic cough, and lifting technique when counseling patients using conservative prolapse management.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) risk factors are parity, constipation and straining, heavy lifting, obesity and chronic pulmonary disease; not abdominal wall defects.\",\"sl\":\"Etiology: parity is the strongest risk factor; constipation and straining (61% vs 4% develop POP); heavy lifting, obesity and chronic pulmonary disease (increase abdominal pressure).\",\"s\":[[\"L37_0\",[8,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F120\"},{\"id\":\"F486\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Risk of Malignancy Index calculation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has a cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) level of 300 U/mL and an adnexal mass with one risk of malignancy index (RMI) ultrasound-risk feature (multilocularity). Using RMI I, what is her Risk of Malignancy Index?\",\"o\":[\"2700\",\"4500\",\"900\",\"3600\",\"1800\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An RMI of 2700 would require a larger ultrasound multiplier than is assigned for a single ultrasound-risk feature in RMI I.\",\"A value of 4500 is not obtained from the standard U × M × CA-125 calculation with U=1 and M=3.\",\"With U=1, postmenopausal M=3, and CA-125=300 U/mL, RMI I is 1 × 3 × 300 = 900.\",\"A value of 3600 does not correspond to the standard RMI I weighting for this combination of menopausal status and ultrasound score.\",\"An RMI of 1800 would imply a nonstandard multiplier and overestimates the calculated score.\"],\"e\":[\"RMI I combines the ultrasound score, menopausal status, and CA-125 concentration.\",\"One qualifying ultrasound feature gives U=1, postmenopausal status gives M=3, and 1 × 3 × 300 yields 900.\",\"The index increases cancer probability by combining independent clinical signals rather than relying on CA-125 alone, which can be elevated in benign conditions.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate RMI I as ultrasound score × menopausal score × CA-125.\",\"pr\":\"In RMI I, two or more ultrasound-risk features increase the ultrasound multiplier to U=3.\",\"tp\":\"Refer a postmenopausal patient with a suspicious complex adnexal mass through the local gynecologic-oncology pathway rather than using CA-125 in isolation.\",\"cn\":\"Risk of Malignancy Index (RMI) = U x M x cancer antigen 125 (CA-125); one ultrasound feature gives U=1, postmenopause M=3.\",\"sl\":\"RMI = U x M x CA-125. Complex cyst features: ascites, bilateral lesions, septations, solid components or papillary projections, metastases.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[14,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F486\"},{\"id\":\"F487\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate toxicity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about magnesium sulfate use for severe preeclampsia or eclampsia is false?\",\"o\":[\"A commonly cited therapeutic serum range is approximately 4–6 mEq/L\",\"Serum magnesium measurement is particularly important when renal impairment raises concern for accumulation\",\"A common regimen uses a 4-g intravenous (IV) loading dose followed by a maintenance infusion\",\"Magnesium toxicity causes an increased respiratory rate\",\"Calcium gluconate is used to treat clinically significant magnesium toxicity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A serum magnesium concentration around 4–6 mEq/L is commonly cited as therapeutic during seizure prophylaxis, although clinical monitoring remains central.\",\"Because magnesium is renally excreted, impaired renal function increases toxicity risk and strengthens the indication for serum-level monitoring.\",\"A 4-g intravenous loading dose followed by maintenance infusion is a standard regimen used in many obstetric protocols.\",\"Magnesium toxicity causes respiratory depression, not tachypnea; progressive toxicity also causes loss of deep-tendon reflexes and cardiac conduction abnormalities.\",\"Calcium gluconate antagonizes magnesium's neuromuscular and cardiac effects and is the standard antidote for significant toxicity.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate is first-line therapy for eclamptic seizures and is used for seizure prophylaxis when indicated in severe preeclampsia.\",\"Toxicity produces loss of reflexes and respiratory depression, making an increased respiratory rate the false statement.\",\"Magnesium reduces neuromuscular transmission and central excitability; accumulation is more likely with renal impairment because renal clearance is the main route of elimination.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize respiratory depression and loss of reflexes as magnesium toxicity, with calcium gluconate as the antidote.\",\"pr\":\"Urine output is clinically important during magnesium therapy because oliguria can rapidly increase serum magnesium concentrations.\",\"tp\":\"Monitor respiratory rate, patellar reflexes, urine output, and level of consciousness while magnesium sulfate is running.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium sulphate toxicity causes respiratory depression (decreased respiratory rate), not tachypnoea; therapeutic range is 4-6 mEq/L.\",\"sl\":\"Dose: 4 g bolus over 5-15 minutes then 1 g/h for 24 hours; therapeutic range 4 to 6 mEq/L, check serum level 4 hours after loading. Toxicity: loss of patellar reflexes (8-10 mEq/L), respiratory depression or arrest (12 mEq/L); treat with 1 g intravenous (IV) calcium gluconate.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[45,46,47]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F204\"},{\"id\":\"F488\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal bleeding with thin endometrium\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has one light episode of bleeding. Transvaginal ultrasound shows a 2-mm endometrium, and the bleeding has stopped. What is the most appropriate next step?\",\"o\":[\"No immediate endometrial sampling; reassure and advise reassessment if bleeding recurs\",\"Start oral progesterone\",\"Insert a levonorgestrel intrauterine system\",\"Perform endometrial biopsy immediately in all cases\",\"Start systemic estrogen therapy\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A 2-mm endometrial thickness after a first, self-limited episode is highly reassuring, so immediate sampling is unnecessary if there are no other compelling concerns; recurrent bleeding still requires reassessment.\",\"Empiric progesterone does not replace evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding and is not needed after a reassuring initial assessment.\",\"A levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) is used for selected endometrial or bleeding indications but is not the next step after an isolated episode with a very thin endometrium.\",\"Routine immediate biopsy is not required after an initial episode when high-quality transvaginal ultrasound (TVUS) shows a thin endometrium and symptoms resolve.\",\"Systemic estrogen can stimulate the endometrium and has no role in treating unexplained postmenopausal bleeding.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal bleeding requires evaluation because endometrial carcinoma must be excluded, even when bleeding is scant.\",\"A clearly visualized endometrium of 2 mm is below the usual reassuring threshold, so immediate biopsy can be deferred after a single resolved episode with safety-netting.\",\"A thin endometrial stripe has a very high negative predictive value for cancer, but persistent or recurrent bleeding overrides thickness alone and warrants tissue evaluation.\"],\"tk\":\"A thin endometrium after a first resolved postmenopausal bleeding (PMB) episode can allow observation without immediate biopsy, provided recurrent bleeding triggers reassessment.\",\"pr\":\"Tamoxifen exposure, inability to visualize the endometrium, or recurrent bleeding lowers the threshold for hysteroscopy or biopsy.\",\"tp\":\"Document explicit return precautions: any recurrent postmenopausal bleeding should prompt repeat assessment even after an initially reassuring ultrasound.\",\"cn\":\"After a single postmenopausal bleed with endometrium under 4 mm on transvaginal ultrasound, biopsy is not routinely needed; reassess if bleeding recurs.\",\"sl\":\"Ultrasound cut-off of 4 mm is used; if the endometrial stripe is thin, no need for histological diagnosis (atrophic endometrium). Endometrial biopsy is required if endometrial thickness (ET) is more than 4 mm and may be selectively performed below 4 mm if other risk factors are present.\",\"s\":[[\"L42_0\",[19]],[\"L33_0\",[57]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F488\"},{\"id\":\"F489\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Physiologic ECG changes in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which ECG finding is NOT considered a typical physiologic change of pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"T-wave inversion in some inferior or lateral leads\",\"T-wave inversion in lead III\",\"Occasional ventricular extrasystoles\",\"Leftward shift of the electrical axis, especially later in pregnancy\",\"ST-segment elevation in inferior and lateral leads\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Minor T-wave flattening or inversion can occur during pregnancy as cardiac position and repolarization patterns change.\",\"T-wave inversion in lead III is a recognized benign pregnancy-associated ECG variant in the appropriate clinical setting.\",\"Isolated premature ventricular beats may occur in otherwise healthy pregnant patients and are often benign when structural heart disease is absent.\",\"The enlarging uterus elevates the diaphragm and can shift the heart, producing a modest leftward electrical-axis shift.\",\"New ST-segment elevation in contiguous inferior or lateral leads should prompt evaluation for myocardial ischemia, pericarditis, or another pathologic process rather than being dismissed as physiologic.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy changes cardiac position, plasma volume, heart rate, and autonomic tone, producing several benign ECG variants.\",\"Minor axis shift, selected T-wave changes, and ectopy may occur, whereas ST-segment elevation in contiguous leads is not a normal expected finding.\",\"Physiologic repolarization changes are usually modest; true ST elevation reflects altered myocardial electrical injury currents and requires clinical correlation.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not regard new regional ST-segment elevation as a normal ECG change of pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Pregnancy increases heart rate by roughly 10–20 beats/min and may shorten the PR and QT intervals modestly.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret ECG abnormalities in the context of symptoms; chest pain, dyspnea, syncope, or persistent arrhythmia warrants prompt cardiovascular assessment.\",\"cn\":\"ST-segment elevation is not a normal pregnancy electrocardiogram (ECG) change; left axis shift, T inversion in lead III and ectopics are physiological.\",\"sl\":\"Electrocardiogram (ECG) changes of normal pregnancy: left axis shift (about 15 degrees) from cardiac elevation; T inversion in III (+/- aVF) and small Q waves in III and aVF; increased heart rate with more frequent atrial and ventricular ectopics.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F489\"},{\"id\":\"F490\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":32,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"WHO semen analysis reference values\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Using contemporary World Health Organization (WHO) semen-analysis reference distributions, which statement is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Progressive motility around 30% corresponds to the lower fifth-centile reference value\",\"Normal morphology around 4% is the lower fifth-centile reference value\",\"A semen pH below 7.1 is considered a normal reference finding\",\"Sperm vitality around 54% is near the lower fifth-centile reference value\",\"Total motility around 42% is near the lower fifth-centile reference value\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The WHO sixth edition reports a lower fifth-centile value of about 30% progressive motility in fertile-reference populations.\",\"Strict morphology remains low numerically, with approximately 4% normal forms near the lower fifth-centile reference value.\",\"A semen pH below 7.2 is abnormal and can suggest reduced seminal-vesicle contribution or urine contamination; a value below 7.1 is not a normal reference finding.\",\"Sperm vitality near 54% live spermatozoa is close to the lower fifth-centile value in the current WHO reference distribution.\",\"Total motility near 42% is close to the lower fifth-centile value reported in the sixth-edition reference population.\"],\"e\":[\"The WHO sixth edition emphasizes reference distributions rather than rigid fertility cutoffs, because semen values overlap between fertile and infertile men.\",\"Approximate lower fifth-centile values include progressive motility 30%, total motility 42%, vitality 54%, and morphology 4%; pH below 7.2 is abnormal.\",\"Semen parameters must be interpreted together because no single threshold can by itself establish or exclude male-factor infertility.\"],\"tk\":\"Interpret semen analysis using current WHO reference distributions and recognize low semen pH as abnormal.\",\"pr\":\"An abnormal first semen analysis should generally be repeated because substantial biologic variation occurs between ejaculates.\",\"tp\":\"Check abstinence interval, collection completeness, transport time, and temperature before interpreting a borderline semen analysis.\",\"cn\":\"Normal semen pH is 7.2 or more, so a pH below 7.1 is abnormal.\",\"sl\":\"Semen analysis (World Health Organization [WHO] 2010): pH 7.2 or more; total motility 40% or more or 32% progressive; vitality 58%; morphology 4% or more. The question's other values are the newer WHO sixth-edition figures.\",\"s\":[[\"L32_0\",[13]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F490\"},{\"id\":\"F491\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Hemostatic targets in obstetric hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During major obstetric hemorrhage, which proposed hemostatic resuscitation target is inappropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Keep PT less than about 1.5 times normal\",\"Accept a platelet count of 20 × 10⁹/L as an adequate target\",\"Keep APTT less than about 1.5 times normal\",\"Maintain fibrinogen above about 2 g/L\",\"Use active warming during hemorrhage resuscitation\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Aiming for PT below about 1.5 times normal is consistent with correction of clinically important coagulation-factor deficiency during major hemorrhage.\",\"A platelet count of 20 × 10⁹/L is dangerously low in active obstetric bleeding; substantially higher platelet levels are targeted to support hemostasis.\",\"Keeping APTT below about 1.5 times normal is a standard practical goal when correcting dilutional or consumptive coagulopathy.\",\"Pregnancy begins with high fibrinogen levels, and a concentration above 2 g/L is an important target during major obstetric hemorrhage.\",\"Low body temperature impairs enzymatic coagulation and platelet function, so active warming helps preserve effective hemostasis during major hemorrhage resuscitation.\"],\"e\":[\"Major obstetric hemorrhage can rapidly produce dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy, especially when fibrinogen falls.\",\"A platelet target as low as 20 × 10⁹/L is unsafe in active major bleeding; coagulation times and fibrinogen also require correction.\",\"Effective resuscitation restores circulating volume while preventing the lethal interaction of coagulopathy, acidosis, and hypothermia.\"],\"tk\":\"Major obstetric hemorrhage requires correction of coagulopathy and platelet depletion, not acceptance of severe thrombocytopenia.\",\"pr\":\"A falling fibrinogen concentration is an early marker of severe postpartum hemorrhage because normal pregnancy fibrinogen is physiologically elevated.\",\"tp\":\"Activate a major-hemorrhage protocol early when bleeding is ongoing, and send repeated full blood count, coagulation studies, and fibrinogen rather than waiting for clinical collapse.\",\"cn\":\"In major obstetric haemorrhage the platelet target is above 50 x 10^9/L; accepting 20 x 10^9/L is inappropriate.\",\"sl\":\"Therapeutic goals: haemoglobin (Hb) above 8 g/dL, platelets above 50 x 10^9/L, prothrombin time (PT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (APTT) less than 1.5 times normal, fibrinogen above 2 g/L. Give 1 pool of platelets if haemorrhage is ongoing and platelets are below 75 x 10^9/L.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[11,12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F491\"},{\"id\":\"F492\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mullerian anomalies and pregnancy outcomes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about congenital Müllerian duct anomalies is correct?\",\"o\":[\"They account for nearly all recurrent pregnancy loss\",\"Hysterosalpingography is routinely performed during pregnancy to define the uterine cavity\",\"Every uterine anomaly requires surgical correction before conception\",\"They are associated with recurrent pregnancy loss, preterm birth, and fetal malpresentation\",\"Three-dimensional ultrasound cannot characterize uterine morphology\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Müllerian anomalies are one recognized contributor to recurrent pregnancy loss, but they account for only a minority of cases rather than nearly all losses.\",\"Hysterosalpingography uses ionizing radiation and contrast and is used in the nonpregnant infertility evaluation, not routinely during an established pregnancy.\",\"Management depends on anomaly type and reproductive history; many variants need no surgery, and indiscriminate correction can create unnecessary harm.\",\"Congenital uterine anomalies can impair implantation or uterine capacity and are associated with miscarriage, preterm birth, and malpresentation.\",\"Three-dimensional transvaginal ultrasound can define both internal cavity and external fundal contour and is highly useful for classifying uterine anomalies.\"],\"e\":[\"Müllerian anomalies arise from abnormal development, fusion, or resorption of the paramesonephric ducts.\",\"Clinically important anomalies can increase recurrent pregnancy loss, preterm delivery, and malpresentation, although risk varies by anomaly.\",\"Distortion of the uterine cavity and reduced effective uterine volume can impair implantation or fetal accommodation, producing adverse reproductive outcomes.\"],\"tk\":\"Müllerian duct anomalies can increase miscarriage, preterm birth, and malpresentation risk.\",\"pr\":\"A septate uterus and a bicornuate uterus can look similar on cavity imaging, but assessment of the external fundal contour distinguishes them.\",\"tp\":\"Use 3D transvaginal ultrasound or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) when accurate classification will alter counseling or management; avoid hysterosalpingography (HSG) in pregnancy.\",\"cn\":\"Congenital Mullerian uterine anomalies are associated with recurrent miscarriage, preterm birth and fetal malpresentation.\",\"sl\":\"Congenital uterine anomalies are a cause of recurrent miscarriage treated by hysteroscopic septum resection; they are a risk factor for preterm labour (uterine abnormalities restricting expansion); shoulder presentation causes include uterine anomaly.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[23]],[\"L19_0\",[13,16]],[\"L16_0\",[39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F492\"},{\"id\":\"F493\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Medication safety in pregnancy — hydroxychloroquine\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is considered compatible with use during pregnancy when clinically indicated?\",\"o\":[\"Cyclophosphamide\",\"Methotrexate\",\"Mycophenolate mofetil\",\"Warfarin\",\"Hydroxychloroquine\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cyclophosphamide is cytotoxic and teratogenic, particularly during organogenesis; exceptional later-pregnancy use is reserved for severe maternal disease under specialist care.\",\"Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is contraindicated in pregnancy because exposure can cause miscarriage and a characteristic embryopathy.\",\"Mycophenolate mofetil is associated with a high risk of pregnancy loss and congenital malformations, so it should be replaced before conception when possible.\",\"Warfarin crosses the placenta and can cause fetal warfarin syndrome and fetal bleeding, although limited specialist indications such as some mechanical valves require individualized anticoagulation.\",\"Hydroxychloroquine is considered compatible with pregnancy and is commonly continued for maternal autoimmune disease because maintaining disease control improves maternal and fetal outcomes.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy medication decisions balance fetal drug toxicity against the harm of uncontrolled maternal disease.\",\"Among these agents, hydroxychloroquine has an established pregnancy-compatible role, whereas methotrexate, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, and warfarin carry important fetal hazards.\",\"Hydroxychloroquine modulates lysosomal and toll-like receptor signaling without the folate antagonism, DNA alkylation, or vitamin-K antagonism responsible for several competing drugs’ teratogenic effects.\"],\"tk\":\"Hydroxychloroquine can usually be continued during pregnancy when clinically indicated, whereas methotrexate and mycophenolate require preconception avoidance or substitution.\",\"pr\":\"Stopping effective antirheumatic therapy can provoke a maternal autoimmune flare, which itself can worsen obstetric outcomes; medication withdrawal is not automatically safer than continuation.\",\"tp\":\"Review all immunomodulators before conception or as soon as pregnancy is recognized, and coordinate any substitution with the relevant maternal-medicine and disease specialist.\",\"cn\":\"Hydroxychloroquine is compatible with pregnancy; methotrexate, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide and warfarin are teratogenic.\",\"sl\":\"Warfarin freely crosses the placenta and causes warfarin embryopathy (nasal hypoplasia, short limbs and digits). The slides do not discuss hydroxychloroquine or immunosuppressants.\",\"s\":[[\"L11_0\",[26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F216\"},{\"id\":\"F494\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":10,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Gestational diabetes screening\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about gestational diabetes mellitus are correct EXCEPT:\",\"o\":[\"Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) increases the risk of fetal macrosomia and shoulder dystocia\",\"Women with recognized risk factors should be offered appropriate testing during pregnancy\",\"Glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) is a reliable stand-alone screening test for diagnosing GDM in mid-pregnancy\",\"Previous GDM substantially increases recurrence risk in a later pregnancy\",\"Dietary modification and physical activity are initial components of management when glucose levels permit\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal hyperglycemia drives fetal hyperinsulinemia and excessive growth, increasing macrosomia and shoulder dystocia risk.\",\"Guidelines use maternal risk factors such as prior gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), obesity, and family history to identify women who need glucose testing.\",\"HbA1c lacks adequate sensitivity and pregnancy-specific performance for stand-alone GDM screening or diagnosis in mid-pregnancy.\",\"A history of GDM strongly predicts recurrence because underlying insulin resistance often persists between pregnancies.\",\"Nutrition therapy and appropriate physical activity are foundational initial measures; pharmacotherapy is added when glucose targets are not achieved.\"],\"e\":[\"Gestational diabetes reflects pregnancy-induced insulin resistance that exceeds maternal beta-cell compensatory capacity.\",\"Risk-based testing and oral glucose testing are used for diagnosis, while HbA1c alone is not a reliable GDM screening test during pregnancy.\",\"Maternal hyperglycemia crosses the placenta, provoking fetal insulin secretion and promoting excessive fetal growth and neonatal metabolic complications.\"],\"tk\":\"Do not use HbA1c alone to screen for or diagnose gestational diabetes in mid-pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"After GDM, postpartum glucose testing is important because affected women have a substantially increased lifetime risk of type 2 diabetes.\",\"tp\":\"When reviewing a glucose result, confirm which diagnostic strategy and thresholds your institution uses rather than mixing 75-g and 100-g OGTT criteria.\",\"cn\":\"Haemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) cannot diagnose gestational diabetes; use the 75 g oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) at 24-28 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Offer women with any risk factor a 75 g 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) at 24-28 weeks. Gestational diabetes increases macrosomia and shoulder dystocia; management begins with diet and exercise, adding metformin if targets are not met in 1-2 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L10_0\",[25,26,27,28,31,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F494\"},{\"id\":\"F495\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Oxytocin at cesarean birth\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"For routine uterotonic prophylaxis at cesarean birth, what oxytocin dose is recommended by National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE)?\",\"o\":[\"5 IU by slow intravenous injection\",\"10 IU intramuscularly only\",\"20 IU as a rapid intravenous bolus\",\"40 IU intramuscularly\",\"50 IU as a rapid intravenous bolus\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends oxytocin 5 IU by slow intravenous injection at cesarean birth to promote uterine contraction and reduce hemorrhage.\",\"Ten units intramuscularly is used in some third-stage uterotonic regimens after vaginal birth, but the route and context differ from routine cesarean prophylaxis.\",\"A rapid 20-IU intravenous bolus risks marked hypotension and tachycardia and exceeds the recommended bolus dose.\",\"Forty units intramuscularly is not a routine prophylactic dose and would be an inappropriate route-dose combination.\",\"A 50-IU rapid bolus is unsafe and far above recommended immediate prophylactic dosing.\"],\"e\":[\"Active uterotonic prophylaxis after cesarean birth reduces the risk of uterine-atony hemorrhage.\",\"NICE recommends 5 IU oxytocin by slow IV injection during cesarean birth.\",\"Slow administration limits abrupt vasodilation and cardiovascular effects while stimulating myometrial oxytocin receptors to produce sustained uterine contraction.\"],\"tk\":\"At cesarean birth, NICE recommends oxytocin 5 IU by slow intravenous injection.\",\"pr\":\"Large rapid IV oxytocin boluses can cause hypotension, tachycardia, and myocardial oxygen-demand changes.\",\"tp\":\"If uterine tone remains poor after prophylaxis, escalate promptly using the local postpartum-hemorrhage uterotonic sequence while checking for retained tissue and trauma.\",\"cn\":\"For postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) prevention at caesarean section, oxytocin 5 IU is given by slow intravenous (IV) injection.\",\"sl\":\"Uterotonics for prevention of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH): 10 IU oxytocin intramuscularly (IM) after vaginal delivery, 5 IU oxytocin intravenously (IV) after caesarean section (CS).\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F353\"},{\"id\":\"F496\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Malpresentation management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which obstetric presentation-management pair is correctly matched?\",\"o\":[\"Persistent mentum-posterior face presentation — routine vaginal delivery\",\"Persistent brow presentation in labor — cesarean delivery\",\"Cord prolapse with a high unengaged presenting part — expectant observation\",\"Shoulder dystocia — fundal pressure as first-line treatment\",\"Persistent transverse lie in labor — vacuum extraction\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Persistent mentum-posterior face presentation usually cannot deliver vaginally because the fetal neck cannot extend sufficiently beneath the pubic symphysis.\",\"A persistent brow presentation presents the largest cephalic diameter and generally requires cesarean delivery when it does not convert.\",\"Cord prolapse with a high presenting part threatens acute cord compression and usually requires urgent delivery rather than observation.\",\"Fundal pressure can worsen impaction in shoulder dystocia; McRoberts positioning with suprapubic pressure is the initial maneuver sequence.\",\"A persistent transverse lie has no cephalic presenting part suitable for vacuum extraction and usually requires cesarean delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"Management of malpresentation depends on whether the presenting diameter can safely traverse the pelvis and whether urgent fetal compromise is present.\",\"Persistent brow presentation usually cannot deliver vaginally because the mentovertical diameter is too large.\",\"Cesarean delivery bypasses the mechanical obstruction when spontaneous conversion does not occur, preventing prolonged obstructed labor and fetal injury.\"],\"tk\":\"Persistent brow presentation generally requires cesarean delivery.\",\"pr\":\"Face presentation can sometimes deliver vaginally when the mentum is anterior, whereas persistent mentum posterior usually cannot.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm presentation and position clinically and with ultrasound when uncertain before committing to operative vaginal delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Persistent brow presentation cannot deliver vaginally and is managed by caesarean section.\",\"sl\":\"A vaginal delivery of a persistent brow presentation is not possible and it is managed by caesarean section. Mentum posterior face presentation should be managed by caesarean delivery; shoulder presentation (transverse lie) should be delivered by caesarean section.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[36,37,39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F496\"},{\"id\":\"F497\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":38,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Adenomyosis — presentation and epidemiology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about adenomyosis is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Medical treatment is an appropriate initial approach for symptom control in many patients\",\"Heavy menstrual bleeding and dysmenorrhea are common presentations\",\"The uterus may be diffusely enlarged, tender, and boggy on examination\",\"Adenomyosis is associated more often with increasing age and prior parity\",\"Nulliparity is a characteristic epidemiologic association\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Medical treatment with non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) or hormonal suppression is commonly used initially for symptomatic adenomyosis when uterine preservation is desired.\",\"Adenomyosis commonly causes heavy menstrual bleeding and secondary dysmenorrhea because ectopic endometrial glands and stroma are embedded within the myometrium.\",\"Diffuse myometrial involvement can produce a symmetrically enlarged, tender, boggy uterus on pelvic examination.\",\"Adenomyosis is classically encountered more often with increasing reproductive age and prior pregnancy/parity, although it can occur in younger or nulliparous patients.\",\"Nulliparity is not the characteristic historical association of adenomyosis; the classic pattern is described more often after prior pregnancy and parity.\"],\"e\":[\"Adenomyosis is the presence of endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium and commonly produces heavy bleeding and painful menses.\",\"Appropriate medical symptom control is compatible with adenomyosis, whereas nulliparity is not the classic epidemiologic association.\",\"Repeated myometrial injury and altered endometrial–myometrial junction biology are proposed contributors, helping explain associations with prior pregnancy and uterine procedures.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize adenomyosis by heavy painful menses with a diffusely enlarged tender uterus, and distinguish its classic associations from those of endometriosis.\",\"pr\":\"Transvaginal ultrasound may show myometrial heterogeneity, fan-shaped shadowing, myometrial cysts, or an asymmetric thickened myometrium; magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can clarify uncertain cases.\",\"tp\":\"For a patient who wants uterine preservation, tailor treatment to bleeding, pain, and fertility goals; levonorgestrel intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) and other hormonal options can provide substantial symptom control.\",\"cn\":\"Adenomyosis is associated with high parity and later reproductive age, not nulliparity.\",\"sl\":\"Adenomyosis occurs most often in multiparous women at the end of their reproductive life; risk factors are high parity and vigorous curettage of the uterus.\",\"s\":[[\"L38_0\",[48,50]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F497\"},{\"id\":\"F498\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy — presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is correct?\",\"o\":[\"A primary urticarial eruption is the usual presenting skin finding\",\"Serum bile acids remain normal despite persistent disease\",\"Pruritus without a primary rash typically develops in the second or third trimester\",\"Fetal risk is unrelated to the maternal bile-acid concentration\",\"A normal cardiotocography (CTG) reliably excludes the risk of sudden fetal compromise\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP) causes pruritus without a primary rash; excoriations can appear secondarily from scratching, whereas urticaria suggests another dermatosis.\",\"The biochemical hallmark is elevation of serum bile acids; an initially normal result can require repeat testing when pruritus persists.\",\"ICP most often presents in late pregnancy with pruritus, frequently involving the palms and soles, in the absence of a primary skin eruption.\",\"Adverse fetal risk rises with increasing maternal bile-acid concentration, so biochemical severity influences counseling and delivery planning.\",\"CTG can assess current fetal status but cannot reliably predict the sudden fetal events associated with ICP.\"],\"e\":[\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy causes maternal pruritus without a primary rash and is diagnosed in conjunction with elevated serum bile acids after excluding other causes.\",\"Late-pregnancy pruritus without a primary eruption is characteristic; excoriations may arise secondarily from scratching.\",\"Accumulation of bile acids from pregnancy-related impairment of hepatobiliary transport is linked to fetal complications, so biochemical severity influences management.\"],\"tk\":\"Suspect intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy when pruritus occurs without a primary rash in later pregnancy, and confirm with serum bile-acid testing.\",\"pr\":\"Pruritus can precede abnormal laboratory results, so repeat bile acids and liver tests when symptoms persist despite initially normal testing.\",\"tp\":\"Ask specifically about palmar and plantar itching, review medications and liver disease, and escalate promptly if jaundice, hypertension, abdominal pain, or other features suggest an alternative diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy causes pruritus without a primary rash, typically in the late second or third trimester.\",\"sl\":\"Obstetric cholestasis most commonly presents in the third trimester (around 30-32 weeks) with pruritus without skin rash other than excoriations, especially palms and soles. Ultrasound and cardiotocography (CTG) are not reliable for preventing fetal death.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[17,19,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F472\"},{\"id\":\"F499\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":43,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Lichen sclerosus — vulvar presentation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has chronic intense vulvar pruritus and thin white plaques involving the labia majora. Which diagnosis is most likely?\",\"o\":[\"Sexually transmitted genital infection\",\"Lichen planus\",\"Allergic contact dermatitis\",\"Lichen sclerosus\",\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Genital herpes or other sexually transmitted infections more often produce vesicles, ulcers, discharge, or focal inflammatory lesions rather than chronic thin white plaques.\",\"Erosive lichen planus often causes painful glazed erythema or erosions with possible vaginal involvement; the described lesion instead has white atrophic plaques.\",\"Contact dermatitis usually causes erythema, burning, scaling, or excoriation after irritant or allergen exposure.\",\"Lichen sclerosus classically causes severe vulvar pruritus with porcelain-white atrophic plaques and carries an increased risk of vulvar squamous-cell carcinoma.\",\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis usually causes intense itch with vulvar erythema and thick curdy discharge rather than chronic atrophic plaques.\"],\"e\":[\"Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory vulvar dermatosis characterized by pruritus and porcelain-white atrophic plaques, often with architectural change.\",\"Chronic itching plus white labial plaques provides the decisive clinical pattern.\",\"Persistent inflammation and epithelial injury can lead to scarring and squamous-cell carcinoma, which is why suspicious, thickened, ulcerated, or nonhealing areas require histologic assessment.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize lichen sclerosus from chronic vulvar pruritus with white atrophic plaques and remember its association with vulvar squamous-cell carcinoma.\",\"pr\":\"Progressive disease can cause loss of normal vulvar architecture, including resorption of the labia minora and clitoral hood scarring.\",\"tp\":\"Treat confirmed uncomplicated disease with an ultrapotent topical corticosteroid and arrange follow-up; biopsy any atypical or treatment-resistant lesion.\",\"cn\":\"Lichen sclerosus presents with chronic intense vulvar itching and thin, white, atrophic plaques, and predisposes to vulvar squamous cancer.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F499\"},{\"id\":\"F500\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":42,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Endometrial carcinoma — lymphatic spread\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In endometrial carcinoma, lymphatic spread can extend from the pelvic nodes to which regional nodal basin?\",\"o\":[\"Paraaortic lymph nodes\",\"Inguinal lymph nodes\",\"Supraclavicular lymph nodes\",\"Axillary lymph nodes\",\"Popliteal lymph nodes\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Endometrial carcinoma spreads through pelvic lymphatics and subsequently to paraaortic nodes; deep myometrial invasion increases the likelihood of nodal dissemination.\",\"Inguinal nodes are more directly involved by lower vulvar and distal vaginal lymphatic drainage and are not the usual regional continuation of uterine corpus lymphatic spread.\",\"Supraclavicular nodes represent distant lymphatic disease and are not the expected regional nodal basin for uterine corpus carcinoma.\",\"Axillary nodes primarily drain the breast and upper limb and have no routine role in regional staging of endometrial carcinoma.\",\"Popliteal nodes drain structures of the distal lower limb and are unrelated to uterine corpus lymphatic pathways.\"],\"e\":[\"Endometrial carcinoma spreads by direct extension, transtubal exfoliation, lymphatic dissemination, and hematogenous routes.\",\"When lymphatic disease progresses beyond pelvic nodes, the next important regional basin is the paraaortic lymph nodes.\",\"Uterine lymphatics reach pelvic nodes first and can continue to paraaortic nodes, particularly with deep myometrial invasion and other high-risk pathologic features.\"],\"tk\":\"Know that endometrial carcinoma can spread from pelvic to paraaortic lymph nodes, a pathway incorporated into regional staging and treatment planning.\",\"pr\":\"Hacker & Moore notes that isolated paraaortic nodal metastasis is uncommon, while paraaortic involvement is substantially more frequent when pelvic nodes are already positive.\",\"tp\":\"When staging high-risk endometrial cancer, review imaging and operative findings for pelvic and paraaortic nodal disease rather than treating all nodal basins as equivalent.\",\"cn\":\"Endometrial carcinoma spreads by lymphatics first to pelvic nodes, then to paraaortic lymph nodes.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F500\"},{\"id\":\"F501\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":5,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Immediate postpartum assessment — uterine tone and bleeding\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which observation is routinely important for all women during the immediate postpartum period?\",\"o\":[\"Maintain an indwelling urinary catheter for 24 hours after every epidural\",\"Regularly assess uterine tone and the amount of vaginal bleeding\",\"Require spontaneous voiding within the first hour after birth\",\"Perform routine pelvic ultrasonography before discharge\",\"Measure serial serum β-hCG after every uncomplicated delivery\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"An indwelling catheter is used according to anesthesia, operative, and urinary-retention needs; a fixed 24-hour duration after every epidural is unnecessary.\",\"Routine postpartum observation includes repeated assessment of uterine contraction and vaginal blood loss, allowing early recognition of uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"Bladder function should be monitored after birth, but current postnatal guidance does not require every woman to void within 1 hour; persistent inability to void warrants assessment.\",\"Routine pelvic ultrasonography is reserved for concerns such as retained products or abnormal bleeding, not required after every uncomplicated birth.\",\"Serial β-hCG measurement is indicated for selected trophoblastic disease or other specific conditions, not after an uncomplicated delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"Immediate postpartum care prioritizes detection of hemorrhage and abnormal uterine involution while also assessing vital signs, pain, bladder function, and maternal wellbeing.\",\"The source gave both first-hour voiding and bleeding/uterine monitoring as correct; current guidance clearly supports routine uterine tone and lochia/bleeding assessment, whereas a universal one-hour voiding requirement is too rigid.\",\"A well-contracted myometrium compresses uterine vessels after placental separation, so loss of uterine tone can rapidly produce major postpartum blood loss.\"],\"tk\":\"Regularly assess uterine tone and vaginal bleeding in the immediate postpartum period to identify uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage early.\",\"pr\":\"A distended bladder can displace the uterus and impair effective contraction, so bladder assessment remains clinically relevant when the fundus is high or bleeding persists.\",\"tp\":\"If bleeding increases, immediately palpate the fundus, quantify blood loss, check vital signs, and escalate the postpartum-hemorrhage pathway while identifying tone, trauma, tissue, or thrombin causes.\",\"cn\":\"In the immediate postpartum period uterine tone (firm central fundus) and vaginal blood loss must be assessed regularly to detect haemorrhage.\",\"sl\":\"First 24 hours: monitor vital signs on an obstetric early-warning chart, firm central fundus with estimated and documented blood loss, and time and volume of first void (expected within about 6 hours).\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[20,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F501\"},{\"id\":\"F502\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ovarian tumors — serum markers\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which ovarian tumor–marker pair is mismatched?\",\"o\":[\"Serous epithelial ovarian carcinoma — cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\",\"Granulosa cell tumor — inhibin\",\"Ovarian choriocarcinoma — β-hCG\",\"Endodermal sinus (yolk sac) tumor — CA19-9\",\"Dysgerminoma — lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"CA-125 is frequently elevated in epithelial ovarian carcinoma, especially advanced serous disease, although it lacks specificity for population screening.\",\"Granulosa cell tumors can secrete inhibin and estrogen; inhibin can aid diagnosis and surveillance in an appropriate clinical setting.\",\"Ovarian choriocarcinoma contains trophoblastic tissue that produces β-hCG, often at markedly elevated serum concentrations.\",\"An endodermal sinus or yolk sac tumor characteristically produces alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) rather than CA19-9.\",\"Dysgerminoma is associated with lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) elevation from high cellular turnover; β-hCG can also rise when syncytiotrophoblastic cells are present.\"],\"e\":[\"Serum tumor markers can reflect the cell lineage of ovarian neoplasms and are especially useful in young patients with suspected germ-cell tumors.\",\"The mismatched pair is endodermal sinus tumor with CA19-9, because the characteristic marker is AFP; the other listed associations are recognized.\",\"Yolk-sac differentiation recapitulates extraembryonic tissue capable of synthesizing alpha-fetoprotein, allowing AFP to assist diagnosis and serial response monitoring.\"],\"tk\":\"Match yolk sac tumor with AFP, dysgerminoma with LDH, granulosa tumor with inhibin, and choriocarcinoma with β-hCG.\",\"pr\":\"A young patient with an adnexal mass should have AFP, β-hCG, and LDH considered before surgery because germ-cell tumor markers can materially alter the differential and fertility-sparing plan.\",\"tp\":\"Record tumor markers before definitive treatment whenever feasible so postoperative decline or persistence can be interpreted against a true baseline.\",\"cn\":\"Endodermal sinus (yolk sac) tumour secretes alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), not carbohydrate antigen 19-9 (CA19-9).\",\"sl\":\"Granulosa cell tumour: inhibin. Germ cell tumours: alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)/human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). Serous carcinoma: raised cancer antigen 125 (CA-125).\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[6,7,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F149\"},{\"id\":\"F503\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Inhibin B feedback\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which physiologic action is most characteristic of follicular inhibin B during the normal menstrual cycle?\",\"o\":[\"Triggers the midcycle luteinising hormone (LH) surge\",\"Directly stimulates theca-cell androgen synthesis\",\"Suppresses corpus-luteum progesterone secretion\",\"Increases hypothalamic gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulse frequency\",\"Decreases pituitary follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) secretion\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The luteinising hormone (LH) surge is driven mainly by sustained high estradiol positive feedback late in the follicular phase, not by follicular inhibin.\",\"Theca-cell androgen production is stimulated predominantly by LH, whereas inhibin chiefly modulates pituitary FSH secretion.\",\"Luteal progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum under LH/human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) support; inhibin is not its primary suppressor.\",\"Gonadotrophin-releasing hormone (GnRH) pulsatility is generated by the hypothalamic pulse generator; inhibin acts chiefly at the anterior pituitary rather than increasing GnRH pulse frequency.\",\"Inhibin B from granulosa cells provides selective negative feedback on pituitary FSH, helping limit recruitment of additional follicles as the dominant follicle emerges.\"],\"e\":[\"Granulosa cells secrete inhibin B during the follicular phase, and its major endocrine action is selective negative feedback on pituitary FSH.\",\"In a normal cycle, falling FSH after follicular recruitment helps favor the follicle with the greatest FSH sensitivity and aromatase activity.\",\"This feedback complements estradiol and helps prevent continued recruitment of multiple follicles by reducing gonadotropin drive.\"],\"tk\":\"Follicular inhibin B suppresses FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary.\",\"pr\":\"Inhibin A becomes more prominent in the luteal phase and is produced by the corpus luteum.\",\"tp\":\"When reviewing infertility hormones, interpret inhibin together with FSH, estradiol, and ovarian reserve context rather than as an isolated diagnostic test.\",\"cn\":\"Inhibin B from the growing follicle suppresses, not stimulates, pituitary follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) secretion.\",\"sl\":\"The follicular phase shows increasing levels of estradiol and inhibin B; when estradiol (E2) and inhibin B fall in the menopausal transition, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) rises.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[5,7]],[\"L34_0\",[9]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F031\"},{\"id\":\"F504\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Physio\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Mid-luteal progesterone timing\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman has regular 25-day menstrual cycles. On which cycle day is serum progesterone most likely to be at its highest?\",\"o\":[\"Day 25\",\"Day 11\",\"Day 18\",\"Day 20\",\"Day 14\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Near day 25, luteolysis causes progesterone to fall if implantation has not occurred, triggering menstruation.\",\"In a 25-day cycle, ovulation is expected around day 11, approximately 14 days before the next menses, so this is too early for peak luteal progesterone.\",\"Progesterone typically peaks about 7 days after ovulation; with ovulation near day 11 in a 25-day cycle, day 18 is the best estimate.\",\"Day 20 is roughly 9 days after expected ovulation and is later than the usual mid-luteal progesterone peak.\",\"By day 14, progesterone is rising after ovulation but has usually not yet reached its mid-luteal maximum.\"],\"e\":[\"After ovulation, the corpus luteum secretes progesterone, which reaches its highest concentration in the mid-luteal phase.\",\"Because the luteal phase is relatively constant at about 14 days, ovulation in a 25-day cycle is expected near day 11 and the progesterone peak about 7 days later.\",\"The timing reflects maximal corpus-luteum function before luteolysis if pregnancy has not occurred.\"],\"tk\":\"Peak progesterone occurs about 7 days after ovulation, not on a fixed calendar day for every cycle length.\",\"pr\":\"A mid-luteal progesterone sample is best timed relative to the expected next period, rather than automatically on day 21.\",\"tp\":\"For ovulation assessment, ask about usual cycle length first and schedule progesterone approximately 7 days before the expected menses.\",\"cn\":\"In a 25-day cycle ovulation is near day 11, so progesterone peaks about 7 days later, around day 18.\",\"sl\":\"Peak levels of progesterone are attained 7 days after ovulation; the luteal (progesterone) phase is fixed at 14 days while the follicular phase is variable.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[10,11]],[\"L33_1\",[2,6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F026\"},{\"id\":\"F505\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":13,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"DVT diagnosis in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman develops unilateral leg swelling and pain suspicious for deep-vein thrombosis. What is the primary diagnostic investigation?\",\"o\":[\"MR venography as the routine first test\",\"Plasma D-dimer\",\"Contrast venography\",\"Computed tomography (CT) pulmonary angiography\",\"Compression duplex ultrasonography of the symptomatic leg\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"MR venography can help when iliac thrombosis remains suspected despite negative or equivocal ultrasound, and is reserved for selected cases rather than routine initial imaging.\",\"D-dimer rises physiologically with gestation and is not the primary imaging test for a symptomatic pregnant patient with suspected DVT.\",\"Contrast venography is invasive and has largely been replaced by duplex ultrasonography for initial DVT diagnosis.\",\"Computed tomography (CT) pulmonary angiography evaluates suspected pulmonary embolism, not an isolated symptomatic leg for DVT.\",\"Compression duplex ultrasonography is the first-line noninvasive test for suspected lower-extremity DVT in pregnancy and has high accuracy for proximal thrombosis.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy increases venous stasis and hypercoagulability, but suspected DVT still requires objective imaging because symptoms are nonspecific.\",\"Compression duplex ultrasonography is first-line for a symptomatic leg; persistent suspicion after a negative study may require repeat imaging or evaluation of the iliac veins.\",\"This strategy avoids unnecessary ionizing radiation while directly assessing venous compressibility and flow.\"],\"tk\":\"Compression duplex ultrasonography is the first-line diagnostic test for suspected DVT in pregnancy.\",\"pr\":\"Pregnancy-associated DVT is relatively more likely to involve the left leg and proximal/iliac veins than DVT in nonpregnant adults.\",\"tp\":\"If swelling involves the whole leg or iliac thrombosis is suspected despite a negative standard scan, discuss additional iliac imaging or repeat ultrasound.\",\"cn\":\"Compression duplex ultrasound of the leg is the primary diagnostic test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in pregnancy; D-dimer is unhelpful.\",\"sl\":\"Compression duplex ultrasound is the primary diagnostic test for deep vein thrombosis (DVT); if negative with high clinical suspicion repeat on days 3 and 7. D-dimer rises progressively in pregnancy and is not helpful for diagnosis.\",\"s\":[[\"L13_0\",[11,12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F505\"},{\"id\":\"F506\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":39,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"High-risk HPV genotypes\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following human papillomavirus (HPV) genotypes are recognized as high-risk oncogenic types except which one?\",\"o\":[\"16\",\"11\",\"18\",\"33\",\"35\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"HPV 16 is a high-risk oncogenic type and is responsible for a large proportion of cervical squamous cancers.\",\"HPV 11 is a low-risk type associated mainly with anogenital warts and laryngeal papillomatosis rather than cervical carcinogenesis.\",\"HPV 18 is high risk and has a particularly strong association with cervical adenocarcinoma.\",\"HPV 33 belongs to the recognized high-risk oncogenic HPV group implicated in cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and cancer.\",\"HPV 35 is also classified as a high-risk HPV genotype with oncogenic potential.\"],\"e\":[\"Persistent infection with high-risk HPV is the necessary causal driver of most cervical cancers, especially genotypes 16 and 18.\",\"Among the listed genotypes, HPV 11 belongs to the low-risk group, whereas 16, 18, 33, and 35 have oncogenic potential.\",\"Low-risk HPV types favor productive epithelial infection and condyloma formation rather than integration-associated E6/E7-mediated carcinogenesis.\"],\"tk\":\"HPV 11 is a low-risk genotype, whereas HPV 16, 18, 33, and 35 are oncogenic high-risk types.\",\"pr\":\"HPV vaccination protects against major oncogenic types and, depending on vaccine formulation, also against HPV 6 and 11 that cause most genital warts.\",\"tp\":\"Do not use HPV genotype alone to diagnose cancer; abnormal screening results should follow the applicable risk-based cervical screening pathway.\",\"cn\":\"Human papillomavirus (HPV) 11, like HPV 6, is low-risk and causes genital warts; HPV 16, 18, 33 and 35 are oncogenic.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors: human papillomavirus (HPV) 16, 18, 31, 33, 45. The 9-valent vaccine covers HPV 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52 and 58.\",\"s\":[[\"L39_0\",[10,45]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F506\"},{\"id\":\"F507\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"First-trimester pregnancy dating\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which parameter provides the most accurate estimate of gestational age in the first trimester?\",\"o\":[\"Biparietal diameter\",\"Crown-rump length on first-trimester ultrasound\",\"Last menstrual period alone\",\"Fundal height\",\"Abdominal circumference\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Biparietal diameter is useful later in pregnancy but is not the most accurate first-trimester dating parameter.\",\"First-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate ultrasound parameter for establishing gestational age when the embryo is measurable.\",\"A reliable last menstrual period (LMP) can estimate gestational age, but variation in ovulation and recall makes it less precise than first-trimester ultrasound dating.\",\"Fundal height becomes useful after the uterus is abdominally palpable and is too imprecise for first-trimester dating.\",\"Abdominal circumference is central to later growth assessment and fetal-weight estimation, not first-trimester dating.\"],\"e\":[\"Early pregnancy dating is most precise when based on crown-rump length (CRL) because fetal size varies least in the first trimester.\",\"CRL is preferred over later biometric measurements such as biparietal diameter or abdominal circumference when an adequate first-trimester scan is available.\",\"Accurate dating reduces false classification of fetal growth restriction or post-term pregnancy later in gestation.\"],\"tk\":\"First-trimester crown-rump length is the most accurate biometric measure for pregnancy dating.\",\"pr\":\"When a reliable early ultrasound establishes gestational age, the estimated due date is generally not repeatedly changed by later scans showing normal biologic size variation.\",\"tp\":\"Document the dating method and established estimated date of delivery clearly so later growth assessments use a consistent gestational age.\",\"cn\":\"Crown-rump length (CRL) on first-trimester ultrasound gives the most accurate estimate of gestational age.\",\"sl\":\"Gestational age should be established by an ultrasound scan in the first trimester; accurate dating by first-trimester crown-rump length (CRL) is essential.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[2,8]],[\"L24_0\",[33]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F507\"},{\"id\":\"F508\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":34,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Early versus late menopause effects\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is not an early manifestation of the menopausal transition?\",\"o\":[\"Sleep disturbance\",\"Hot flushes\",\"Night sweats\",\"Mood symptoms\",\"Osteoporosis\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Sleep disturbance commonly appears during the menopausal transition and may be worsened by nocturnal vasomotor symptoms.\",\"Hot flushes are classic early vasomotor symptoms caused by narrowing of the hypothalamic thermoneutral zone during estrogen withdrawal.\",\"Night sweats are an early vasomotor manifestation and often accompany hot flushes.\",\"Mood changes can occur during the perimenopausal transition, particularly in association with sleep disturbance and vasomotor symptoms.\",\"Osteoporosis is a later consequence of sustained estrogen deficiency and accelerated bone resorption rather than an acute menopausal manifestation.\"],\"e\":[\"Menopause causes early vasomotor and neuropsychological symptoms, while long-term estrogen deficiency has progressive effects on bone and urogenital tissues.\",\"Hot flushes, night sweats, sleep disturbance, and mood symptoms may occur around the final menstrual period; osteoporosis develops over a longer time course.\",\"Loss of estrogen increases osteoclast-mediated bone resorption, producing cumulative reduction in bone mineral density and fracture risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Osteoporosis is a long-term consequence of estrogen deficiency, not an acute menopausal symptom.\",\"pr\":\"Genitourinary syndrome of menopause can progress rather than spontaneously resolve, unlike vasomotor symptoms that often lessen over time.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing menopausal symptoms, also evaluate individualized fracture and cardiovascular risk rather than focusing only on hot flushes.\",\"cn\":\"Osteoporosis is a long-term consequence of menopausal estrogen deficiency, whereas hot flushes, night sweats, mood change and sleep disturbance are early symptoms.\",\"sl\":\"Acute manifestations: hot flushes and night sweats (70-80%), mood swings and headaches. Long-term health implications: osteoporosis, cardiovascular disease, stroke and dementia.\",\"s\":[[\"L34_0\",[16,18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F028\"},{\"id\":\"F509\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":0,\"L\":22,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Renal and systemic physiology of pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about normal maternal physiologic changes during pregnancy is false?\",\"o\":[\"Plasma volume expands\",\"Total serum calcium may fall because albumin decreases\",\"Serum creatinine rises above the nonpregnant baseline\",\"Mild respiratory alkalosis develops\",\"Glomerular filtration rate increases\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal plasma volume expands markedly and exceeds the increase in red-cell mass, producing physiologic hemodilution.\",\"Total calcium can decrease because of lower albumin, while ionized calcium is generally maintained.\",\"Pregnancy increases renal plasma flow and GFR, so serum creatinine normally falls; a value that seems normal outside pregnancy can therefore represent renal impairment.\",\"Progesterone increases ventilation, causing chronic mild respiratory alkalosis with renal bicarbonate compensation.\",\"GFR increases substantially in normal pregnancy, contributing to lower serum urea and creatinine concentrations.\"],\"e\":[\"Normal pregnancy produces major cardiovascular, renal, respiratory, and hematologic adaptations, including increased plasma volume and glomerular filtration rate.\",\"Because GFR rises, serum creatinine normally decreases from the nonpregnant baseline rather than increasing.\",\"These adaptations improve uteroplacental perfusion and excretion of maternal-fetal metabolic products but alter the reference ranges used to interpret laboratory tests.\"],\"tk\":\"Serum creatinine normally falls in pregnancy because GFR increases.\",\"pr\":\"A creatinine concentration that would be considered normal in a nonpregnant adult can be abnormally high in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Interpret renal indices against pregnancy-specific physiology and investigate a rising creatinine promptly, especially with hypertension or proteinuria.\",\"cn\":\"Serum creatinine falls in pregnancy (35-80 micromol/L) because glomerular filtration rate (GFR) rises about 50%.\",\"sl\":\"Creatinine falls from 45-90 to 35-80 micromol/L and urea from 2.5-7.5 to 2.0-4.5 mmol/L. Total calcium falls with low albumin while ionised calcium is unchanged.\",\"s\":[[\"L22_0\",[38,51]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F509\"},{\"id\":\"F510\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Continuous CTG indication\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman previously considered low risk develops fresh vaginal bleeding during established labor. Maternal observations are stable. What fetal monitoring strategy is most appropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Continuous cardiotocography\",\"No fetal monitoring if maternal vital signs remain normal\",\"Routine intermittent auscultation only despite new bleeding\",\"A single Doppler check at the end of the first stage\",\"Ultrasound every 30 minutes\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends continuous CTG when vaginal blood loss other than a show is present or develops during labour.\",\"Normal maternal observations do not exclude placental bleeding or fetal hypoxia; fetal status requires continuous assessment in this setting.\",\"Fresh vaginal bleeding other than a show is an intrapartum risk factor for fetal compromise, so intermittent auscultation alone is insufficient once this develops.\",\"A single later Doppler assessment would miss evolving compromise; bleeding warrants ongoing fetal heart-rate surveillance.\",\"Repeated ultrasound does not provide beat-to-beat assessment of fetal oxygenation and is not a substitute for intrapartum CTG.\"],\"e\":[\"Continuous cardiotocography (CTG) is recommended when antenatal or intrapartum factors increase the risk of fetal compromise.\",\"Fresh vaginal bleeding in labour, beyond a normal show, raises concern for placental bleeding and therefore warrants continuous fetal heart-rate monitoring even if the mother is hemodynamically stable.\",\"CTG provides continuous assessment of baseline rate, variability, and decelerations so evolving fetal hypoxia can be recognized alongside the maternal evaluation.\"],\"tk\":\"Fresh intrapartum vaginal bleeding is an indication for continuous CTG monitoring.\",\"pr\":\"If CTG was started only for a transient auscultation concern and becomes normal after reassessment, NICE allows consideration of returning to intermittent auscultation when no other risk factor persists.\",\"tp\":\"With intrapartum bleeding, assess blood loss, maternal observations, placental location, uterine findings, and fetal status in parallel rather than treating CTG as the sole evaluation.\",\"cn\":\"Vaginal bleeding in labour makes the pregnancy high risk and calls for continuous cardiotocography (CTG).\",\"sl\":\"When to use cardiotocography (CTG): high-risk pregnancies need continuous electronic fetal monitoring, including medical disorders, vaginal bleeding, meconium-stained liquor and previous caesarean section (CS).\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[62]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F510\"},{\"id\":\"F511\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Causes of hypothyroidism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following conditions can present with hypothyroidism except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Hypothyroid phase of subacute thyroiditis\",\"Postpartum thyroiditis\",\"Hashimoto thyroiditis\",\"Radioiodine ablation of the thyroid\",\"Untreated Graves disease\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Subacute thyroiditis may have a transient hypothyroid phase after release and depletion of stored thyroid hormone.\",\"Postpartum thyroiditis can evolve from a thyrotoxic phase to a hypothyroid phase before recovery or permanent dysfunction.\",\"Hashimoto thyroiditis is the common autoimmune cause of primary hypothyroidism and is associated with thyroid-peroxidase antibodies.\",\"Prior radioiodine ablation commonly causes permanent hypothyroidism by destroying functioning thyroid tissue.\",\"Untreated Graves disease classically causes hyperthyroidism through TSH-receptor-stimulating antibodies rather than primary hypothyroidism.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary hypothyroidism results from inadequate thyroid-hormone production and commonly follows autoimmune destruction, thyroid ablation, or a hypothyroid phase of thyroiditis.\",\"Untreated Graves disease instead produces thyrotoxicosis because stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies activate thyroid hormone synthesis.\",\"Thyroiditis can be biphasic because follicular injury first releases stored hormone and later leaves a period of depleted hormone synthesis.\"],\"tk\":\"Untreated Graves disease is a cause of hyperthyroidism, whereas thyroiditis or ablation can produce hypothyroidism.\",\"pr\":\"Patients treated definitively for Graves disease with radioiodine or thyroidectomy may subsequently require lifelong levothyroxine.\",\"tp\":\"In pregnancy, use trimester-appropriate thyroid tests and distinguish active Graves disease from hypothyroidism after previous definitive therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Untreated Graves disease causes hyperthyroidism; Hashimoto thyroiditis, radioiodine ablation and the late phase of subacute or postpartum thyroiditis cause hypothyroidism.\",\"sl\":\"Postpartum thyroiditis: transient hyperthyroid phase followed by hypothyroid phase; can mimic depression.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F022\"},{\"id\":\"F512\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":33,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea mechanism\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about dysmenorrhea are true except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea often improves after menstrual flow is established\",\"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are effective treatment for primary dysmenorrhea\",\"Secondary dysmenorrhea warrants evaluation for pelvic pathology\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea is mediated by increased endometrial prostaglandins\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea is caused by cervical stenosis preventing menstrual outflow\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea typically begins shortly before or with menses and often lessens after the first 24–72 hours as prostaglandin activity falls.\",\"Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) inhibit cyclooxygenase and prostaglandin synthesis and are first-line therapy when not contraindicated.\",\"New or progressive dysmenorrhea, especially with dyspareunia or abnormal bleeding, should prompt evaluation for endometriosis, adenomyosis, or other pelvic pathology.\",\"Excess endometrial prostaglandin F2α and E2 increases uterine contractions and ischemia, producing primary dysmenorrhea.\",\"Primary dysmenorrhea occurs despite normal pelvic anatomy; cervical stenosis is an obstructive pathologic cause rather than its usual mechanism.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary dysmenorrhea is painful menstruation without structural pelvic disease and is driven largely by excess endometrial prostaglandin production.\",\"The statement attributing primary dysmenorrhea to cervical stenosis is false; stenosis represents an obstructive secondary cause rather than the usual physiology.\",\"Prostaglandin-mediated uterine hypercontractility and vasoconstriction produce myometrial ischemia, explaining the benefit of NSAIDs.\"],\"tk\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea is prostaglandin mediated, not ordinarily caused by cervical outflow obstruction.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms that begin years after menarche or progressively worsen raise concern for secondary dysmenorrhea, particularly endometriosis or adenomyosis.\",\"tp\":\"Ask about dyspareunia, infertility, abnormal bleeding, bowel/bladder symptoms, and examination findings before labeling severe pain as primary dysmenorrhea.\",\"cn\":\"Primary dysmenorrhea is prostaglandin- and contraction-mediated; cervical stenosis is a cause of secondary dysmenorrhea.\",\"sl\":\"Primary dysmenorrhoea theories: uterine contraction and vasoconstriction; treated with prostaglandin synthetase inhibitors (PGSI) such as mefenamic acid. Cervical stenosis (iatrogenic after large loop excision of the transformation zone, LLETZ) is listed as a secondary cause.\",\"s\":[[\"L33_0\",[24,26,27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F512\"},{\"id\":\"F513\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Screening\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Proteinuria screening in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the recommended initial screening test for proteinuria in a pregnant woman being assessed for a hypertensive disorder?\",\"o\":[\"Urine microalbumin concentration without creatinine correction\",\"Renal ultrasound\",\"Automated urine reagent-strip dipstick for initial screening\",\"24-hour urine collection for every patient\",\"Serum creatinine alone\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A raw urine microalbumin concentration varies with urine dilution; albumin:creatinine ratio is the validated quantitative measure.\",\"Renal ultrasound assesses anatomy and obstruction, not proteinuria screening.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends an automated reagent-strip dipstick for initial proteinuria screening, with a positive result quantified using protein:creatinine ratio (PCR) or albumin:creatinine ratio (ACR).\",\"Routine 24-hour urine collection is no longer recommended for initial quantification because spot protein:creatinine or albumin:creatinine ratios are faster and accurate.\",\"Serum creatinine assesses renal function but does not screen for urinary protein loss.\"],\"e\":[\"Proteinuria contributes to the diagnosis of pre-eclampsia but must be interpreted within the full maternal clinical assessment.\",\"NICE recommends automated urine reagent-strip testing for screening; a result of 1+ or more should be quantified with a protein:creatinine or albumin:creatinine ratio.\",\"A spot PCR of 30 mg/mmol or ACR of 8 mg/mmol is used as a diagnostic threshold, avoiding routine 24-hour urine collection.\"],\"tk\":\"Use an automated dipstick for screening, then quantify a positive result with PCR or ACR.\",\"pr\":\"Pre-eclampsia can be diagnosed without proteinuria when new hypertension is accompanied by maternal organ dysfunction or uteroplacental dysfunction.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay evaluation of severe symptoms while awaiting urine results; check blood pressure, platelets, creatinine, liver enzymes, and fetal status concurrently.\",\"cn\":\"Screen for proteinuria in pregnancy with an automated reagent-strip dipstick; quantify positives (1+ or more) with a protein:creatinine ratio.\",\"sl\":\"Use an automated reagent-strip reading device for dipstick screening for proteinuria; if 1+ or more, use spot protein:creatinine or albumin:creatinine ratio. The 24-hour urine collection is the gold standard but inconvenient and not routinely recommended.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[29]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F170\"},{\"id\":\"F514\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"External cephalic version safety\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about external cephalic version is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Ultrasound should confirm presentation and assess relevant obstetric factors\",\"The procedure should be performed where urgent cesarean delivery is available\",\"A non-stress test and written consent are the only prerequisites\",\"Fetal heart-rate assessment is required before and after the attempt\",\"Unsensitized RhD-negative women should receive anti-D after external cephalic version (ECV)\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ultrasound helps confirm breech presentation, fetal position, placental location, amniotic-fluid volume, and factors affecting ECV safety.\",\"Because rare complications include abruption or persistent fetal bradycardia, ECV should occur where urgent cesarean birth can be performed.\",\"ECV requires more than an NST and consent; presentation, placental location, liquor, contraindications, and access to emergency delivery must be assessed.\",\"Assessment of the fetal heart rate before and after ECV is part of safe procedural monitoring.\",\"ECV can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage, so unsensitized RhD-negative women should receive anti-D prophylaxis after the procedure.\"],\"e\":[\"External cephalic version is an elective attempt to turn a term breech fetus to cephalic presentation under appropriate maternal-fetal assessment.\",\"It is incorrect to reduce the prerequisites to only an NST and consent; safe ECV also requires ultrasound assessment, exclusion of contraindications, and immediate access to cesarean delivery.\",\"Manipulation can rarely cause fetomaternal hemorrhage or fetal compromise, which explains anti-D prophylaxis for eligible RhD-negative women and post-procedure fetal monitoring.\"],\"tk\":\"ECV requires structured safety assessment and emergency-delivery capability, not only consent and an NST.\",\"pr\":\"Reversion to breech after a successful near-term ECV is uncommon, which is one reason the procedure is generally offered near term.\",\"tp\":\"Before ECV, document presentation, placental location, amniotic fluid, fetal status, rhesus D (RhD) status, and the availability of urgent operative delivery.\",\"cn\":\"External cephalic version (ECV) needs ultrasound, fetal heart monitoring, caesarean access and anti-D, not just a non-stress test (NST) and consent.\",\"sl\":\"Prerequisites include a reactive non-stress test (NST) and informed consent; ultrasonographic guidance is an important adjunct to confirm position and monitor fetal heart rate (FHR). After the procedure monitor continuously until FHR is reactive; Rh-negative patients should receive Rh(D) immune globulin.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[24,27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F514\"},{\"id\":\"F515\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Second-stage labor assessment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about assessment of progress in the second stage of labor is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Two hours of pushing in every nulliparous woman is always an abnormal alert threshold regardless of epidural use or descent\",\"Assessment of progress includes descent, position, contractions, and maternal-fetal condition\",\"The second stage begins at full cervical dilatation\",\"Maternal exhaustion can justify assisted vaginal birth when prerequisites are met\",\"Operative vaginal birth requires assessment of fetal head position and station\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A rigid 2-hour threshold ignores epidural use, active versus passive second stage, descent, parity, and current individualized labor assessment.\",\"Second-stage progress is judged by descent, rotation, contraction quality, and maternal-fetal status, not time alone.\",\"The second stage of labor begins when the cervix is fully dilated and ends with birth of the baby.\",\"Maternal exhaustion can support expedited birth when operative-vaginal prerequisites are fulfilled and the fetal head is appropriately low.\",\"Before forceps or vacuum, the clinician must know head position and station, confirm full dilatation, and ensure no contraindication.\"],\"e\":[\"The second stage extends from full cervical dilatation to birth, and its management depends on both elapsed time and evidence of descent and rotation.\",\"A fixed rule that every nulliparous woman reaches an abnormal threshold after exactly 2 hours of pushing is too rigid; epidural use, descent, fetal status, and maternal condition alter management.\",\"Operative delivery decisions require confirmation of station, position, pelvic adequacy, analgesia, and fetal status, not time alone.\"],\"tk\":\"Second-stage delay should be assessed clinically rather than by an inflexible 2-hour rule.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent lack of descent despite adequate contractions raises concern for malposition or cephalopelvic disproportion.\",\"tp\":\"Before escalating to forceps, vacuum, or cesarean delivery, record the fetal position, station, caput/molding, contraction pattern, and cardiotocography (CTG).\",\"cn\":\"Second-stage delay is judged by parity, epidural use, descent and maternal-fetal condition, not a rigid 2-hour rule.\",\"sl\":\"The second stage is from full cervical dilation to delivery. Nulliparous: maximum of 2 hours (3 if on epidural); multiparous: maximum of 1 hour (2 if on epidural). Alert values for the second stage: 3 h or more in nulliparous and 2 h or more in multiparous women from the start of the active second stage.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[45]],[\"L15_0\",[34,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F515\"},{\"id\":\"F516\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Streptococcal vulvovaginitis in young girls\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which organism is a recognized common bacterial cause of acute vulvovaginitis with discharge in a prepubertal girl?\",\"o\":[\"Chlamydia trachomatis\",\"Trichomonas vaginalis\",\"Candida albicans\",\"Gardnerella-associated bacterial vaginosis\",\"Group A beta-hemolytic Streptococcus\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Detection of Chlamydia trachomatis in a prepubertal child requires careful assessment for sexual transmission rather than being treated as the usual nonspecific pubertal discharge.\",\"Trichomonas vaginalis is sexually transmitted and is not the usual organism causing nonspecific vulvovaginitis in young girls.\",\"Candida can cause vulvovaginitis in adolescents but is not the classic leading bacterial pathogen in prepubertal vulvovaginitis.\",\"Bacterial vaginosis reflects an altered vaginal microbiome and is more characteristic after estrogenization and sexual maturity than as the classic prepubertal infection.\",\"Group A Streptococcus is a recognized cause of acute vulvovaginitis in prepubertal girls, often producing erythema and purulent discharge.\"],\"e\":[\"Prepubertal vulvovaginitis is promoted by thin hypoestrogenic epithelium, neutral vaginal pH, and local irritation; group A Streptococcus is a recognized bacterial pathogen.\",\"Acute erythema and purulent discharge in a young girl can therefore justify targeted testing for group A beta-hemolytic streptococci when infection is suspected.\",\"Sexually transmitted organisms require a different safeguarding context because their presence may imply sexual exposure rather than routine childhood vulvovaginitis.\"],\"tk\":\"Group A Streptococcus is a recognized bacterial cause of prepubertal vulvovaginitis.\",\"pr\":\"Many cases in young girls are nonspecific irritant vulvovaginitis, so hygiene, avoidance of irritants, and examination remain important even when cultures are negative.\",\"tp\":\"Persistent, bloody, foul, or recurrent discharge warrants evaluation for foreign body, dermatologic disease, pinworms, and safeguarding concerns.\",\"cn\":\"Group A beta-haemolytic Streptococcus is the commonest specific bacterial pathogen of prepubertal vulvovaginitis.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F516\"},{\"id\":\"F517\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Anti-D after sensitizing events\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which event is not usually an indication for anti-D immunoglobulin in an unsensitized RhD-negative woman?\",\"o\":[\"Amniocentesis\",\"Minor self-limited spotting before 10 weeks with no instrumentation\",\"Abdominal trauma with suspected fetomaternal hemorrhage\",\"Surgical management of a miscarriage at 13 weeks\",\"External cephalic version\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Amniocentesis can permit fetal red cells to enter the maternal circulation and is a sensitizing event.\",\"National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) does not recommend routine anti-D for threatened miscarriage with minor early bleeding when there has been no surgical instrumentation.\",\"Significant abdominal trauma can produce fetomaternal hemorrhage and is an indication for anti-D in an unsensitized RhD-negative woman.\",\"Miscarriage at 12 weeks or more, especially with surgical evacuation, is a sensitising event, so an unsensitised RhD-negative woman is given anti-D (slides; NICE).\",\"External cephalic version (ECV) can cause fetomaternal hemorrhage and is a recognized sensitizing event requiring anti-D in an eligible RhD-negative patient.\"],\"e\":[\"Anti-D prophylaxis prevents maternal rhesus D (RhD) alloimmunization after events that can transfer fetal erythrocytes into the maternal circulation.\",\"Minor threatened bleeding early in pregnancy without instrumentation is generally not treated with anti-D under NICE early-pregnancy guidance, unlike invasive procedures or miscarriage at 12 weeks or more.\",\"Passive anti-D clears RhD-positive fetal cells before the maternal immune system forms durable anti-D memory.\"],\"tk\":\"Minor early threatened bleeding without instrumentation does not routinely require anti-D under NICE guidance.\",\"pr\":\"Local protocols may differ by gestational age and national guidance, particularly for first-trimester bleeding and abortion care.\",\"tp\":\"Check gestation, RhD status, sensitization status, and the exact event before prescribing; anti-D has no preventive role once true alloimmunization is established.\",\"cn\":\"Minor early threatened bleeding without instrumentation does not need anti-D; invasive procedures, abdominal trauma, external cephalic version and miscarriage at 12 weeks or more do.\",\"sl\":\"Anti-D immunoglobulin for all threatened or spontaneous miscarriages at 12 weeks or more and all miscarriages where the uterus is evacuated surgically.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F517\"},{\"id\":\"F518\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":18,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Biochemical testing for PPROM\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant woman reports a gush of fluid. Sterile speculum examination shows no pooling in the posterior fornix. Which test is an appropriate next investigation for suspected preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM)?\",\"o\":[\"Placental alpha-microglobulin-1 test (PAMG-1; AmniSure)\",\"Digital vaginal examination\",\"Cardiotocography to confirm membrane rupture\",\"Routine ultrasound alone\",\"Nitrazine testing as the sole confirmatory test\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"When sterile speculum examination shows no pooling, a PAMG-1 test is an accepted biochemical test for suspected PPROM.\",\"A digital vaginal examination can shorten latency and increase infection risk in suspected PPROM and is avoided unless labor assessment makes it necessary.\",\"Cardiotocography (CTG) evaluates fetal heart rate and uterine activity; it does not diagnose whether the membranes have ruptured.\",\"Ultrasound can show low fluid but oligohydramnios is neither sufficiently sensitive nor specific to confirm membrane rupture by itself.\",\"Nitrazine can be falsely positive with blood, semen, or infection and is not the preferred standalone confirmatory test when pooling is absent.\"],\"e\":[\"Suspected PPROM is first assessed with a sterile speculum examination looking for visible pooling of amniotic fluid.\",\"If no pooling is seen, National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends a biochemical vaginal-fluid test such as PAMG-1 or IGFBP-1 rather than relying on history alone.\",\"These proteins are present at high concentrations in amniotic fluid, so their detection increases diagnostic confidence while avoiding a digital examination that may increase infection risk.\"],\"tk\":\"When pooling is absent, PAMG-1 or IGFBP-1 testing is appropriate for suspected PPROM.\",\"pr\":\"A positive biochemical test should still be interpreted with the clinical history, gestational age, and examination, not used in isolation.\",\"tp\":\"Avoid routine digital examination in suspected PPROM unless labor is established or immediate delivery planning requires it; document temperature, fetal status, and infection features.\",\"cn\":\"Without visible pooling in suspected preterm prelabour rupture of membranes (PPROM), test for placental alpha-microglobulin-1 (PAMG-1), for example AmniSure.\",\"sl\":\"The AmniSure rupture of membranes (ROM) test detects the placental alpha-microglobulin-1 (PAMG-1) protein marker with 98.9% sensitivity and 100% specificity, gives results within minutes and has no gestational age limitation; it is used in the absence of demonstrable fluid loss.\",\"s\":[[\"L18_0\",[9,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F518\"},{\"id\":\"F519\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Conservative stress-incontinence treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which intervention is not a first-line conservative measure specifically for pure stress urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Weight reduction when body mass index (BMI) is above 30 kg/m²\",\"Supervised pelvic-floor muscle training for at least 3 months\",\"Lifestyle measures that reduce provoking pelvic-floor strain\",\"Bladder retraining as first-line therapy for pure stress incontinence\",\"Management of chronic cough and constipation\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Weight loss is recommended when body mass index (BMI) exceeds 30 kg/m², because obesity increases intra-abdominal pressure and stress leakage.\",\"At least 3 months of supervised pelvic-floor muscle training is first-line treatment for stress urinary incontinence.\",\"Reducing activities that repeatedly provoke marked pelvic-floor strain can be incorporated into individualized conservative management.\",\"Bladder retraining targets urgency and frequency and is first-line for urgency or mixed incontinence, not the principal conservative therapy for pure stress leakage.\",\"Treating chronic cough and constipation reduces repeated rises in intra-abdominal pressure that can worsen stress incontinence.\"],\"e\":[\"Stress urinary incontinence results from inadequate urethral support or sphincter function during rises in intra-abdominal pressure.\",\"Supervised pelvic-floor muscle training for at least 3 months is first-line, whereas bladder training primarily treats urgency or mixed incontinence.\",\"Strengthening pelvic-floor contraction improves urethral closure pressure and support during coughing, exercise, and lifting.\"],\"tk\":\"Bladder training is primarily for urgency or mixed incontinence, not isolated stress incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Before PFMT, National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE) recommends confirming that the woman can perform an effective pelvic-floor contraction.\",\"tp\":\"Ask the patient to demonstrate a pelvic-floor contraction and address weight, cough, constipation, and activity triggers before discussing surgery.\",\"cn\":\"Bladder retraining is first-line for overactive bladder, not pure stress urinary incontinence (SUI), which uses pelvic floor training.\",\"sl\":\"Pelvic floor muscle training (PFMT) of at least three months is first-line for stress (SUI) or mixed urinary incontinence; SUI conservative measures: PFMT, biofeedback, electrical stimulation, vaginal cones. Bladder retraining is listed under overactive bladder (OAB).\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[59,73,76]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F156\"},{\"id\":\"F520\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":30,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PCOS treatment principles\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about treatment of polycystic ovary syndrome is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Hormonal therapy can provide cycle control and endometrial protection when pregnancy is not desired\",\"Long-term management should address cardiometabolic risk as well as reproductive symptoms\",\"Letrozole is preferred because it reliably induces bifollicular growth\",\"Letrozole is first-line pharmacologic ovulation induction when polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is the sole identified infertility factor\",\"Weight reduction can improve ovulation and metabolic health in patients with overweight\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Combined hormonal contraception or cyclic progestogen can provide cycle control and endometrial protection when pregnancy is not desired.\",\"PCOS management includes assessment of glucose intolerance, cardiovascular risk factors, weight, and psychological health in addition to menstrual and fertility issues.\",\"Letrozole is not preferred because it 'reliably induces bifollicular growth'; its evidence-based advantage relates to improved ovulation, pregnancy, and live-birth outcomes with relatively low multiple-pregnancy risk.\",\"Current international guidance recommends letrozole as first-line pharmacologic ovulation induction for anovulatory infertility due to PCOS when no other infertility factor is present.\",\"In patients with overweight or obesity, weight reduction can improve insulin resistance, ovulatory function, and cardiometabolic risk.\"],\"e\":[\"PCOS management is individualized around metabolic risk, menstrual control, hyperandrogenism, and fertility goals.\",\"Letrozole is first-line pharmacologic ovulation induction for suitable anovulatory infertility, but its benefit is not because the goal is bifollicular growth.\",\"Aromatase inhibition lowers estrogen feedback transiently, increasing endogenous follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and promoting follicular recruitment while aiming for effective single-ovulation cycles.\"],\"tk\":\"Letrozole is first-line for suitable anovulatory PCOS infertility, but bifollicular growth is not the reason for its superiority.\",\"pr\":\"Chronic anovulation exposes the endometrium to prolonged unopposed estrogen, increasing the risk of endometrial hyperplasia.\",\"tp\":\"At each visit, clarify whether the priority is pregnancy, cycle control, hirsutism, or metabolic health because treatment choices differ by goal.\",\"cn\":\"Letrozole tends to produce monofollicular ovulation; bifollicular growth is not its advantage in polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) ovulation induction.\",\"sl\":\"Weight reduction improves all symptoms of polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). Ovulation induction: clomiphene citrate and/or metformin first, with folliculometry; aromatase inhibitors are listed for clomiphene resistance.\",\"s\":[[\"L30_0\",[18,19]],[\"L32_0\",[27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"About the key: Key C is correct as the false statement, since letrozole is not chosen for bifollicular growth. Note the slides (L30 page 19, L32 page 27) give clomiphene as first-line and aromatase inhibitors for clomiphene resistance, so option D (letrozole first-line, per international guidance) also conflicts with the doctors' slides.\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F520\"},{\"id\":\"F521\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Uterine rupture recognition\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about uterine rupture during labor is most accurate?\",\"o\":[\"Maternal hypotension is always the earliest sign\",\"Sudden pain is present in every case\",\"Ultrasound reliably excludes uterine rupture\",\"Fetal heart-rate/cardiotocography (CTG) abnormality is the most consistent warning sign\",\"Vaginal bleeding is invariably massive\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Maternal hypotension is a late sign of significant hemorrhage; fetal heart-rate abnormalities may appear before maternal decompensation.\",\"Pain can occur with uterine rupture but may be absent or masked by regional analgesia, so it is not universal.\",\"Ultrasound may aid evaluation but cannot reliably exclude rupture in an acutely deteriorating laboring patient.\",\"An acute CTG abnormality, particularly fetal bradycardia, is the most consistent sign of uterine rupture during labor.\",\"Bleeding can be concealed within the abdomen, so visible vaginal hemorrhage is not invariably massive.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine rupture is a time-critical obstetric emergency, especially in labor after a previous full-thickness uterine scar.\",\"The most consistent early warning is an abrupt fetal heart-rate or CTG abnormality, often bradycardia, rather than a reliable maternal symptom pattern.\",\"Rupture can cause sudden loss of uteroplacental perfusion and fetal oxygenation before maternal hemorrhage produces hypotension or shock.\"],\"tk\":\"Acute fetal heart-rate abnormality is the most consistent sign of uterine rupture.\",\"pr\":\"Other clues include cessation of contractions, abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, loss of fetal station, and maternal hemodynamic deterioration, but none is universally present.\",\"tp\":\"With suspected rupture, call for senior help and prepare for immediate laparotomy and delivery while resuscitation and blood-product preparation proceed.\",\"cn\":\"Cardiotocography (CTG) abnormality or loss of fetal heart rate is the most consistent sign of uterine rupture.\",\"sl\":\"Symptoms of uterine rupture are often non-specific: abdominal pain between contractions, with or without bleeding, loss of station, inability to identify contractions, haematuria and blood-stained liquor, cardiotocography (CTG) abnormalities or lost fetal heart rate (FHR).\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[57]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F521\"},{\"id\":\"F522\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":24,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ultrasound detection of fetal growth restriction\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about ultrasound detection and assessment of fetal growth restriction is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Growth velocity on serial ultrasound is clinically important\",\"Serial abdominal circumference is useful for detecting impaired fetal growth\",\"Umbilical-artery Doppler helps assess placental insufficiency\",\"Head circumference is the most effective single biometric parameter for detecting fetal growth restriction (FGR)\",\"FGR is associated with increased perinatal morbidity\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Serial measurements allow assessment of growth velocity, which can reveal faltering growth even before a single size threshold becomes extreme.\",\"Abdominal circumference is particularly sensitive to reduced hepatic glycogen and subcutaneous fat and is central to fetal growth assessment.\",\"Umbilical-artery Doppler evaluates placental resistance and helps stratify risk in suspected placental FGR.\",\"Head circumference is relatively preserved in asymmetric FGR and is therefore not the most effective single parameter for detection.\",\"FGR increases risks of stillbirth, intrapartum compromise, neonatal morbidity, and long-term cardiometabolic disease.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal growth assessment integrates estimated fetal weight, abdominal circumference, serial growth velocity, and Doppler studies.\",\"Head circumference may be relatively spared in placental insufficiency, so calling it the most effective single parameter for FGR detection is incorrect.\",\"Reduced placental nutrient delivery disproportionately affects liver size and fat deposition, making abdominal circumference a sensitive biometric marker.\"],\"tk\":\"Abdominal circumference and estimated fetal weight are more useful than head circumference alone for detecting FGR.\",\"pr\":\"Umbilical-artery Doppler showing increased placental resistance identifies fetuses at higher risk of hypoxemia and stillbirth.\",\"tp\":\"When growth is suspected to be abnormal, review dating accuracy, serial biometry, liquor, Dopplers, and maternal disease rather than relying on one measurement.\",\"cn\":\"Abdominal circumference (AC), not head circumference, is the most effective single biometric parameter for detecting fetal growth restriction (FGR).\",\"sl\":\"Abdominal circumference (AC) or estimated fetal weight (EFW) below the 10th centile defines small for gestational age (SGA); the AC falls first while the head is spared. Growth velocity matters, and umbilical artery (UA) Doppler is the primary tool in early FGR.\",\"s\":[[\"L24_0\",[7,12,33,39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F522\"},{\"id\":\"F523\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Twin-pregnancy risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about twin pregnancy is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Hypertensive disorders are more common in multifetal pregnancy\",\"A family history mainly increases the likelihood of dizygotic twinning\",\"Twin pregnancy increases the risk of preterm birth\",\"Twin pregnancy increases the risk of malpresentation and fetal growth problems\",\"A family history is a major risk factor for monozygotic twinning\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Greater placental mass and maternal physiologic load contribute to a higher incidence of gestational hypertension and pre-eclampsia.\",\"Familial clustering is most relevant to dizygotic twinning, reflecting inherited tendencies toward multiple ovulation.\",\"Preterm birth is substantially more common in twins because of uterine overdistension and multifetal-pregnancy complications.\",\"Twins have increased risks of malpresentation and fetal growth restriction, and chorionicity adds specific fetal risks.\",\"Monozygotic twinning is largely sporadic and is not strongly driven by a family history of twins.\"],\"e\":[\"Twin gestations have higher maternal and fetal complication rates than singleton pregnancies, including preterm birth, hypertensive disease, growth abnormalities, and malpresentation.\",\"A family tendency is associated mainly with dizygotic twinning through inherited multiple ovulation; monozygotic twinning is generally sporadic.\",\"Chorionicity, rather than zygosity alone, determines unique placental complications such as twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome.\"],\"tk\":\"Family history is associated mainly with dizygotic, not monozygotic, twinning.\",\"pr\":\"Chorionicity should be established early because monochorionic twins require more intensive ultrasound surveillance.\",\"tp\":\"On the first-trimester scan, document chorionicity and amnionicity and schedule follow-up according to the twin type.\",\"cn\":\"Family history mainly increases dizygotic twinning; it is not a major risk factor for monozygotic twinning.\",\"sl\":\"Family history: dizygotic (DZ) twinning runs in the maternal line. Monozygotic (MZ) twins: mostly random; slight increase after assisted reproduction (ART).\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F161\"},{\"id\":\"F524\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":29,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"46,XY disorders of sex development\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which condition-feature pairing is mismatched?\",\"o\":[\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) — chronic anovulation with hyperandrogenism\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome — small uterus present\",\"Müllerian agenesis — 46,XX with absent or rudimentary uterus\",\"Swyer syndrome — 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis with a uterus\",\"Turner syndrome — streak ovaries\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) commonly produces ovulatory dysfunction and clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism.\",\"In complete androgen insensitivity, functioning testes secrete anti-Müllerian hormone, so Müllerian structures including the uterus are absent.\",\"Müllerian agenesis occurs in 46,XX individuals with congenital absence or severe underdevelopment of the uterus and upper vagina.\",\"In Swyer syndrome, dysgenetic 46,XY gonads do not produce effective AMH, so Müllerian structures including a uterus are present.\",\"Turner syndrome commonly features streak gonads and hypergonadotropic hypogonadism.\"],\"e\":[\"Internal reproductive anatomy in 46,XY disorders depends on fetal anti-Müllerian hormone and androgen action.\",\"Complete androgen insensitivity has functioning testes that produce AMH, so the patient does not have a uterus, despite female external genitalia and breast development.\",\"By contrast, 46,XY gonadal dysgenesis lacks effective testicular hormone production, allowing Müllerian structures to persist.\"],\"tk\":\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome is associated with an absent uterus.\",\"pr\":\"Pubic and axillary hair is often sparse or absent in complete androgen insensitivity because androgen receptors are nonfunctional.\",\"tp\":\"In primary amenorrhea, combine uterine anatomy, breast development, pubic hair, gonadotropins, and karyotype before assigning a difference of sex development (DSD) diagnosis.\",\"cn\":\"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) is 46,XY with an absent uterus, because testicular anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) is present.\",\"sl\":\"Complete androgen insensitivity (CAIS): 46,XY, uterus absent, no pubic or axillary hair and a short vagina. Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser syndrome has an absent uterus; Swyer syndrome (46,XY) has a uterus.\",\"s\":[[\"L29_0\",[6,7]],[\"L28_0\",[17]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F524\"},{\"id\":\"F525\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Filling cystometry interpretation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about cystometric bladder sensation and urodynamic testing is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Bladder sensation is documented during filling cystometry\",\"Pressure-flow studies can help evaluate voiding dysfunction in selected patients\",\"A first bladder sensation of 50–100 mL is a fixed diagnostic cutoff that must be met\",\"Maximum cystometric capacity is measured during filling studies\",\"Detrusor overactivity is an involuntary detrusor contraction during filling\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Filling cystometry records patient-reported bladder sensations alongside pressure measurements as bladder volume increases.\",\"A pressure-flow study can distinguish poor detrusor contractility from outlet obstruction when voiding dysfunction requires urodynamic clarification.\",\"Normal bladder sensation volumes vary considerably, so 50–100 mL is not a universal diagnostic cutoff that every patient must meet.\",\"Maximum cystometric capacity is one of the standard observations during filling cystometry.\",\"Detrusor overactivity is defined urodynamically by involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase.\"],\"e\":[\"Filling cystometry measures bladder pressure-volume relationships while documenting sensation, compliance, detrusor activity, and capacity.\",\"A fixed first-sensation volume of 50–100 mL should not be treated as an absolute diagnostic criterion because sensation is patient dependent and influenced by testing conditions.\",\"Urodynamics are most useful when the result could change management, especially in complex or unclear lower urinary tract dysfunction.\"],\"tk\":\"Bladder sensation volumes are descriptive urodynamic findings, not rigid universal diagnostic cutoffs.\",\"pr\":\"Detrusor overactivity is a urodynamic observation, whereas overactive bladder is a symptom syndrome defined clinically by urgency with or without urgency incontinence.\",\"tp\":\"Before requesting invasive urodynamics, define the clinical question and review symptoms, bladder diary, urinalysis, residual volume, and prior treatment.\",\"cn\":\"Bladder sensation, capacity and detrusor overactivity are recorded during filling cystometry; sensation volumes are not fixed diagnostic cutoffs.\",\"sl\":\"Filling cystometry: retrograde bladder filling with pressures measured via transducers. Detrusor overactivity (DO): involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase, spontaneous or provoked.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[34,40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F525\"},{\"id\":\"F526\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":6,\"L\":30,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Comprehensive PCOS management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about management of polycystic ovary syndrome is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Metabolic risk assessment is part of long-term polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) care\",\"Cycle regulation or progestogen exposure can protect the endometrium in chronic anovulation\",\"Weight loss can improve outcomes when overweight or obesity is present\",\"Weight reduction is required only when body mass index (BMI) exceeds 24 kg/m² and is the sole necessary treatment\",\"Lifestyle advice should be discussed with all patients with PCOS\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"PCOS carries increased risk of dysglycemia and cardiometabolic disease, making ongoing risk assessment important.\",\"Chronic anovulation can expose the endometrium to unopposed estrogen, so cycle regulation or periodic progestogen provides endometrial protection.\",\"For patients with overweight or obesity, even modest weight loss can improve metabolic health and may improve ovulatory function.\",\"There is no evidence-based rule that body mass index (BMI) must exceed 24 kg/m² before lifestyle care matters, and weight reduction alone does not address all reproductive, metabolic, or psychological needs.\",\"All patients benefit from discussion of healthy lifestyle and prevention of excess weight gain, regardless of starting BMI.\"],\"e\":[\"PCOS is a chronic condition requiring care of reproductive, metabolic, dermatologic, and psychological domains.\",\"Management should not be reduced to weight loss above an arbitrary BMI threshold; lifestyle discussion is relevant to all, while specific therapies depend on symptoms and fertility goals.\",\"Insulin resistance and chronic anovulation link PCOS to dysglycemia and endometrial hyperplasia, so risk reduction extends beyond body weight alone.\"],\"tk\":\"PCOS management is individualized and is not limited to weight reduction above a fixed BMI cutoff.\",\"pr\":\"Patients with prolonged amenorrhea need attention to endometrial protection even when hirsutism or fertility is not their presenting concern.\",\"tp\":\"Record the patient's priorities and screen for glycemic status, blood pressure, lipids, depression/anxiety, and sleep-apnea risk as clinically appropriate.\",\"cn\":\"Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) management is individualized; weight loss helps but is not the sole treatment or tied to a fixed cut-off.\",\"sl\":\"Treatment depends on the needs of the patient and preventing long-term health problems; a 10% weight loss restores normal hormones and spontaneous ovulation in 40% of women.\",\"s\":[[\"L30_0\",[13,14,18]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F526\"},{\"id\":\"F527\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Cause\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Partial versus complete hydatidiform mole\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about hydatidiform mole is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"p57 immunostaining helps distinguish complete from partial mole\",\"Partial moles are invariably triploid with no exceptions\",\"Complete mole is usually androgenetic diploid\",\"Serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) surveillance is required after molar evacuation\",\"Partial moles usually contain maternal as well as paternal chromosomal material\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Complete moles lack maternal genomic contribution and therefore usually lack p57 expression, whereas partial moles generally retain p57.\",\"Partial moles are usually diandric triploid, but the absolute word 'invariably' is too strong because uncommon nontriploid molar conceptions can occur.\",\"A typical complete mole is androgenetic diploid, most often 46,XX from duplication of a paternal haploid genome in an empty ovum.\",\"Post-evacuation serial human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) surveillance is required to detect persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.\",\"A partial mole usually has a maternal haploid set plus an extra paternal set, explaining its typical diandric triploid constitution.\"],\"e\":[\"Complete and partial hydatidiform moles differ in parental genomic contribution, ploidy, fetal tissue, p57 expression, and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) risk.\",\"Partial moles are characteristically diandric triploid, but an absolute claim that every partial mole is triploid is too categorical.\",\"p57 is maternally expressed; its absence in villous cytotrophoblast and stromal cells supports an androgenetic complete mole.\"],\"tk\":\"Partial moles are usually, but not absolutely invariably, triploid.\",\"pr\":\"Complete moles carry a higher risk of post-molar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia than partial moles.\",\"tp\":\"After evacuation, ensure reliable quantitative hCG follow-up and contraception according to the current gestational trophoblastic disease (GTD) surveillance protocol.\",\"cn\":\"Partial moles are usually (about 90%) triploid, not always; some are tetraploid or mosaic.\",\"sl\":\"Partial mole: 90% triploid with two paternal and one maternal haploid sets; occasionally tetraploid or mosaic. Complete mole: diploid and androgenic. p57 immunostaining helps distinguish partial from complete mole.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[6,5,20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F527\"},{\"id\":\"F528\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":4,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Electrolytes in hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which laboratory disturbance is not typically associated with hyperemesis gravidarum?\",\"o\":[\"Hypernatremia as the typical sodium disturbance\",\"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis\",\"Hyponatremia\",\"Hypokalemia\",\"Prerenal rise in urea/creatinine with dehydration\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hyperemesis more often causes hyponatremia from sodium loss and free-water handling; hypernatremia is not the typical sodium disturbance.\",\"Loss of gastric hydrochloric acid from persistent vomiting produces hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis.\",\"Volume depletion and impaired intake can produce hyponatremia, especially with inappropriate hypotonic fluid replacement.\",\"Vomiting, secondary hyperaldosteronism, and renal losses commonly produce hypokalemia.\",\"Severe dehydration can reduce renal perfusion, causing a prerenal rise in urea and creatinine.\"],\"e\":[\"Hyperemesis gravidarum can cause marked volume depletion, electrolyte loss, and acid-base disturbance from prolonged vomiting and poor intake.\",\"The classic pattern includes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, hypokalemia, and often hyponatremia rather than hypernatremia.\",\"Loss of hydrogen and chloride ions raises serum bicarbonate, while volume contraction activates aldosterone and worsens renal potassium loss.\"],\"tk\":\"Hypernatremia is not the typical sodium abnormality in hyperemesis gravidarum.\",\"pr\":\"Give thiamine before dextrose in prolonged vomiting to reduce the risk of precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy.\",\"tp\":\"Check electrolytes, renal function, weight, hydration, and nutrition; replace potassium and chloride carefully while treating nausea and excluding alternative diagnoses.\",\"cn\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum typically causes hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia and hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, not hypernatraemia.\",\"sl\":\"Hyperemesis gravidarum: intractable vomiting, dehydration, alkalosis, hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, weight loss more than 5%, elevated haematocrit and ketonuria.\",\"s\":[[\"L04_0\",[4]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F368\"},{\"id\":\"F529\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Meigs syndrome and stromal tumors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about ovarian sex-cord stromal tumors is most accurate?\",\"o\":[\"Ovarian fibroma is classically associated with Meigs syndrome; thecoma is often estrogenic\",\"Thecoma is the classic tumor of Meigs syndrome and is hormonally inactive\",\"Granulosa-cell tumor is characteristically associated with lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) rather than inhibin\",\"Dysgerminoma typically secretes inhibin\",\"Yolk-sac tumor is usually monitored with cancer antigen 125 (CA-125)\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Ovarian fibroma classically causes Meigs syndrome when accompanied by ascites and pleural effusion; thecomas are stromal tumors that can secrete estrogen.\",\"The classic Meigs tumor is a fibroma, and thecomas may be hormonally active through estrogen production.\",\"Granulosa-cell tumors commonly produce inhibin and estrogen; lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) is not their characteristic marker.\",\"Dysgerminoma is associated with LDH, while inhibin is more characteristic of granulosa-cell tumors.\",\"A yolk-sac tumor is associated with alpha-fetoprotein rather than cancer antigen 125 (CA-125).\"],\"e\":[\"Sex-cord stromal tumors arise from ovarian stromal and sex-cord elements and may produce sex steroids or peptide hormones.\",\"Meigs syndrome is classically the triad of ovarian fibroma, ascites, and pleural effusion; the effusions resolve after tumor removal.\",\"Thecoma contains theca-like cells and may produce estrogen, whereas fibroma is predominantly collagen-producing and hormonally inactive.\"],\"tk\":\"Meigs syndrome is classically linked to ovarian fibroma, while thecoma may be estrogen producing.\",\"pr\":\"Granulosa-cell tumors can cause postmenopausal bleeding or precocious puberty through estrogen secretion and are often followed with inhibin.\",\"tp\":\"When a solid ovarian mass coexists with ascites and pleural effusion, remember that Meigs syndrome can mimic advanced ovarian cancer.\",\"cn\":\"Ovarian fibroma is the classic tumour of Meigs syndrome, while thecoma is often oestrogen-secreting.\",\"sl\":\"Sex cord-stromal tumours: granulosa cell tumour secretes inhibin; thecoma/fibroma group usually benign except fibrosarcoma. Meigs syndrome is not named on the slides.\",\"s\":[[\"L41_0\",[6]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F529\"},{\"id\":\"F530\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":5,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"GBS prevention during labor\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which antibiotic is preferred for intrapartum prophylaxis against neonatal group B streptococcal disease in a woman without penicillin allergy?\",\"o\":[\"Intravenous penicillin G\",\"Metronidazole\",\"Doxycycline\",\"Fluconazole\",\"Ceftriaxone\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Penicillin G is the preferred intrapartum antibiotic for eligible women with GBS colonization because GBS remains highly susceptible and penicillin has a narrow spectrum.\",\"Metronidazole targets anaerobes and protozoa rather than group B Streptococcus.\",\"Doxycycline is not used for intrapartum GBS prophylaxis and is generally avoided in pregnancy when safer alternatives are appropriate.\",\"Fluconazole is an antifungal and has no role in preventing neonatal early-onset group B streptococcal disease.\",\"Ceftriaxone treats gonorrhea and several invasive bacterial infections but is not the preferred narrow-spectrum intrapartum agent for GBS prophylaxis.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal rectovaginal group B Streptococcus colonization can cause early-onset neonatal sepsis, pneumonia, and meningitis.\",\"Intrapartum penicillin G is the preferred prophylactic antibiotic for eligible colonized women who do not have a relevant penicillin allergy.\",\"Antibiotic concentrations in maternal blood and amniotic fluid reduce the newborn's exposure to GBS during labor and birth.\"],\"tk\":\"Penicillin G is the preferred intrapartum agent for GBS prophylaxis when there is no penicillin allergy.\",\"pr\":\"Previous infant with invasive GBS disease or GBS bacteriuria in the current pregnancy are major indications for intrapartum prophylaxis.\",\"tp\":\"Verify the allergy history and susceptibility information early enough to choose an appropriate beta-lactam or allergy alternative before labor becomes advanced.\",\"cn\":\"Intravenous benzylpenicillin (penicillin G) is the first-line intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis (IAP) for group B Streptococcus (GBS).\",\"sl\":\"Benzylpenicillin (penicillin G) is the standard first-line intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis (IAP): 3 g intravenous loading dose, then 1.5 g intravenously every 4 hours until delivery.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[58]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F530\"},{\"id\":\"F531\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Vacuum cup flexion point\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about vacuum-assisted vaginal birth is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Vacuum should not be applied to a face presentation\",\"The cervix should be fully dilated before vacuum extraction\",\"The flexion point is 3 cm anterior to the anterior fontanelle\",\"Prerequisites include an engaged head and no suspected cephalopelvic disproportion\",\"The fetal head position must be known before cup application\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Vacuum is contraindicated in face presentation because cup application to the face can cause serious trauma.\",\"Operative vaginal birth requires full cervical dilatation to avoid cervical injury and obstruction.\",\"The flexion point lies on the sagittal suture about 3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle, not anterior to the anterior fontanelle.\",\"An engaged head and absence of suspected major cephalopelvic disproportion are essential prerequisites for safe vacuum birth.\",\"The clinician must know the fetal head position so the cup is applied correctly and traction follows the pelvic axis.\"],\"e\":[\"Vacuum extraction requires full dilatation, ruptured membranes, known fetal position, an engaged head, adequate pelvis, and appropriate cup placement.\",\"The flexion point is on the sagittal suture approximately 3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle; placing the cup near the anterior fontanelle promotes deflexion.\",\"Correct flexion-point placement encourages the smallest effective head diameter to descend and reduces cup detachment and failed extraction.\"],\"tk\":\"The vacuum flexion point is near the posterior fontanelle, not anterior to the anterior fontanelle.\",\"pr\":\"Vacuum is avoided in face presentation and at very preterm gestations because of fetal injury risk.\",\"tp\":\"Before traction, confirm position and station abdominally and vaginally, empty the bladder, obtain consent, and have a clear stop plan for failed operative birth.\",\"cn\":\"The vacuum flexion point lies on the sagittal suture about 3 cm anterior to the posterior fontanelle.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F531\"},{\"id\":\"F532\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":26,\"ty\":[\"Cause\",\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Autoimmune thyroid dysfunction after birth\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about postpartum thyroiditis is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"It usually occurs within the first postpartum year\",\"It may have a transient thyrotoxic phase followed by hypothyroidism\",\"Some women develop permanent hypothyroidism\",\"Only about 30% of affected women have thyroid-peroxidase antibodies\",\"Thyroid-peroxidase antibodies are commonly present\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Postpartum thyroiditis occurs during the first year after delivery in women without pre-existing overt thyroid disease.\",\"Postpartum thyroiditis often follows a thyrotoxic-hypothyroid-biphasic course, although some women present with only one phase.\",\"Although many recover euthyroidism, a proportion develop permanent hypothyroidism and need long-term surveillance.\",\"The claim that only 30% have thyroid-peroxidase antibodies understates their strong autoimmune association and is not a reliable defining statistic.\",\"Autoimmune postpartum thyroiditis is strongly associated with thyroid-peroxidase antibodies, particularly in women positive during pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Postpartum thyroiditis is an autoimmune destructive thyroiditis occurring in the first postpartum year, often in thyroid-peroxidase-antibody-positive women.\",\"A fixed statement that only 30% of cases have TPO antibodies is inaccurate because antibody positivity is strongly associated with the disorder.\",\"Follicular destruction releases preformed hormone during the thyrotoxic phase and can be followed by temporary depletion and hypothyroidism.\"],\"tk\":\"Postpartum thyroiditis is strongly associated with thyroid-peroxidase antibodies.\",\"pr\":\"Antithyroid drugs do not treat the thyrotoxic phase because excess hormone comes from destructive release rather than increased synthesis.\",\"tp\":\"Distinguish postpartum thyroiditis from Graves disease using timing, examination, antibodies, and—when necessary—TSH-receptor antibody testing.\",\"cn\":\"Postpartum thyroiditis is autoimmune: thyroid peroxidase (TPO) antibodies are present in most affected women, not only 30%.\",\"sl\":\"Postpartum thyroiditis: transient hyperthyroid phase followed by hypothyroid phase; can mimic depression. The slides do not mention thyroid peroxidase (TPO) antibodies.\",\"s\":[[\"L26_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F061\"},{\"id\":\"F533\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":9,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Benign ovarian cyst morphology\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which adnexal-mass description is most characteristic of a benign simple ovarian cyst?\",\"o\":[\"Postmenopausal multilocular cyst with papillary projections\",\"Complex cyst with solid vascular nodules\",\"Rapidly enlarging irregular solid adnexal mass\",\"Simple unilateral cyst less than 5 cm\",\"Bilateral fixed solid masses with ascites\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"In a postmenopausal woman, multilocularity and papillary projections increase concern for epithelial ovarian malignancy.\",\"Solid vascular nodules within a complex cyst are suspicious ultrasound morphology and warrant further risk assessment.\",\"A rapidly enlarging irregular solid mass is not the typical appearance of a benign physiologic cyst.\",\"A simple, thin-walled, unilateral cyst under 5 cm in a premenopausal woman is commonly functional and has a very low malignancy risk.\",\"Bilateral fixed solid masses with ascites are concerning for malignancy rather than a simple benign functional cyst.\"],\"e\":[\"Ultrasound risk assessment of an adnexal mass considers size, wall characteristics, septations, solid components, vascularity, laterality, and ascites.\",\"A small simple unilateral cyst in a premenopausal woman is usually functional and carries very low malignant potential.\",\"Papillary projections, solid vascular components, irregularity, bilaterality, and ascites reflect increasing neoplastic complexity rather than a physiologic follicular cyst.\"],\"tk\":\"A simple unilateral cyst under 5 cm is usually a benign finding in a premenopausal woman.\",\"pr\":\"Management depends strongly on menopausal status and symptoms, so the same cyst morphology may prompt different follow-up in different patients.\",\"tp\":\"Document cyst size and morphology precisely and use the appropriate local adnexal-mass risk pathway rather than ordering tumor markers reflexively for every simple cyst.\",\"cn\":\"A simple unilateral ovarian cyst under 5 cm is the typical benign, usually physiological, finding.\",\"sl\":\"Simple ovarian cysts under 50 mm are very likely physiological and almost always resolve within 3 menstrual cycles. Complex cysts show ascites, bilateral lesions, septations, solid components or papillary projections, or metastases.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[16,8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F533\"},{\"id\":\"F534\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Screening\",\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Maternal serum AFP abnormalities\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein is increased in all of the following conditions except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Abdominal-wall defect\",\"Fetal demise\",\"Multiple gestation\",\"Down syndrome\",\"Open neural-tube defect\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Open abdominal-wall defects such as gastroschisis and omphalocele can raise maternal serum AFP.\",\"Fetal demise can increase maternal serum AFP through tissue breakdown and altered fetal-maternal transfer.\",\"A multiple gestation increases total fetal AFP production and can produce a higher maternal serum concentration.\",\"Maternal serum AFP is typically decreased in Down syndrome, particularly as part of second-trimester aneuploidy screening patterns.\",\"Open neural-tube defects allow AFP to leak into amniotic fluid and maternal serum, producing elevated levels.\"],\"e\":[\"Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is produced mainly by the fetal liver and crosses into maternal blood.\",\"Open fetal defects and multiple gestation tend to increase AFP, whereas Down syndrome is associated with a reduced maternal serum AFP concentration in classic second-trimester screening.\",\"Open neural-tube or abdominal-wall lesions permit greater transfer of fetal AFP into amniotic fluid and then the maternal circulation.\"],\"tk\":\"Down syndrome is associated with low rather than high maternal serum AFP.\",\"pr\":\"An abnormal AFP result should first prompt confirmation of gestational dating and fetal number, because both strongly affect the expected value.\",\"tp\":\"Do not diagnose a fetal anomaly from serum screening alone; arrange the indicated targeted ultrasound and aneuploidy evaluation based on the screening context.\",\"cn\":\"Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is raised in open fetal defects, multiple gestation and fetal demise, but reduced in Down syndrome.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F025\"},{\"id\":\"F535\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Twin-pregnancy complications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about twin pregnancy are true except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Twin gestation increases the risk of preterm birth\",\"Twin pregnancy has a higher risk of post-maturity syndrome than singleton pregnancy\",\"Family history mainly predisposes to dizygotic twinning\",\"Malpresentation is more common in twin pregnancy\",\"Monochorionic twins require closer surveillance for placental complications\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Twins have a markedly increased incidence of preterm birth compared with singleton pregnancies.\",\"Twin gestations usually deliver earlier and are not characterized by an increased risk of post-maturity syndrome.\",\"Familial multiple ovulation increases the likelihood of dizygotic twinning rather than monozygotic splitting.\",\"Limited intrauterine space and two fetuses make non-vertex presentations more common in twins.\",\"Monochorionic twins need closer surveillance because shared placental circulation can cause twin–twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS) and selective growth restriction.\"],\"e\":[\"Multiple gestation is associated with earlier delivery and increased preterm birth, growth problems, malpresentation, and hypertensive complications.\",\"An increased risk of post-maturity syndrome is not characteristic of twins because spontaneous and indicated delivery commonly occur earlier than in singleton pregnancies.\",\"Uterine overdistension and placental complications shift the gestational-age distribution toward preterm delivery.\"],\"tk\":\"Twin pregnancy increases preterm, not post-maturity, risk.\",\"pr\":\"Monochorionic placentation carries unique risks including twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome and selective fetal growth restriction.\",\"tp\":\"Confirm chorionicity early and follow the appropriate twin-surveillance and planned-birth schedule rather than applying singleton timing.\",\"cn\":\"Twins deliver earlier and have more preterm birth and malpresentation; they are not at increased risk of postmaturity syndrome.\",\"sl\":\"Each extra fetus shortens pregnancy: mean gestation at birth is about 39.0 weeks for singletons and 35.3 weeks for twins. Fetal risks include preterm birth (main driver), fetal growth restriction (FGR) and discordance, stillbirth and anomalies.\",\"s\":[[\"L23_0\",[26,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F066\"},{\"id\":\"F536\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Mx\",\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Induction-of-labor principles\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about induction of labor is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Induction can be offered for selected maternal, fetal, or gestational indications\",\"Induced labor is generally less painful than spontaneous labor\",\"Bishop score helps assess cervical favorability before induction\",\"Oxytocin can be used for induction or augmentation with appropriate monitoring\",\"Prostaglandins may be used for cervical ripening when not contraindicated\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Induction may be indicated for conditions such as post-term pregnancy, hypertensive disease, or membrane rupture, depending on the clinical setting.\",\"Induced labor is not reliably less painful; uterotonic agents can generate strong, frequent contractions, and analgesia needs should be anticipated.\",\"The Bishop score assesses dilatation, effacement, station, consistency, and position to estimate cervical favorability before induction.\",\"Oxytocin is a standard induction/augmentation agent, but tachysystole and fetal heart-rate changes require dose adjustment and monitoring.\",\"Prostaglandins can ripen an unfavorable cervix but require attention to contraindications, particularly certain prior uterine scars.\"],\"e\":[\"Induction of labor uses mechanical or pharmacologic methods to initiate labor when the expected benefit of birth exceeds the risk of continued pregnancy, with cervical favorability guiding method choice.\",\"The claim that induced labor is generally less painful than spontaneous labor is incorrect; prostaglandins and oxytocin may produce intense contractions.\",\"Oxytocin increases intracellular calcium in myometrial cells, so excessive dosing can cause uterine tachysystole and fetal hypoxia.\"],\"tk\":\"Induction should not be described as less painful than spontaneous labor.\",\"pr\":\"An unfavorable cervix often requires cervical ripening before or alongside amniotomy and oxytocin.\",\"tp\":\"Before starting induction, document the indication, presentation, cervical assessment, fetal status, uterine-scar history, and a plan for monitoring tachysystole.\",\"cn\":\"Induced labour is more painful than spontaneous labour and more likely to need epidural analgesia and assisted birth.\",\"sl\":\"Induction may be less efficient, more painful and more likely to require epidural analgesia and assisted birth; explain to women that induced labour may be more painful than spontaneous labour.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[14,19,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F536\"},{\"id\":\"F537\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Primary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"All of the following statements about primary postpartum hemorrhage are correct except which one?\",\"o\":[\"Cesarean delivery increases the efficacy of uterine-artery embolization\",\"Active management of the third stage reduces postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) risk\",\"Persistent bleeding requires rapid escalation while resuscitation continues\",\"Uterine atony is the commonest cause of primary PPH\",\"Oxytocin is a first-line uterotonic for atonic PPH\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cesarean delivery does not inherently increase the efficacy of uterine-artery embolization; embolization success depends on bleeding source, hemodynamic state, anatomy, and interventional expertise.\",\"Active management of the third stage, including prophylactic uterotonic use, reduces the risk of postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"Ongoing postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) requires simultaneous resuscitation, cause-directed treatment, tranexamic acid when indicated, and escalation to tamponade, embolization, or surgery.\",\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of primary postpartum hemorrhage and is promoted by overdistension, prolonged labor, and uterine-relaxing drugs.\",\"Oxytocin is a first-line uterotonic for prevention and initial treatment of uterine-atony-related hemorrhage.\"],\"e\":[\"Primary postpartum hemorrhage is most commonly caused by uterine atony, while trauma, retained tissue, and coagulopathy are the other major etiologic groups.\",\"Cesarean birth is a hemorrhage risk factor but does not make uterine-artery embolization intrinsically more effective; that claim confuses risk with treatment efficacy.\",\"Atony reflects failure of myometrial contraction to compress the spiral arteries at the placental bed after separation.\"],\"tk\":\"Cesarean delivery does not itself increase the efficacy of uterine-artery embolization.\",\"pr\":\"The 2025 World Health Organization (WHO) consolidated guidance emphasizes rapid recognition and bundled treatment of PPH rather than sequential delay between interventions.\",\"tp\":\"In active hemorrhage, quantify blood loss, call for help, obtain large-bore access and bloods, give uterotonics and tranexamic acid promptly when indicated, and escalate by cause and response.\",\"cn\":\"Caesarean delivery, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and transfusion over 10 units are associated with failed uterine artery embolisation.\",\"sl\":\"Selective arterial occlusion or embolisation by interventional radiology: caesarean delivery, disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) and transfusion of more than 10 units of packed red cells were related to failed embolisation.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F537\"},{\"id\":\"F538\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":8,\"L\":14,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"PID diagnosis and genital-tract microscopy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) and common genital-tract infections is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"Diagnosis of uncomplicated PID is primarily clinical\",\"Chlamydia trachomatis is best detected with nucleic acid amplification testing\",\"Clue cells on vaginal microscopy diagnose vulvovaginal candidiasis\",\"Transvaginal ultrasound is useful when a tubo-ovarian abscess or an alternate pelvic diagnosis is suspected\",\"Budding yeast or pseudohyphae on microscopy support vulvovaginal candidiasis\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Uncomplicated pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) is usually diagnosed clinically from pelvic or lower-abdominal pain plus cervical-motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness; imaging is reserved when diagnostic uncertainty or complications such as a tubo-ovarian abscess are concerns.\",\"Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT) is the recommended and most sensitive test for urogenital Chlamydia trachomatis, using vaginal/cervical swabs or first-void urine as appropriate.\",\"Clue cells are vaginal epithelial cells coated with bacteria and are characteristic of bacterial vaginosis, whereas candidiasis is identified by yeast forms or pseudohyphae.\",\"Transvaginal ultrasonography can show thickened fluid-filled tubes or a tubo-ovarian complex and is especially useful when complicated PID or an alternative pelvic diagnosis is being considered.\",\"Vulvovaginal candidiasis can show budding yeast, hyphae, or pseudohyphae on wet mount or potassium hydroxide microscopy, typically with pruritus and vulvar inflammation.\"],\"e\":[\"Pelvic inflammatory disease is a clinical syndrome of upper-genital-tract infection, and treatment should not be delayed while awaiting imaging when minimum clinical criteria are met.\",\"The false statement assigns clue cells to Candida; clue cells instead indicate bacterial vaginosis, while candidiasis is demonstrated by yeast forms or pseudohyphae.\",\"This distinction reflects the organisms involved: bacterial coating of epithelial cells produces clue cells, whereas Candida is a budding yeast that forms hyphae or pseudohyphae under suitable conditions.\"],\"tk\":\"Clue cells indicate bacterial vaginosis, not vulvovaginal candidiasis, while uncomplicated PID remains primarily a clinical diagnosis.\",\"pr\":\"A normal pelvic ultrasound does not exclude PID; imaging becomes more valuable when tubo-ovarian abscess, torsion, or another structural diagnosis is suspected.\",\"tp\":\"In a patient with suspected PID, obtain pregnancy testing and sexually transmitted infection (STI) samples promptly, but start empiric broad-spectrum treatment when clinical criteria are met rather than waiting for confirmatory imaging.\",\"cn\":\"Clue cells indicate bacterial vaginosis (BV); yeast and pseudohyphae indicate vulvovaginal candidiasis.\",\"sl\":\"Bacterial vaginosis is diagnosed by Amsel's criteria (3 out of 4). Candidiasis: microscopy of vaginal smear and Gram stain show hyphae and spores; culture on Sabouraud agar.\",\"s\":[[\"L14_0\",[4,10]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F538\"},{\"id\":\"F539\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Cerebral hemorrhage in severe preeclampsia\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In severe preeclampsia or eclampsia, which complication is a major cause of maternal death and is closely linked to uncontrolled severe hypertension?\",\"o\":[\"Intracranial cerebral hemorrhage\",\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation\",\"Acute kidney injury\",\"Pulmonary edema\",\"Subcapsular hepatic hematoma with rupture\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intracranial hemorrhage is a major fatal complication of severe preeclampsia/eclampsia because abrupt severe hypertension can exceed cerebral autoregulatory capacity and cause hemorrhagic stroke.\",\"Disseminated intravascular coagulation can complicate severe placental abruption or haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome and causes consumption of platelets and clotting factors with diffuse bleeding.\",\"Acute kidney injury may occur from endothelial injury or renal hypoperfusion, typically manifesting as rising creatinine and oliguria rather than a primary cerebrovascular event.\",\"Pulmonary edema is a life-threatening severe feature caused by endothelial leak, reduced oncotic pressure, cardiac dysfunction, or excess intravascular fluid, requiring careful fluid management.\",\"A subcapsular hepatic hematoma is an uncommon but catastrophic complication of HELLP syndrome that can rupture and cause hemoperitoneum and hemorrhagic shock.\"],\"e\":[\"Severe preeclampsia can cause lethal neurologic, pulmonary, hepatic, renal, and hematologic complications, with intracranial hemorrhage historically accounting for a large share of maternal deaths.\",\"In a woman with severe hypertension or eclampsia, the danger of hemorrhagic stroke makes rapid blood-pressure treatment as important as seizure prevention with magnesium sulfate.\",\"Cerebral endothelial dysfunction and loss of autoregulation allow sudden pressure surges to injure small cerebral vessels, producing intracerebral bleeding and secondary brain injury.\"],\"tk\":\"Intracranial hemorrhage is a major cause of maternal death in severe preeclampsia/eclampsia, particularly when severe hypertension is not treated promptly.\",\"pr\":\"Acute-onset persistent blood pressure of 160/110 mmHg or higher in pregnancy is a hypertensive emergency requiring prompt treatment to reduce severe maternal morbidity.\",\"tp\":\"When severe-range blood pressure is confirmed, arrange urgent antihypertensive therapy and neurologic assessment while giving magnesium sulfate when indicated for eclampsia treatment or seizure prophylaxis.\",\"cn\":\"Intracranial (cerebral) haemorrhage from uncontrolled severe hypertension is a leading direct cause of maternal death in pre-eclampsia and eclampsia.\",\"sl\":\"Maternal complications of pre-eclampsia include renal failure, acute cardiac failure, pulmonary oedema, thrombocytopenia, disseminated intravascular coagulopathy (DIC) and cerebrovascular accidents.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[50,53]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":4,\"rs\":\"F099\"},{\"id\":\"F540\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":7,\"L\":35,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Combined oral contraceptives and cancer risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Use of combined oral contraceptive pills is associated with a small increased risk of which cancer?\",\"o\":[\"Colorectal cancer\",\"Ovarian cancer\",\"Vulvar cancer\",\"Endometrial cancer\",\"Cervical cancer\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Combined oral contraceptive use is not known to increase colorectal cancer risk and has been associated in epidemiologic studies with a modest risk reduction.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives confer durable protection against epithelial ovarian cancer, with a larger risk reduction after longer cumulative use.\",\"Vulvar cancer is principally associated with persistent high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) infection or HPV-independent pathways such as lichen sclerosus, rather than combined oral contraceptive exposure.\",\"Progestin opposition of estrogenic endometrial stimulation gives combined oral contraceptives a protective effect against endometrial cancer by reducing unopposed endometrial proliferation.\",\"Longer-duration combined oral contraceptive use is associated with a small increase in cervical cancer risk, although persistent high-risk HPV infection remains the major causal factor for most cervical cancers.\"],\"e\":[\"Combined oral contraceptives have different cancer associations: they reduce the risk of ovarian and endometrial cancers while epidemiologic data show a small increase in cervical cancer risk with longer use.\",\"Among the listed cancers, cervical cancer is therefore the increased-risk association, whereas ovarian and endometrial cancers are protected against.\",\"The cervical association is interpreted alongside persistent high-risk HPV infection, which drives cervical carcinogenesis and remains the dominant causal exposure.\"],\"tk\":\"Combined oral contraceptive use is associated with a small increase in cervical cancer risk while reducing ovarian and endometrial cancer risk.\",\"pr\":\"The protective effect against ovarian and endometrial cancer persists for years after oral contraceptive discontinuation, making cumulative duration of use clinically relevant.\",\"tp\":\"When counseling about combined hormonal contraception, discuss the patient’s HPV vaccination and cervical-screening status separately from contraceptive eligibility because screening prevents morbidity regardless of contraceptive choice.\",\"cn\":\"Combined oral contraceptives (COC) carry a slight increase in cervical cancer risk but reduce ovarian, endometrial and colorectal cancer.\",\"sl\":\"Cancer: breast small increase (10/1000 to 11/1000), cervical slight increased risk, liver slight increase in benign and malignant tumours; endometrial and ovarian cancer decrease by 50% and colorectal by 20%.\",\"s\":[[\"L35_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F540\"},{\"id\":\"F541\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":2,\"L\":3,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Antiseizure medication in pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which statement about antiseizure medication use in pregnancy is incorrect?\",\"o\":[\"When clinically appropriate, lamotrigine, levetiracetam, or oxcarbazepine can be considered to minimize major congenital malformation risk\",\"Valproic acid should be avoided when feasible because of major congenital malformation and adverse neurodevelopmental risks\",\"Abruptly stopping an effective antiseizure medication during pregnancy can increase seizure frequency and endanger the pregnant patient and fetus\",\"Multi-agent antiseizure treatment is routinely preferred because it is less teratogenic than effective single-agent treatment\",\"At least 0.4 mg of folic acid daily is recommended before and during pregnancy for people with epilepsy who may become pregnant\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The 2024 AAN/AES/Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (SMFM) guideline identifies lamotrigine, levetiracetam, and oxcarbazepine as options to consider when appropriate because they have comparatively favorable major-congenital-malformation profiles.\",\"Valproic acid carries substantial dose-related risks of major congenital malformations and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes, so alternatives should be used when they can maintain effective seizure control.\",\"Stopping antiseizure therapy can provoke tonic-clonic seizures, which can cause maternal trauma, hypoxemia, and fetal compromise; medication changes should therefore be planned rather than abrupt.\",\"Using multiple antiseizure medications is not routinely safer; teratogenic risk depends strongly on the specific drug and dose, and unnecessary multi-agent exposure should be avoided when one effective agent can maintain seizure control.\",\"The 2024 guideline recommends at least 0.4 mg folic acid daily before and during pregnancy for people with epilepsy who may become pregnant, while acknowledging uncertainty about the optimal dose.\"],\"e\":[\"Pregnancy management in epilepsy balances seizure control against medication-related fetal risks, and regimen choice should be optimized before conception whenever possible.\",\"The incorrect statement claims that polytherapy is routinely less teratogenic; contemporary guidance instead emphasizes selecting the safest effective drug and dose and avoiding unnecessary exposure to higher-risk agents.\",\"Antiseizure-medication teratogenicity is drug- and dose-dependent, while uncontrolled tonic-clonic seizures independently threaten maternal and fetal safety, so indiscriminate medication reduction can be harmful.\"],\"tk\":\"Effective antiseizure therapy in pregnancy should use the safest appropriate medication and dose while preserving seizure control; polytherapy is not inherently safer than monotherapy.\",\"pr\":\"Serum concentrations of lamotrigine and levetiracetam may fall during pregnancy because clearance increases, so clinically guided level monitoring can help prevent breakthrough seizures.\",\"tp\":\"Review antiseizure medication before conception whenever possible; once pregnancy is established, do not abruptly stop an effective regimen solely because of fetal-risk concern—coordinate changes with neurology and maternal-fetal medicine.\",\"cn\":\"Antiepileptic monotherapy at the lowest effective dose is preferred; polytherapy carries greater teratogenic risk than monotherapy.\",\"sl\":\"Polytherapy with more than one drug carries greater risk than monotherapy; simplify to a single antiepileptic drug (AED) at the lowest effective dose. Valproate carries the greatest risk; levetiracetam and lamotrigine are less harmful; folic acid 5 mg daily.\",\"s\":[[\"L03_0\",[3,10,11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F541\"},{\"id\":\"F542\",\"k\":\"F\",\"b\":\"yaqeen\",\"g\":5,\"L\":5,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Congenital toxoplasmosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which constellation is the classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis?\",\"o\":[\"Sensorineural hearing loss, patent ductus arteriosus, and cataracts\",\"Hydrocephalus, retinochoroiditis, and intracranial calcifications\",\"Hydrops fetalis, severe fetal anemia, and cardiomegaly\",\"Periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, and sensorineural hearing loss\",\"Snuffles, hepatosplenomegaly, and osteochondritis\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Congenital rubella classically causes sensorineural deafness, eye disease such as cataracts, and congenital heart disease including patent ductus arteriosus.\",\"Congenital toxoplasmosis classically produces the triad of hydrocephalus, retinochoroiditis, and intracranial calcifications, although many infected newborns are initially asymptomatic.\",\"Parvovirus B19 can cause profound fetal anemia leading to high-output cardiac failure, cardiomegaly, and nonimmune hydrops fetalis.\",\"Congenital cytomegalovirus commonly causes periventricular calcifications, microcephaly, growth restriction, and sensorineural hearing loss.\",\"Congenital syphilis may cause snuffles, hepatosplenomegaly, rash, and osteochondritis, with later stigmata if untreated.\"],\"e\":[\"Congenital toxoplasmosis is caused by transplacental infection with Toxoplasma gondii, usually after primary maternal infection during pregnancy.\",\"Its classic triad is retinochoroiditis, hydrocephalus, and intracranial calcifications, although the full triad is not required and many affected newborns appear normal initially.\",\"Fetal injury results from parasite invasion and inflammation of neural and retinal tissue, producing necrosis and calcification that can lead to seizures, visual impairment, and hydrocephalus.\"],\"tk\":\"The classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis is hydrocephalus, retinochoroiditis, and intracranial calcifications.\",\"pr\":\"Transmission risk generally increases with advancing gestation, but fetal disease is often more severe when maternal primary infection occurs earlier in pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"When maternal toxoplasmosis is suspected, document timing of infection and fetal assessment carefully and involve maternal-fetal medicine or infectious-disease expertise because interpretation and treatment depend on gestational age and evidence of fetal infection.\",\"cn\":\"Congenital toxoplasmosis classic triad: chorioretinitis, intracranial calcifications and hydrocephalus.\",\"sl\":\"Fetal toxoplasma infection: chorioretinitis, intracranial calcifications, hydrocephalus.\",\"s\":[[\"L05_0\",[8]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"F542\"},{\"id\":\"M01\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Vertex presentation skull diameter\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a well-flexed vertex presentation during labor, which fetal skull diameter presents to the maternal pelvis?\",\"o\":[\"Mentovertical diameter\",\"Biparietal diameter\",\"Occipitofrontal diameter\",\"Suboccipitobregmatic diameter\",\"Submentobregmatic diameter\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Mentovertical diameter is about 13.5 cm and is the large presenting diameter of brow presentation.\",\"Biparietal diameter is the transverse diameter whose passage through the pelvic inlet defines engagement.\",\"Occipitofrontal diameter is about 11.5 cm and is associated with a deflexed or sinciput presentation.\",\"A well-flexed vertex presents the suboccipitobregmatic diameter. A well-flexed vertex presentation brings the occiput into the leading position and presents the smallest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head.\",\"Submentobregmatic diameter is about 9.5 cm and presents with a fully extended face.\"],\"e\":[\"A well-flexed vertex presentation brings the occiput into the leading position and presents the smallest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head.\",\"In this patient, a well-flexed vertex presents the suboccipitobregmatic diameter.\",\"The suboccipitobregmatic diameter is approximately 9.5 cm and allows the fetal head to negotiate the pelvis most efficiently. Deflexion increases the presenting diameter and may contribute to labor dystocia.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the presenting fetal skull diameter in vertex presentation.\",\"pr\":\"The smaller the presenting fetal head diameter, the more favorable the mechanics of labor.\",\"tp\":\"When assessing labor progress, relate the presenting part to fetal head flexion and station.\",\"cn\":\"A well-flexed vertex presents the suboccipitobregmatic diameter, the smallest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head.\",\"sl\":\"When the vertex presents and the fetal head is well flexed, the smallest anteroposterior diameter, suboccipito-bregmatic, enters the birth canal.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[14,26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M01\"},{\"id\":\"M02\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Face presentation skull diameter\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which fetal skull diameter engages in a face presentation when the fetal head is fully extended?\",\"o\":[\"Suboccipitobregmatic diameter\",\"Mentovertical diameter\",\"Biparietal diameter\",\"Submentobregmatic diameter\",\"Occipitofrontal diameter\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Suboccipitobregmatic diameter is about 9.5 cm and presents with a well-flexed vertex.\",\"Mentovertical diameter is about 13.5 cm and is the large presenting diameter of brow presentation.\",\"Biparietal diameter is the transverse diameter whose passage through the pelvic inlet defines engagement.\",\"Complete extension in face presentation engages the submentobregmatic diameter. Face presentation occurs when the fetal head is fully extended so that the face becomes the presenting part.\",\"Occipitofrontal diameter is about 11.5 cm and is associated with a deflexed or sinciput presentation.\"],\"e\":[\"Face presentation occurs when the fetal head is fully extended so that the face becomes the presenting part.\",\"This presentation shows that complete extension in face presentation engages the submentobregmatic diameter.\",\"With complete extension, the submentobregmatic diameter, about 9.5 cm, is the engaging anteroposterior diameter. Vaginal delivery is possible only when the mentum rotates anteriorly.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the engaging diameter in face presentation.\",\"pr\":\"Face presentation can deliver vaginally only if the chin rotates anteriorly.\",\"tp\":\"Always document mentum anterior versus posterior in face presentation.\",\"cn\":\"In face presentation the fully extended head presents the submentobregmatic diameter, about 9.5 cm.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F390\"},{\"id\":\"M03\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Brow presentation recognition\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During vaginal examination in labor, the frontal bone and supraorbital ridges are palpated as the presenting structures. Which presentation and diameter are most consistent with this finding?\",\"o\":[\"Brow presentation with mentovertical diameter\",\"Face presentation with submentobregmatic diameter\",\"Breech presentation with bitrochanteric diameter\",\"Sinciput presentation with occipitofrontal diameter\",\"Vertex presentation with suboccipitobregmatic diameter\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Palpation of the brow structures indicates brow presentation with the mentovertical diameter. Brow presentation is an intermediate deflexed attitude between vertex and face presentation.\",\"Submentobregmatic diameter is about 9.5 cm and presents with a fully extended face.\",\"Bitrochanteric diameter is a transverse diameter relevant to a breech presentation.\",\"Occipitofrontal diameter is about 11.5 cm and is associated with a deflexed or sinciput presentation.\",\"Suboccipitobregmatic diameter is about 9.5 cm and presents with a well-flexed vertex.\"],\"e\":[\"Brow presentation is an intermediate deflexed attitude between vertex and face presentation.\",\"In this patient, palpation of the brow structures indicates brow presentation with the mentovertical diameter.\",\"The brow, including the frontal bone and supraorbital ridges, becomes the presenting part. It presents the mentovertical diameter, approximately 13.5 cm, which is the largest anteroposterior diameter and usually prevents vaginal delivery if persistent.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish brow presentation from face and vertex presentations by examination findings.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent brow presentation usually cannot pass through a normal pelvis because the presenting diameter is too large.\",\"tp\":\"If the brow is felt, reassess because it may convert to vertex or face, but persistent brow usually needs cesarean delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Palpable frontal bone and supraorbital ridges indicate brow presentation, which presents the mentovertical diameter (about 13.5 cm).\",\"sl\":\"In brow presentation the denominator is the frontal bone; brow presentation results from partial deflexion of the fetal neck, and a persistent brow cannot deliver vaginally.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[9,12]],[\"L16_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M03\"},{\"id\":\"M04\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Fetal station at ischial spines\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The leading bony point of the fetal head is palpated at the level of the maternal ischial spines. What station does this represent?\",\"o\":[\"Station +2\",\"Station −3\",\"Station 0\",\"Station +1\",\"Station −1\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Station +2 places the presenting part 2 cm below the ischial spines.\",\"Station −3 places the presenting part several centimeters above the ischial spines.\",\"The presenting part at the ischial spines is station 0. Fetal station describes the relationship of the presenting part to the ischial spines.\",\"Station +1 places the presenting part 1 cm below the ischial spines.\",\"Station −1 places the presenting part 1 cm above the ischial spines.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal station describes the relationship of the presenting part to the ischial spines.\",\"This presentation shows that the presenting part at the ischial spines is station 0.\",\"The ischial spines define station 0 and correspond clinically to the midpelvis. Stations above the spines are negative and stations below the spines are positive.\"],\"tk\":\"Use the ischial spines as the reference point for fetal station.\",\"pr\":\"The ischial spines are the zero point for fetal station.\",\"tp\":\"Record station numerically rather than only saying the head is high or low.\",\"cn\":\"Station 0 means the leading bony part of the fetal head is at the level of the maternal ischial spines.\",\"sl\":\"Station is the level of the denominator of the presenting part above or below the plane of the ischial spines; the ischial spines are palpable vaginally and used to assess descent.\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[3,16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":3,\"rs\":\"F049\"},{\"id\":\"M05\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Engagement and ballottement\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"On abdominal examination in labor, the fetal head is ballottable and has not engaged. Where is the presenting part located?\",\"o\":[\"Above the pelvic inlet\",\"Below the pelvic outlet\",\"Within the midpelvis below the ischial spines\",\"At the level of the ischial spines\",\"At the pelvic floor\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A ballottable unengaged head remains above the pelvic inlet. Engagement is descent of the widest transverse diameter of the presenting part through the pelvic inlet.\",\"The pelvic outlet is bounded by the pubic arch anteriorly, ischial tuberosities laterally, and coccyx posteriorly.\",\"The midpelvis is the plane of the ischial spines and is important for fetal descent.\",\"A presenting part at the ischial spines is at station 0 and is already within the maternal pelvis.\",\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training is first-line for stress urinary incontinence and is usually continued for at least 3 months.\"],\"e\":[\"Engagement is descent of the widest transverse diameter of the presenting part through the pelvic inlet.\",\"In this patient, a ballottable unengaged head remains above the pelvic inlet.\",\"A ballottable fetal head is still mobile above the inlet and has not engaged. This finding is important because a high unengaged presenting part increases concern for malpresentation or cord prolapse if membranes rupture.\"],\"tk\":\"Define engagement and relate ballottement to pelvic level.\",\"pr\":\"A ballottable head is high and mobile rather than engaged.\",\"tp\":\"Avoid artificial rupture of membranes when the presenting part is high unless conditions are controlled.\",\"cn\":\"A ballottable, non-engaged fetal head lies above the pelvic inlet.\",\"sl\":\"Engagement is when the widest diameter of the presenting part has passed through the pelvic inlet, assessed abdominally (rule of fifths) and vaginally (station).\",\"s\":[[\"L15_0\",[3]],[\"L00_0\",[36,39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M05\"},{\"id\":\"M06\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":15,\"ty\":[\"Physio\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Pelvic inlet boundaries\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"The sacral promontory, arcuate lines, pectineal lines, and superior border of the symphysis pubis together define which pelvic structure?\",\"o\":[\"Subpubic arch\",\"Midpelvis\",\"Pelvic cavity\",\"Pelvic inlet\",\"Pelvic outlet\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The subpubic arch is an anterior feature of the pelvic outlet formed by the inferior pubic rami.\",\"The midpelvis is the plane of the ischial spines and is important for fetal descent.\",\"The pelvic cavity lies between the inlet and outlet rather than being defined by the pelvic brim landmarks.\",\"These landmarks form the pelvic inlet. The pelvic inlet is the superior opening of the true pelvis.\",\"The pelvic outlet is bounded by the pubic arch anteriorly, ischial tuberosities laterally, and coccyx posteriorly.\"],\"e\":[\"The pelvic inlet is the superior opening of the true pelvis.\",\"This presentation shows that these landmarks form the pelvic inlet.\",\"It is bounded posteriorly by the sacral promontory, laterally by the arcuate and pectineal lines, and anteriorly by the superior border of the pubic symphysis. Engagement occurs when the presenting part passes through this plane.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the anatomic boundaries of the pelvic inlet.\",\"pr\":\"The pelvic inlet is the first fixed pelvic plane the fetal head must negotiate.\",\"tp\":\"Connect pelvic landmarks with obstetric events such as engagement.\",\"cn\":\"The pelvic inlet is bounded by the sacral promontory, iliopectineal (arcuate and pectineal) lines and upper border of the symphysis pubis.\",\"sl\":\"Pelvic inlet landmarks: upper border of symphysis pubis (anterior), iliopectineal line (lateral), sacral promontory (posterior).\",\"s\":[[\"L00_0\",[39]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M06\"},{\"id\":\"M07\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":20,\"ty\":[\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Bishop score calculation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the Bishop score for a cervix that is 4 cm dilated, fully effaced, soft, anterior, and has the presenting part at station −2?\",\"o\":[\"10\",\"11\",\"8\",\"7\",\"9\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"The component scores add to 10. The Bishop score estimates cervical favorability for induction using dilation, effacement, station, cervical consistency, and cervical position.\",\"A Bishop score of 11 is 1 point above the calculated total; none of the listed cervical components receives an additional point beyond the stated values.\",\"A Bishop score of 8 is 2 points below the calculated total; the combination of full effacement, soft consistency, and anterior position contributes more than this value allows.\",\"A Bishop score of 7 is 3 points below the calculated total because the stated cervix has advanced dilation, complete effacement, soft consistency, anterior position, and station −2.\",\"A Bishop score of 9 is 1 point below the calculated total after adding the station −2 contribution to the other favorable cervical findings.\"],\"e\":[\"The Bishop score estimates cervical favorability for induction using dilation, effacement, station, cervical consistency, and cervical position.\",\"This presentation shows that the component scores add to 10.\",\"Dilation of 3-4 cm scores 2, effacement of at least 80% scores 3, station −2 scores 1, soft consistency scores 2, and anterior position scores 2. The total score is therefore 10.\"],\"tk\":\"Calculate the Bishop score using all five cervical assessment components.\",\"pr\":\"A high Bishop score predicts a more favorable induction.\",\"tp\":\"Break Bishop scoring into its five components before adding the total.\",\"cn\":\"Bishop score: 4 cm (2) + fully effaced (3) + soft (2) + anterior (2) + station -2 (1) = 10.\",\"sl\":\"The Bishop score is based on dilation, effacement (or length), position and consistency of the cervix and station of the head relative to the ischial spines; a score above 6 favours amniotomy and oxytocin.\",\"s\":[[\"L20_0\",[20,21]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M07\"},{\"id\":\"M08\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":16,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Persistent brow presentation delivery\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A persistent brow presentation is diagnosed during active labor in a term pregnancy. What is the most appropriate mode of delivery?\",\"o\":[\"Continue labor with oxytocin augmentation\",\"Perform cesarean delivery\",\"Perform vacuum-assisted delivery\",\"Attempt forceps rotation\",\"Attempt manual conversion to vertex presentation\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oxytocin augments uterine contractions but cannot correct a fixed mechanical obstruction from malpresentation.\",\"Persistent brow presentation at term requires cesarean delivery. Brow presentation presents the mentovertical diameter, the largest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head.\",\"Vacuum-assisted delivery is reserved for selected vertex births and is unsuitable for persistent brow presentation.\",\"Forceps delivery requires a suitable cephalic presentation, full dilation, a known position, and an engaged head.\",\"Manual conversion of persistent brow presentation is not a standard safe corrective maneuver because manipulation can traumatize the fetus or uterus.\"],\"e\":[\"Brow presentation presents the mentovertical diameter, the largest anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head.\",\"In this patient, persistent brow presentation at term requires cesarean delivery.\",\"If brow presentation persists at term, vaginal delivery is generally impossible in a normal pelvis because the head cannot engage safely. Cesarean delivery is the appropriate management for persistent brow presentation.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose safe delivery management for persistent brow presentation.\",\"pr\":\"Persistent brow presentation is a mechanical problem, not an indication for stronger contractions.\",\"tp\":\"Do not attempt instrumental correction of a persistent brow presentation.\",\"cn\":\"Persistent brow presentation cannot deliver vaginally and is managed by caesarean section.\",\"sl\":\"Brow presentation results from partial deflexion of the fetal neck; most convert spontaneously to a flexed attitude; a vaginal delivery of a persistent brow presentation is not possible and it is managed by caesarean section.\",\"s\":[[\"L16_0\",[37]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F496\"},{\"id\":\"M09\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":17,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Pathological cardiotocography\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A cardiotocography tracing shows a normal fetal heart rate baseline, reduced baseline variability, recurrent late decelerations, and absent accelerations. How should this tracing be interpreted?\",\"o\":[\"Pathological tracing requiring urgent assessment\",\"Early deceleration pattern only\",\"Reassuring tracing\",\"Variable deceleration pattern\",\"Normal fetal sleep cycle\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Late decelerations with reduced variability are pathological despite a normal baseline. Fetal heart rate interpretation integrates baseline rate, variability, accelerations, and decelerations.\",\"Early decelerations mirror contractions and usually reflect fetal head compression; they do not cause persistently reduced variability.\",\"A reassuring CTG requires normal baseline variability without recurrent pathological decelerations.\",\"Variable decelerations are abrupt and usually reflect cord compression, whereas late decelerations begin after the contraction peak.\",\"A fetal sleep cycle can transiently reduce variability and accelerations, but recurrent late decelerations require separate assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"Fetal heart rate interpretation integrates baseline rate, variability, accelerations, and decelerations.\",\"This presentation shows that late decelerations with reduced variability are pathological despite a normal baseline.\",\"A normal baseline alone does not make a tracing reassuring when reduced variability and recurrent late decelerations are present. Late decelerations reflect uteroplacental insufficiency and require urgent maternal resuscitative measures and reassessment for expedited delivery if they persist.\"],\"tk\":\"Interpret CTG by combining all features rather than relying only on the baseline rate.\",\"pr\":\"A normal baseline does not compensate for recurrent late decelerations.\",\"tp\":\"Start intrauterine resuscitation and escalate early when late decelerations persist.\",\"cn\":\"Reduced variability with recurrent late decelerations and absent accelerations on cardiotocography (CTG) is a pathological trace needing urgent assessment.\",\"sl\":\"Reduced variability with decelerations should be taken seriously; recurrent decelerations with loss of beat-to-beat variability is a sign of poor prognosis. Sequence in hypoxia: absent accelerations, then loss of variability, then decelerations.\",\"s\":[[\"L17_0\",[13,14,15]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M09\"},{\"id\":\"M10\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmolar chemotherapy indications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT an indication for chemotherapy after evacuation of a molar pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"A plateau in serum beta-hCG over serial measurements\",\"Metastatic gestational trophoblastic disease\",\"A rising serum beta-hCG over serial measurements\",\"Serum beta-hCG below 25 IU/L four weeks after evacuation\",\"Histologic diagnosis of choriocarcinoma\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Quantitative beta-hCG is interpreted serially with transvaginal ultrasound when pregnancy location or viability is uncertain.\",\"Metastatic gestational trophoblastic disease is an indication for chemotherapy after staging because it represents persistent malignant disease.\",\"Quantitative beta-hCG is interpreted serially with transvaginal ultrasound when pregnancy location or viability is uncertain.\",\"Beta-hCG below 25 IU/L at four weeks is reassuring rather than an indication for chemotherapy. Postmolar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia is suspected when beta-hCG plateaus, rises, remains persistently elevated beyond the expected interval, or when choriocarcinoma or metastases are diagnosed.\",\"Choriocarcinoma is malignant gestational trophoblastic neoplasia and requires oncologic staging and chemotherapy.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmolar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia is suspected when beta-hCG plateaus, rises, remains persistently elevated beyond the expected interval, or when choriocarcinoma or metastases are diagnosed.\",\"In this patient, beta-hCG below 25 IU/L at four weeks is reassuring rather than an indication for chemotherapy.\",\"A very low and falling beta-hCG shortly after evacuation suggests remission rather than active neoplasia. Such patients require surveillance, not chemotherapy.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize indications for treatment of postmolar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN).\",\"pr\":\"The trend of beta-hCG matters more than a single isolated value.\",\"tp\":\"Follow beta-hCG after molar evacuation until remission criteria are met.\",\"cn\":\"A beta human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG) below 25 IU/L at 4 weeks is not a chemotherapy indication; 20,000 IU/L or more is.\",\"sl\":\"Indications for chemotherapy: plateaued or rising human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) after evacuation, histological choriocarcinoma, metastases, serum hCG of 20 000 IU/L or more 4 weeks or more after evacuation, raised hCG 6 months after evacuation.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[31,28]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M10\"},{\"id\":\"M11\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Low-risk GTN treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient with gestational trophoblastic neoplasia has an International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)/World Health Organization (WHO) risk score of 5. Which treatment is most appropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Pelvic radiation treatment\",\"Primary hysterectomy without systemic antineoplastic treatment\",\"Multiagent EMA-CO regimen\",\"Observation without serial beta-hCG follow-up\",\"Single-agent methotrexate with folinic acid rescue\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Pelvic radiation treatment is not routine first-line therapy for low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.\",\"Hysterectomy is major surgery reserved for selected definitive indications and is not first-line for uncomplicated functional disease.\",\"EMA-CO is multiagent chemotherapy used for high-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia or resistant disease.\",\"Quantitative beta-hCG is interpreted serially with transvaginal ultrasound when pregnancy location or viability is uncertain.\",\"An International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)/World Health Organization (WHO) score of 5 is low-risk GTN and is treated with single-agent methotrexate-based chemotherapy.\"],\"e\":[\"FIGO/WHO scoring classifies GTN into low-risk and high-risk disease.\",\"In this patient, a FIGO/WHO score of 5 is low-risk GTN and is treated with single-agent methotrexate-based chemotherapy.\",\"Scores of 0-6 are low risk and are usually treated with single-agent chemotherapy, most commonly methotrexate with folinic acid rescue or actinomycin D. Multiagent chemotherapy is reserved for high-risk disease or resistant low-risk disease.\"],\"tk\":\"Select treatment for low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.\",\"pr\":\"Low-risk GTN is highly chemosensitive.\",\"tp\":\"Always calculate the FIGO/WHO score before choosing the GTN regimen.\",\"cn\":\"Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) scoring 6 or less is low risk, treated with single-agent methotrexate plus folinic acid.\",\"sl\":\"Scores of 6 or less are low risk and treated with single-agent intramuscular methotrexate alternating daily with folinic acid for 1 week followed by 6 rest days; 7 or greater are high risk and get EMA-CO.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[35,36]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M11\"},{\"id\":\"M12\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Dx\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Complete hydatidiform mole\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 40-year-old woman presents with first-trimester vaginal bleeding. Serum beta-hCG is 200,000 IU/L, and ultrasound shows a snowstorm pattern with no fetal parts. What is the most likely diagnosis and initial management?\",\"o\":[\"Partial molar pregnancy; medical termination with misoprostol\",\"Missed miscarriage; expectant management\",\"Complete molar pregnancy; suction evacuation\",\"Choriocarcinoma; immediate multiagent systemic treatment\",\"Ectopic pregnancy; methotrexate therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Misoprostol is used for selected medical management of miscarriage, but molar pregnancy is usually evacuated by suction.\",\"Missed miscarriage is a nonviable intrauterine pregnancy retained in the uterus, typically with a closed cervix.\",\"The snowstorm appearance with no fetal parts indicates complete mole, managed by suction evacuation.\",\"Chemotherapy is reserved for confirmed malignant or trophoblastic disease and is not used for a benign simple cyst.\",\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\"],\"e\":[\"Complete hydatidiform mole classically presents with vaginal bleeding, markedly elevated beta-hCG, and ultrasound showing diffuse hydropic villi without fetal parts.\",\"In this patient, the snowstorm appearance with no fetal parts indicates complete mole, managed by suction evacuation.\",\"Initial management in a patient desiring uterine preservation is suction evacuation with careful follow-up of serial beta-hCG. Chemotherapy is not given initially unless gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) criteria develop.\"],\"tk\":\"Diagnose complete molar pregnancy and choose initial management.\",\"pr\":\"Complete mole has no fetal tissue and very high beta-hCG.\",\"tp\":\"After suction evacuation, arrange reliable serial beta-hCG follow-up before discharge.\",\"cn\":\"Very high human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) with a snowstorm ultrasound and no fetal parts indicates complete mole, treated by suction evacuation.\",\"sl\":\"Complete mole ultrasound: snow storm appearance, no identifiable gestational sac, theca lutein cysts; hCG usually greater than 100,000 mIU/mL. Suction curettage is the method of choice; medical termination should be avoided.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[14,18,22]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M12\"},{\"id\":\"M13\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Complication\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Molar evacuation complications\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which of the following is NOT a recognized complication of suction evacuation for molar pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"Uterine perforation\",\"Gestational diabetes mellitus\",\"Infection (endometritis)\",\"Retained molar tissue\",\"Hemorrhage\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Uterine perforation can occur during cervical dilation or uterine instrumentation and may injure adjacent bowel, bladder, or blood vessels.\",\"Gestational diabetes mellitus results from pregnancy-associated insulin resistance and is not a recognized complication of suction evacuation.\",\"Infection of the uterine cavity can follow suction evacuation, as after any uterine instrumentation, so it is a recognised complication.\",\"Retained molar tissue can cause continued bleeding or persistent human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) elevation after evacuation.\",\"Hemorrhage can occur during molar evacuation because the enlarged uterus and trophoblastic tissue are highly vascular.\"],\"e\":[\"Suction evacuation is the preferred fertility-preserving treatment for most molar pregnancies. Recognized complications include hemorrhage, uterine perforation, infection, and incomplete evacuation.\",\"Gestational diabetes mellitus is the exception because it is a metabolic complication of pregnancy rather than a consequence of uterine evacuation.\",\"Uterine instrumentation can cause mechanical injury or introduce bacteria, while residual trophoblastic tissue and inadequate uterine contraction can contribute to bleeding; these mechanisms do not cause gestational diabetes.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize procedural complications of molar evacuation.\",\"pr\":\"A plateauing or rising hCG level after molar evacuation requires assessment for postmolar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia and should not automatically be attributed to retained tissue.\",\"tp\":\"Advise patients to seek urgent assessment for heavy vaginal bleeding, fever, worsening abdominal pain, dizziness, or fainting after evacuation.\",\"cn\":\"Complications of suction evacuation of a molar pregnancy include haemorrhage, uterine perforation and retained tissue, not gestational diabetes.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M13\"},{\"id\":\"M14\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Eclampsia stabilization\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 32-week pregnant patient has a generalized seizure, blood pressure of 170/112 mmHg, and 3+ proteinuria. What is the immediate management?\",\"o\":[\"Diazepam alone without blood pressure control\",\"Immediate cesarean delivery before maternal stabilization\",\"Oral antihypertensive therapy and outpatient review\",\"Intravenous phenytoin without antihypertensive treatment\",\"Intravenous magnesium sulfate plus urgent intravenous antihypertensive therapy\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Phenytoin or diazepam may treat selected seizure disorders, but magnesium sulfate is preferred for eclampsia.\",\"Cesarean delivery is used when vaginal birth is unsafe or urgent birth is required; maternal stabilization still precedes operative delivery when feasible.\",\"Outpatient oral therapy alone is unsafe after an eclamptic seizure with severe-range blood pressure because immediate seizure and blood-pressure control are required.\",\"Phenytoin or diazepam may treat selected seizure disorders, but magnesium sulfate is preferred for eclampsia.\",\"This patient has eclampsia with severe hypertension and needs magnesium sulfate plus IV blood pressure control.\"],\"e\":[\"Eclampsia is the occurrence of seizures in a patient with preeclampsia and is an obstetric emergency.\",\"In this patient, this patient has eclampsia with severe hypertension and needs magnesium sulfate plus IV blood pressure control.\",\"Immediate priorities are airway and maternal stabilization, seizure control with magnesium sulfate, and treatment of severe hypertension with intravenous labetalol, hydralazine, or nifedipine protocols. Delivery is definitive, but it should occur after stabilization unless there is an immediate life-threatening indication.\"],\"tk\":\"Stabilize eclampsia before arranging definitive delivery.\",\"pr\":\"In eclampsia, stabilize the mother first; fetal status usually improves after maternal stabilization.\",\"tp\":\"Have calcium gluconate available whenever magnesium sulfate is administered.\",\"cn\":\"Eclampsia is treated with airway, breathing and circulation (ABC) support, intravenous (IV) magnesium sulphate and urgent IV antihypertensive therapy.\",\"sl\":\"Eclampsia: ABCs (airway, breathing, circulation), seizure control with 6 g magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) intravenous (IV) bolus (extra 2 g if further seizure), refractory seizures with IV phenytoin or benzodiazepine, and control of severe hypertension; delivery after stabilisation.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[54,43]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M14\"},{\"id\":\"M15\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Urgency urinary incontinence\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 60-year-old woman has sudden urgency followed by leakage before she can reach the toilet. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Overflow urinary incontinence\",\"Stress urinary incontinence\",\"Continuous fistula-related incontinence\",\"Functional urinary incontinence\",\"Urge urinary incontinence\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Overflow incontinence occurs with incomplete bladder emptying and an elevated postvoid residual.\",\"Stress urinary incontinence causes leakage with cough, sneeze, exertion, or other increases in intra-abdominal pressure.\",\"Genitourinary fistula classically causes continuous leakage rather than episodic urgency-associated loss.\",\"Functional incontinence results from cognitive or mobility barriers that prevent timely toileting despite a normally functioning lower urinary tract.\",\"Leakage preceded by urgency is urge urinary incontinence. Urge urinary incontinence is involuntary leakage accompanied by or immediately preceded by urgency.\"],\"e\":[\"Urge urinary incontinence is involuntary leakage accompanied by or immediately preceded by urgency.\",\"In this patient, leakage preceded by urgency is urge urinary incontinence.\",\"It is commonly part of overactive bladder syndrome and reflects detrusor overactivity in many patients. Stress incontinence occurs with raised intra-abdominal pressure such as coughing or sneezing.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate urge incontinence from stress and overflow incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Urgency is the key word for urge urinary incontinence.\",\"tp\":\"Ask whether leakage happens with urgency or with cough because management differs.\",\"cn\":\"Urge urinary incontinence (UUI) is involuntary leakage accompanied or immediately preceded by urgency.\",\"sl\":\"Urge urinary incontinence (UUI): involuntary leakage accompanied by or immediately preceded by urgency.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[5]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M15\"},{\"id\":\"M16\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"First-line urge incontinence therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the first-line conservative management for uncomplicated urge urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Mid-urethral sling surgery\",\"Long-term indwelling catheterization\",\"Pelvic floor muscle training alone\",\"Bladder training\",\"Immediate botulinum toxin injection\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A mid-urethral sling is a surgical option for stress urinary incontinence after conservative treatment has been unsuccessful and informed consent is completed.\",\"Long-term indwelling catheterization is reserved for selected chronic retention or palliative situations and is not conservative treatment for uncomplicated urgency incontinence.\",\"Supervised pelvic floor muscle training is first-line for stress urinary incontinence and is usually continued for at least 3 months.\",\"Bladder training is the first-line management. Initial management of urge urinary incontinence and overactive bladder includes lifestyle advice, bladder training, and treatment of reversible causes such as urinary tract infection.\",\"Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A is an escalation option for refractory overactive bladder after conservative and medication therapy.\"],\"e\":[\"Initial management of urge urinary incontinence and overactive bladder includes lifestyle advice, bladder training, and treatment of reversible causes such as urinary tract infection.\",\"This presentation shows that bladder training is the first-line management.\",\"Bladder training aims to progressively increase voiding intervals and suppress urgency. Drug therapy is considered if conservative measures fail or symptoms remain bothersome.\"],\"tk\":\"Select initial treatment for uncomplicated urge urinary incontinence.\",\"pr\":\"Bladder training is first-line for urgency-predominant incontinence.\",\"tp\":\"Review a bladder diary before escalating therapy.\",\"cn\":\"Bladder training for at least 6 weeks is first-line conservative treatment of urge incontinence or overactive bladder (OAB).\",\"sl\":\"Overactive bladder (OAB) conservative management: fluid advice (1-1.5 L/day), reduce caffeine and alcohol, bladder retraining (cure rate 44-90%). National Institute for Health and Care Excellence (NICE): bladder retraining for a minimum of 6 weeks.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[76,84]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"M16\"},{\"id\":\"M17\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Refractory overactive bladder therapy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman with urge urinary incontinence remains symptomatic despite bladder training and appropriate oral medical therapy. Which treatment is an appropriate next escalation?\",\"o\":[\"Cervical cerclage\",\"Mid-urethral sling surgery\",\"Endometrial ablation\",\"Anterior colporrhaphy\",\"Intravesical botulinum toxin injection\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cervical cerclage supports a cervix at risk of preterm dilation and is not a treatment for hemorrhage or urinary symptoms.\",\"A mid-urethral sling is a surgical option for stress urinary incontinence after conservative treatment has been unsuccessful and informed consent is completed.\",\"Endometrial ablation treats selected heavy menstrual bleeding and has no role in refractory overactive bladder.\",\"Anterior colporrhaphy repairs anterior vaginal wall prolapse and does not treat detrusor overactivity.\",\"Botulinum toxin is an accepted escalation for refractory urgency incontinence. Refractory overactive bladder may be treated with third-line therapies such as intradetrusor botulinum toxin injection, sacral neuromodulation, or posterior tibial nerve stimulation.\"],\"e\":[\"Refractory overactive bladder may be treated with third-line therapies such as intradetrusor botulinum toxin injection, sacral neuromodulation, or posterior tibial nerve stimulation.\",\"In this patient, botulinum toxin is an accepted escalation for refractory urgency incontinence.\",\"Botulinum toxin reduces involuntary detrusor contractions but can cause urinary retention and recurrent urinary tract infection. Patients must be counseled about the possible need for intermittent self-catheterization.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize escalation options for refractory overactive bladder.\",\"pr\":\"Botulinum toxin helps the bladder, but it can also make emptying too weak.\",\"tp\":\"Check postvoid residual risk and counsel about self-catheterization before botulinum toxin.\",\"cn\":\"Refractory overactive bladder (OAB) after bladder training and drugs can be treated with intravesical botulinum toxin.\",\"sl\":\"Detrusor overactivity (DO) intravesical therapy: botulinum toxin. Sacral neuromodulation is an alternative in severe, intractable overactive bladder (OAB) before reconstructive surgery.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[80,81]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M17\"},{\"id\":\"M18\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Initial incontinence investigation\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"What is the basic initial investigation for a patient presenting with urinary incontinence?\",\"o\":[\"Urinalysis\",\"Diagnostic laparoscopy\",\"Cystoscopy for all patients\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) measurement\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Urinalysis is the basic first investigation. Initial evaluation of urinary incontinence includes history, examination, bladder diary when useful, and urinalysis.\",\"Diagnostic laparoscopy evaluates intra-abdominal or pelvic pathology and is not part of the initial workup for uncomplicated urinary incontinence.\",\"Cystoscopy is reserved for selected lower urinary tract indications such as hematuria or suspected bladder pathology, not routine uncomplicated incontinence.\",\"Pelvic magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is not a routine first test for uncomplicated urinary incontinence.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) can support ovarian-mass risk assessment, particularly after menopause, but is not a stand-alone diagnostic test.\"],\"e\":[\"Initial evaluation of urinary incontinence includes history, examination, bladder diary when useful, and urinalysis.\",\"In this patient, urinalysis is the basic first investigation.\",\"Urinalysis screens for urinary tract infection, hematuria, glycosuria, and proteinuria, which may change management. More invasive testing is reserved for selected patients or before complex surgery.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose the initial test in urinary incontinence evaluation.\",\"pr\":\"Do not diagnose overactive bladder before excluding infection.\",\"tp\":\"A urine dipstick is a low-cost test that can redirect the entire assessment.\",\"cn\":\"Urine testing (urinalysis) is a basic first investigation for urinary incontinence.\",\"sl\":\"Basic investigations: urine test, bladder diary, pad test, ultrasound scan. Advanced: cystometry, videocystourethrography, ambulatory urodynamics, urethral pressure profilometry, imaging, cystourethroscopy.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[24,25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M18\"},{\"id\":\"M19\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Ix\",\"Criteria\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Detrusor overactivity\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A urodynamic study in a woman with overactive bladder shows involuntary detrusor contractions during bladder filling. What is this finding called?\",\"o\":[\"Low urethral closure pressure\",\"Detrusor overactivity\",\"Intrinsic sphincter deficiency\",\"Bladder outlet obstruction\",\"Acontractile detrusor\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Intrinsic sphincter deficiency causes stress leakage from inadequate urethral closure rather than involuntary detrusor contraction.\",\"Involuntary contractions during filling are detrusor overactivity. Detrusor overactivity is a urodynamic observation characterized by involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase.\",\"Intrinsic sphincter deficiency causes stress leakage from inadequate urethral closure rather than involuntary detrusor contraction.\",\"Bladder outlet obstruction produces impaired emptying and may cause elevated postvoid residual rather than filling-phase detrusor contractions.\",\"An acontractile detrusor fails to generate an effective voiding contraction and is associated with retention.\"],\"e\":[\"Detrusor overactivity is a urodynamic observation characterized by involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase.\",\"In this patient, involuntary contractions during filling are detrusor overactivity.\",\"It is commonly associated with urgency, frequency, nocturia, and urge urinary incontinence. Not every patient with symptoms needs urodynamics, but the finding explains urgency leakage when testing is performed.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify the urodynamic correlate of overactive bladder symptoms.\",\"pr\":\"Symptoms are called overactive bladder; the urodynamic sign is detrusor overactivity.\",\"tp\":\"Reserve urodynamics for uncertain, refractory, or preoperative cases.\",\"cn\":\"Involuntary detrusor contractions during bladder filling on urodynamics are called detrusor overactivity (DO).\",\"sl\":\"Detrusor overactivity (DO): a urodynamic observation characterised by involuntary detrusor contractions during the filling phase which may be spontaneous or provoked.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[18,40]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M19\"},{\"id\":\"M20\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Stable placenta previa management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A hemodynamically stable patient with placenta previa at 32 weeks has painless vaginal spotting and a reassuring nonstress test. What is the most appropriate plan?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate emergency cesarean delivery\",\"Expectant management until 40 weeks without planned delivery\",\"Antenatal corticosteroids with planned cesarean delivery at 36-37 weeks\",\"Digital vaginal examination and induction of labor\",\"External cephalic version at term\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cesarean delivery is used when vaginal birth is unsafe or urgent birth is required; maternal stabilization still precedes operative delivery when feasible.\",\"Waiting until 40 weeks without a planned delivery strategy exposes a placenta previa patient to avoidable hemorrhage risk.\",\"Stable preterm placenta previa is managed expectantly with corticosteroids and planned cesarean delivery.\",\"Digital vaginal examination is avoided in known placenta previa because palpation can provoke severe hemorrhage.\",\"External cephalic version addresses breech presentation and does not treat placenta previa.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa classically causes painless antepartum bleeding.\",\"In this patient, stable preterm placenta previa is managed expectantly with corticosteroids and planned cesarean delivery.\",\"In a stable preterm patient with reassuring fetal assessment, management is expectant with avoidance of digital vaginal examination, antenatal corticosteroids if preterm delivery risk is present, and planned cesarean delivery near 36-37 weeks. Emergency delivery is reserved for maternal instability, heavy bleeding, or nonreassuring fetal status.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage stable placenta previa based on bleeding severity, gestational age, and fetal status.\",\"pr\":\"Never perform a digital vaginal examination when placenta previa is suspected.\",\"tp\":\"In placenta previa, maternal stability and fetal status determine urgency of delivery.\",\"cn\":\"Stable placenta praevia with minor bleeding is managed expectantly with corticosteroids and planned caesarean section around 36-37 weeks.\",\"sl\":\"Offer a single course of antenatal corticosteroids at 24+0 to 34+6 weeks to women at risk of preterm birth. For uncomplicated placenta praevia, elective caesarean section should be considered at 36+0 to 37+0 weeks; late preterm delivery (34+0 to 36+6 weeks) should be considered with a history of vaginal bleeding.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[22,43]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M20\"},{\"id\":\"M21\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Placenta previa recurrence risk\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient has a history of placenta previa and two previous cesarean deliveries. How does this history affect her risk of placenta previa in the current pregnancy?\",\"o\":[\"It increases only the risk of placental abruption, not previa\",\"It increases the risk substantially, approximately tenfold compared with no such history\",\"It lowers the risk because the placenta avoids scar tissue\",\"It increases accreta risk but not placenta previa recurrence\",\"It has no effect on recurrence risk\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Wrong. Previous placenta previa and cesarean deliveries raise the risk of placenta previa itself, not only of placental abruption.\",\"Correct. A previous placenta previa together with prior cesarean deliveries substantially increases the recurrence risk of placenta previa, roughly tenfold, and the risk rises with each additional cesarean scar.\",\"Wrong. A uterine scar does not protect against low implantation; more cesarean scars mean a higher risk of placenta previa and placenta accreta spectrum.\",\"Wrong. This history raises the risk of both placenta accreta spectrum and recurrent placenta previa, not accreta alone.\",\"Wrong. Previous placenta previa and prior cesarean delivery are both established risk factors, so recurrence risk is clearly increased.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa risk increases with previous placenta previa and with prior cesarean delivery.\",\"In this patient, prior placenta previa plus two cesareans substantially increases recurrence risk.\",\"The risk rises as the number of cesarean scars increases because implantation over a lower uterine segment scar is more likely. This history also raises concern for placenta accreta spectrum if the placenta overlies the scar.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize the cumulative effect of prior previa and cesarean scars on abnormal placentation.\",\"pr\":\"The combination of previa and cesarean scar also raises concern for accreta.\",\"tp\":\"Always review placental location carefully in patients with prior cesarean deliveries.\",\"cn\":\"Previous placenta praevia raises the risk of praevia about tenfold, and each prior caesarean section increases it further.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors for placenta praevia: previous placenta praevia (adjusted odds ratio 9.7); previous caesarean section odds ratio (OR) 2.2, two previous OR 4.1, three previous OR 22.4.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[38]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M21\"},{\"id\":\"M22\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Twelve days after cesarean delivery, a patient develops heavy vaginal bleeding, fever, uterine tenderness, and a uterus larger than expected. Which initial investigation best assesses for retained products of conception?\",\"o\":[\"Serum progesterone level\",\"Cervical cytology\",\"Pelvic ultrasound\",\"Abdominal radiography\",\"Hysterosalpingography\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Serum progesterone may help in selected early-pregnancy viability questions but does not assess retained postpartum tissue.\",\"Cervical cytology screens for cervical precancer and does not evaluate pregnancy location or retained intrauterine tissue.\",\"Pelvic ultrasound is the initial investigation for suspected retained products postpartum. Secondary postpartum hemorrhage with fever and uterine tenderness suggests retained products of conception with endometritis until proven otherwise.\",\"Plain abdominal radiography does not visualize retained intrauterine products adequately.\",\"Hysterosalpingography evaluates tubal patency in infertility and is not used to assess retained postpartum tissue.\"],\"e\":[\"Secondary postpartum hemorrhage with fever and uterine tenderness suggests retained products of conception with endometritis until proven otherwise.\",\"In this patient, pelvic ultrasound is the initial investigation for suspected retained products postpartum.\",\"Pelvic ultrasound is the usual initial imaging test to assess for retained intrauterine tissue or clot, while broad-spectrum antibiotics are started if infection is suspected. Hysteroscopy may be diagnostic or therapeutic later, but it is not the basic first investigation in an infected postpartum patient.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate secondary postpartum hemorrhage with suspected infection and retained tissue.\",\"pr\":\"Bleeding plus fever after delivery is retained tissue and infection until proven otherwise.\",\"tp\":\"Treat suspected endometritis promptly while arranging imaging and evacuation if retained tissue is confirmed.\",\"cn\":\"Pelvic ultrasound is the initial test for retained products of conception (RPOC) in secondary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH).\",\"sl\":\"Secondary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) is abnormal bleeding between 24 hours and 12 weeks postnatally; causes include endometritis and retained placental tissue. Pelvic ultrasound may help exclude retained products of conception (RPOC), though diagnosis is unreliable; give clindamycin and gentamicin if endometritis is suspected.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[24,25]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M22\"},{\"id\":\"M23\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Incomplete miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 13-week pregnant patient has vaginal bleeding and lower abdominal pain. Speculum examination shows an open cervical os with fetal tissue, and ultrasound shows retained intrauterine tissue. What is the diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Incomplete miscarriage\",\"Threatened miscarriage\",\"Complete miscarriage\",\"Missed miscarriage\",\"Inevitable miscarriage\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Open os with expelled fetal tissue and retained products indicates incomplete miscarriage. Incomplete miscarriage refers to partial expulsion of products of conception with retained tissue in the uterus.\",\"Threatened miscarriage causes early-pregnancy bleeding with a closed cervical os and an ongoing intrauterine pregnancy.\",\"Complete miscarriage means all products of conception have passed and the uterus is empty.\",\"Missed miscarriage is a nonviable intrauterine pregnancy retained in the uterus, typically with a closed cervix.\",\"Inevitable miscarriage presents with bleeding and an open cervix before expulsion is complete.\"],\"e\":[\"Incomplete miscarriage refers to partial expulsion of products of conception with retained tissue in the uterus.\",\"In this patient, open os with expelled fetal tissue and retained products indicates incomplete miscarriage.\",\"The cervix is often open, bleeding and cramping are present, and ultrasound may show retained products. Management depends on stability, bleeding, infection, gestational age, and patient preference.\"],\"tk\":\"Differentiate incomplete miscarriage from threatened, inevitable, complete, and missed miscarriage.\",\"pr\":\"An open os plus retained products is incomplete miscarriage.\",\"tp\":\"Assess hemodynamic stability and infection before choosing expectant, medical, or surgical management.\",\"cn\":\"Incomplete miscarriage: some products of conception have passed through an open cervical os and some remain in the uterus.\",\"sl\":\"Incomplete miscarriage occurs when some of the products of conception have been passed and some are left in the uterine cavity; diagnosis is clinical if tissue is seen passing through an open cervical os, or by ultrasound.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M23\"},{\"id\":\"M24\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Inevitable miscarriage\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 14-week pregnant patient has vaginal bleeding and lower abdominal pain. Speculum examination shows an open cervical os, but ultrasound shows an intact gestational sac. What is the diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Complete miscarriage\",\"Threatened miscarriage\",\"Incomplete miscarriage\",\"Ectopic pregnancy\",\"Inevitable miscarriage\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Complete miscarriage means all products of conception have passed and the uterus is empty.\",\"Threatened miscarriage causes early-pregnancy bleeding with a closed cervical os and an ongoing intrauterine pregnancy.\",\"Complete miscarriage means all products of conception have passed and the uterus is empty.\",\"Ectopic pregnancy must be considered when pregnancy location is unknown, especially with pain, bleeding, or hemodynamic instability.\",\"Bleeding with an open os and intact sac is inevitable miscarriage. Inevitable miscarriage is diagnosed when vaginal bleeding occurs with an open cervical os but the products of conception have not yet been expelled.\"],\"e\":[\"Inevitable miscarriage is diagnosed when vaginal bleeding occurs with an open cervical os but the products of conception have not yet been expelled.\",\"In this patient, bleeding with an open os and intact sac is inevitable miscarriage.\",\"An intact gestational sac may still be seen on ultrasound. Threatened miscarriage has a closed cervix, while incomplete miscarriage has partial expulsion with retained tissue.\"],\"tk\":\"Use cervical os status and ultrasound findings to classify miscarriage.\",\"pr\":\"An open cervix means the pregnancy is no longer simply threatened.\",\"tp\":\"Document whether tissue has passed because it distinguishes inevitable from incomplete miscarriage.\",\"cn\":\"Inevitable miscarriage: bleeding with an open cervical os while the gestational sac is still intact in the uterus.\",\"sl\":\"\",\"s\":[],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M24\"},{\"id\":\"M25\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Septic miscarriage management\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 7-week pregnant patient has fever, purulent vaginal discharge, lower abdominal pain, and an open cervical os. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Oral doxycycline alone without evacuation\",\"Expectant management until spontaneous passage\",\"Immediate broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics followed by uterine evacuation\",\"Outpatient oral analgesia only\",\"Methotrexate therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oral doxycycline alone is inadequate for septic miscarriage, which requires broad-spectrum parenteral therapy and source control.\",\"Expectant management is unsafe when fever, purulent discharge, and pelvic pain indicate infected retained products.\",\"Septic miscarriage requires intravenous (IV) antibiotics and uterine evacuation. Septic miscarriage is suggested by fever, uterine or pelvic pain, purulent discharge, and an open cervix in early pregnancy.\",\"Analgesia alone provides no source control or antimicrobial coverage for septic miscarriage.\",\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\"],\"e\":[\"Septic miscarriage is suggested by fever, uterine or pelvic pain, purulent discharge, and an open cervix in early pregnancy.\",\"In this patient, septic miscarriage requires IV antibiotics and uterine evacuation.\",\"Management requires urgent broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics, resuscitation as needed, and evacuation of infected retained products after antibiotic coverage has begun. Delay increases the risk of sepsis, hemorrhage, and disseminated infection.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage septic miscarriage as an emergency.\",\"pr\":\"In septic miscarriage, antibiotics come first but source control is also essential.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay senior review for fever and purulent discharge in early pregnancy loss.\",\"cn\":\"Septic miscarriage requires admission for intravenous antibiotics followed by surgical evacuation of the uterus.\",\"sl\":\"Septic miscarriage: retained products of conception (RPOC) may become infected, presenting with malaise, offensive vaginal discharge and fever; management is admission for intravenous antibiotics and surgical evacuation of the uterus.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M25\"},{\"id\":\"M26\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Pregnancy of unknown location\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 26-year-old nulliparous patient presents with early pregnancy bleeding and lower abdominal pain. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an empty uterus. What is the next step?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate oxytocin infusion\",\"Routine Pap smear\",\"Empiric systemic antineoplastic treatment\",\"Quantitative serum beta-hCG measurement\",\"Repeat transvaginal ultrasound in 6 months\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Oxytocin augments uterine contractions but cannot correct a fixed mechanical obstruction from malpresentation.\",\"Cervical cytology screens for cervical precancer and does not evaluate pregnancy location or retained intrauterine tissue.\",\"Chemotherapy is reserved for confirmed malignant or trophoblastic disease and is not used for a benign simple cyst.\",\"Quantitative beta-hCG is needed to evaluate pregnancy of unknown location. Early pregnancy bleeding with an empty uterus on ultrasound is managed as pregnancy of unknown location until intrauterine pregnancy, early pregnancy loss, or ectopic pregnancy is clarified.\",\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\"],\"e\":[\"Early pregnancy bleeding with an empty uterus on ultrasound is managed as pregnancy of unknown location until intrauterine pregnancy, early pregnancy loss, or ectopic pregnancy is clarified.\",\"In this patient, quantitative beta-hCG is needed to evaluate pregnancy of unknown location.\",\"Quantitative serum beta-hCG helps interpret whether an intrauterine gestational sac should be visible and guides repeat testing and imaging. Ectopic pregnancy must be considered until excluded.\"],\"tk\":\"Use beta-hCG and ultrasound together in early pregnancy bleeding.\",\"pr\":\"An empty uterus does not exclude ectopic pregnancy.\",\"tp\":\"Arrange follow-up beta-hCG and safety-net ectopic warning symptoms.\",\"cn\":\"Pregnancy of unknown location (PUL) is assessed with serial quantitative serum human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG) 48 hours apart.\",\"sl\":\"Pregnancy of unknown location (PUL): positive pregnancy test without intrauterine or extrauterine pregnancy on transvaginal scan (TVS); manage with serum hCG repeated in 48 hours and repeat TVS when hCG is 1000 IU/L or more.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M26\"},{\"id\":\"M27\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":1,\"L\":27,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Bleeding after confirmed intrauterine pregnancy\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A pregnant patient has vaginal bleeding and lower abdominal pain. Two weeks earlier, ultrasound confirmed an intrauterine gestational sac. What is the next step?\",\"o\":[\"Chest radiography\",\"No assessment until 20 weeks\",\"Serum cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) measurement\",\"Repeat pelvic ultrasound\",\"Empiric methotrexate without repeat imaging\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Chest radiography does not assess viability of a previously documented intrauterine gestation.\",\"Deferring assessment until 20 weeks risks missing early pregnancy loss or another complication causing current bleeding and pain.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) can support ovarian-mass risk assessment, particularly after menopause, but is not a stand-alone diagnostic test.\",\"Repeat pelvic ultrasound evaluates current viability and miscarriage status. When a prior intrauterine gestational sac has been documented, new bleeding and pain require reassessment of pregnancy viability and miscarriage status.\",\"Methotrexate is used for selected ectopic pregnancies and low-risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia, not for routine miscarriage or urinary symptoms.\"],\"e\":[\"When a prior intrauterine gestational sac has been documented, new bleeding and pain require reassessment of pregnancy viability and miscarriage status.\",\"In this patient, repeat pelvic ultrasound evaluates current viability and miscarriage status.\",\"Repeat pelvic ultrasound can assess fetal cardiac activity, growth, sac morphology, and retained products if tissue has passed. Serial beta-hCG may support management but ultrasound is the key immediate reassessment.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose repeat ultrasound when symptoms develop after a previously documented intrauterine gestation.\",\"pr\":\"A previous sac does not guarantee ongoing viability.\",\"tp\":\"Reassess with ultrasound rather than relying only on old imaging.\",\"cn\":\"Bleeding with pain after a confirmed intrauterine pregnancy needs repeat ultrasound to assess viability and type of miscarriage.\",\"sl\":\"Threatened miscarriage: any vaginal bleeding before 24 completed weeks; management: ultrasound (the majority will have a viable pregnancy), blood group and Rhesus (Rh), reassurance, speculum to rule out local causes and confirm a closed cervix.\",\"s\":[[\"L27_0\",[11]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M27\"},{\"id\":\"M28\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":9,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Refractory uterine atony at cesarean\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"During cesarean delivery, the patient develops 1800 mL postpartum hemorrhage after delivery of the fetus and placenta. The uterus is atonic and does not respond to uterotonic medications. What is the next appropriate surgical step?\",\"o\":[\"Cervical cerclage\",\"B-Lynch uterine compression suture\",\"Endometrial biopsy\",\"Manual rotation of the fetal head\",\"Uterine curettage for presumed atony\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Cervical cerclage supports a cervix at risk of preterm dilation and is not a treatment for hemorrhage or urinary symptoms.\",\"Refractory atony at cesarean is appropriately treated with a B-Lynch compression suture. Uterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"Endometrial biopsy samples the uterine lining for hyperplasia or malignancy when age, risk factors, or persistent abnormal bleeding warrant sampling.\",\"Manual rotation is an intrapartum maneuver for selected fetal head malpositions.\",\"Uterine curettage treats retained intrauterine tissue, not primary uterine atony without evidence of retained products.\"],\"e\":[\"Uterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage.\",\"In this patient, refractory atony at cesarean is appropriately treated with a B-Lynch compression suture.\",\"Initial treatment includes uterine massage and uterotonic medications, but persistent atony during cesarean delivery can be treated with uterine compression sutures such as the B-Lynch suture. If bleeding continues, escalation may include uterine artery ligation, internal iliac ligation, balloon tamponade, or hysterectomy depending on the situation.\"],\"tk\":\"Escalate postpartum hemorrhage management stepwise while resuscitation continues.\",\"pr\":\"A B-Lynch suture mechanically compresses an atonic uterus.\",\"tp\":\"Call for help early in postpartum hemorrhage and move through the hemorrhage protocol.\",\"cn\":\"B-Lynch compression suture is suitable for atonic postpartum haemorrhage (PPH) unresponsive to uterotonics when the uterus is open at caesarean section.\",\"sl\":\"Intrauterine balloon tamponade is the first-line surgical intervention for atony; the B-Lynch haemostatic brace suture requires hysterotomy and is particularly suitable when the uterus is opened for caesarean section.\",\"s\":[[\"L09_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M28\"},{\"id\":\"M29\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":4,\"L\":8,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Unstable placenta previa\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A patient with known placenta previa presents with severe vaginal bleeding, heart rate 120/min, blood pressure 90/65 mmHg, and fetal heart rate 100/min. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Discharge after reassurance\",\"Tranexamic acid alone followed by observation\",\"Wait for spontaneous vaginal delivery\",\"Digital vaginal examination to assess dilation\",\"Maternal resuscitation followed by emergency cesarean delivery\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Reassurance or observation is appropriate only when the clinical and imaging findings indicate a low-risk condition that does not require urgent intervention.\",\"Tranexamic acid can be an adjunct in major hemorrhage, but it cannot replace resuscitation and definitive delivery when both mother and fetus are compromised.\",\"Waiting for spontaneous vaginal delivery is unsafe when placenta previa causes maternal shock and fetal compromise.\",\"Digital vaginal examination is contraindicated in placenta previa because it can trigger catastrophic bleeding.\",\"Severe bleeding with maternal and fetal compromise requires resuscitation and emergency cesarean delivery.\"],\"e\":[\"Placenta previa can cause major antepartum hemorrhage.\",\"In this patient, severe bleeding with maternal and fetal compromise requires resuscitation and emergency cesarean delivery.\",\"Maternal instability and fetal bradycardia require simultaneous resuscitation and preparation for emergency cesarean delivery. Digital vaginal examination is contraindicated because it can worsen bleeding.\"],\"tk\":\"Prioritize maternal stabilization and urgent delivery in unstable placenta previa.\",\"pr\":\"In obstetric hemorrhage, maternal resuscitation is fetal resuscitation.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay emergency delivery when placenta previa causes shock and fetal bradycardia.\",\"cn\":\"Placenta praevia with maternal shock and fetal compromise needs resuscitation and immediate caesarean delivery.\",\"sl\":\"Primary survey follows airway, breathing and circulation (ABC) with resuscitation. Women with antepartum haemorrhage (APH) and maternal and/or fetal compromise are required to be delivered immediately; if the fetus is compromised, caesarean section with concurrent maternal resuscitation.\",\"s\":[[\"L08_0\",[12,34,44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M29\"},{\"id\":\"M30\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":2,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmolar hCG surveillance\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"After evacuation of a complete molar pregnancy, beta-hCG normalizes within 56 days. What follow-up schedule is appropriate?\",\"o\":[\"Monthly beta-hCG monitoring for 6 months from normalization\",\"Monthly beta-hCG monitoring for 6 months from evacuation\",\"No further beta-hCG follow-up is required\",\"Weekly beta-hCG monitoring for 6 months from normalization\",\"Fortnightly beta-hCG for 1 month after normalization\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Counting 6 months from normalization is used when hCG normalization is delayed beyond the early post-evacuation interval, not when it normalizes within 56 days.\",\"Normalization within 56 days leads to monthly follow-up for 6 months from evacuation. Postmolar follow-up uses serial beta-hCG to confirm remission and detect persistent gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN).\",\"Stopping surveillance immediately after normalization can miss postmolar GTN, so a defined period of serial hCG follow-up is still required.\",\"Continuing weekly hCG for 6 months is more intensive than the surveillance schedule used after prompt normalization; follow-up frequency decreases once remission is confirmed.\",\"A 1-month fortnightly schedule is shorter than the recommended surveillance interval after a complete mole.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmolar follow-up uses serial beta-hCG to confirm remission and detect persistent GTN.\",\"In this patient, normalization within 56 days leads to monthly follow-up for 6 months from evacuation.\",\"In many protocols, if beta-hCG normalizes within 56 days after evacuation of a complete mole, monitoring continues for 6 months from the date of evacuation. If normalization is delayed, follow-up is usually counted from normalization.\"],\"tk\":\"Apply postmolar beta-hCG surveillance rules.\",\"pr\":\"The timing of beta-hCG normalization determines the follow-up clock.\",\"tp\":\"Advise reliable contraception during surveillance so beta-hCG interpretation remains clear.\",\"cn\":\"If human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) normalises within 56 days of evacuating a complete mole, monitor monthly until 6 months from evacuation.\",\"sl\":\"Weekly hCG until three consecutive normal values. If normalised within 56 days of evacuation, measure monthly for 6 months from the date of evacuation; if more than 56 days, monthly for 6 months from the date hCG became normal.\",\"s\":[[\"L02_0\",[26]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M30\"},{\"id\":\"M31\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":8,\"L\":36,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":1,\"ti\":\"Antimuscarinic therapy for overactive bladder\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which medication is an antimuscarinic option for overactive bladder syndrome?\",\"o\":[\"Mirabegron\",\"Oxybutynin\",\"Duloxetine\",\"Furosemide\",\"Desmopressin\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Mirabegron is a beta-3 adrenergic agonist for overactive bladder rather than an antimuscarinic.\",\"Oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic used for overactive bladder. Overactive bladder is characterized by urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urge incontinence.\",\"Duloxetine has a role in selected stress urinary incontinence settings but is not an antimuscarinic.\",\"Furosemide is a loop diuretic used for volume overload rather than as primary therapy for overactive bladder or uncomplicated severe preeclampsia.\",\"Desmopressin reduces urine production and is used in selected nocturia or diabetes insipidus settings, not as an antimuscarinic.\"],\"e\":[\"Overactive bladder is characterized by urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without urge incontinence.\",\"This presentation shows that oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic used for overactive bladder.\",\"Antimuscarinic drugs such as oxybutynin reduce detrusor contractions by blocking muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. Common adverse effects include dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision, and cognitive effects in susceptible older patients.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify drug therapy for overactive bladder and its adverse effects.\",\"pr\":\"Oxybutynin treats urgency but often causes dry mouth.\",\"tp\":\"Review anticholinergic burden before prescribing oxybutynin to older patients.\",\"cn\":\"Oxybutynin is an antimuscarinic for overactive bladder (OAB); mirabegron is a beta-3 agonist, not an antimuscarinic.\",\"sl\":\"Overactive bladder (OAB) antimuscarinics: oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin, darifenacin, trospium, fesoterodine. Mirabegron is a beta-3 agonist (not antimuscarinic), 50 mg once daily.\",\"s\":[[\"L36_0\",[77,79]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M31\"},{\"id\":\"M32\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Simple premenopausal ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 35-year-old premenopausal woman is incidentally found to have a simple 4 cm ovarian cyst and is asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy\",\"Reassurance without urgent intervention\",\"Multiagent systemic antineoplastic treatment\",\"Emergency detorsion\",\"Serial cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) every 2 weeks\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Hysterectomy is major surgery reserved for selected definitive indications and is not first-line for uncomplicated functional disease.\",\"A simple 4 cm cyst in a premenopausal patient is low risk and can be reassured. Simple ovarian cysts in premenopausal women are commonly functional and often resolve spontaneously.\",\"Chemotherapy is reserved for confirmed malignant or trophoblastic disease and is not used for a benign simple cyst.\",\"Urgent ovarian detorsion is indicated when torsion is suspected to preserve ovarian perfusion.\",\"Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) can support ovarian-mass risk assessment, particularly after menopause, but is not a stand-alone diagnostic test.\"],\"e\":[\"Simple ovarian cysts in premenopausal women are commonly functional and often resolve spontaneously.\",\"In this patient, a simple 4 cm cyst in a premenopausal patient is low risk and can be reassured.\",\"An asymptomatic simple cyst around 4 cm with benign ultrasound features is usually managed conservatively with reassurance or limited follow-up depending on local protocol. Complex features, persistence, symptoms, or high-risk markers require further evaluation.\"],\"tk\":\"Manage simple premenopausal ovarian cysts according to size and ultrasound morphology.\",\"pr\":\"A simple cyst is not managed like a complex solid ovarian mass.\",\"tp\":\"Describe cyst morphology clearly because management depends on it.\",\"cn\":\"A simple ovarian cyst under 50 mm in a premenopausal woman is likely physiological and needs no follow-up.\",\"sl\":\"Simple ovarian cysts under 50 mm do not require follow-up as they are very likely physiological and almost always resolve within 3 menstrual cycles; 50-70 mm should have yearly ultrasound follow-up.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[16]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M32\"},{\"id\":\"M33\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":41,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Postmenopausal suspicious adnexal mass\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 69-year-old postmenopausal woman has a multiloculated ovarian cyst and cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) of 150 U/mL. What is the most appropriate next step?\",\"o\":[\"Computed tomography (CT) imaging and referral to a gynecologic oncology multidisciplinary team\",\"Combined oral contraceptive therapy for 3 months\",\"Repeat ultrasound in 2 years\",\"Reassurance only\",\"Empiric antibiotics\"],\"a\":0,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Postmenopausal complex cyst with elevated CA-125 requires oncologic evaluation. Postmenopausal ovarian masses require risk stratification using menopausal status, ultrasound features, and CA-125.\",\"Combined oral contraceptives do not treat a suspicious postmenopausal adnexal mass and should not delay oncologic evaluation.\",\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\",\"Reassurance or observation is appropriate only when the clinical and imaging findings indicate a low-risk condition that does not require urgent intervention.\",\"Empiric antibiotics are used for suspected infection, not for a multiloculated postmenopausal ovarian mass with tumor-marker elevation.\"],\"e\":[\"Postmenopausal ovarian masses require risk stratification using menopausal status, ultrasound features, and CA-125.\",\"In this patient, postmenopausal complex cyst with elevated CA-125 requires oncologic evaluation.\",\"A multiloculated cyst with elevated CA-125 is concerning for malignancy and usually warrants CT staging and referral to a gynecologic oncology team. Conservative follow-up is reserved for low-risk simple cysts with normal CA-125.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize high-risk postmenopausal ovarian mass features.\",\"pr\":\"Complex morphology plus raised CA-125 in a postmenopausal patient is high risk.\",\"tp\":\"Refer suspicious adnexal masses to gynecologic oncology before definitive surgery.\",\"cn\":\"A postmenopausal multilocular ovarian cyst with raised cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) needs computed tomography (CT) and gynaecological oncology multidisciplinary team (MDT) referral.\",\"sl\":\"A cyst in a postmenopausal woman is a tumour until proven otherwise: ultrasound, CA-125, CT of thorax, abdomen and pelvis (TAP) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Work up: imaging, histology, MDT - always.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[20]],[\"L41_0\",[16,23]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M33\"},{\"id\":\"M34\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Ovarian torsion\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A woman develops sudden severe left pelvic pain with vomiting. Doppler ultrasound shows absent venous flow to the ovary. What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Analgesia and discharge without detorsion\",\"Endometrial biopsy\",\"Laparoscopic or open ovarian detorsion\",\"Six-month ultrasound follow-up\",\"Oral antimuscarinic therapy\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Urgent ovarian detorsion is indicated when torsion is suspected to preserve ovarian perfusion.\",\"Endometrial biopsy samples the uterine lining for hyperplasia or malignancy when age, risk factors, or persistent abnormal bleeding warrant sampling.\",\"The symptoms and absent venous flow indicate ovarian torsion requiring detorsion. Ovarian torsion presents with sudden unilateral pelvic pain, often with nausea and vomiting.\",\"Ultrasound is a first-line imaging modality for many obstetric and gynecologic conditions because it directly evaluates pelvic or intrauterine anatomy.\",\"Antimuscarinic therapy treats overactive bladder and has no role in an acutely ischemic adnexa.\"],\"e\":[\"Ovarian torsion presents with sudden unilateral pelvic pain, often with nausea and vomiting.\",\"In this patient, the symptoms and absent venous flow indicate ovarian torsion requiring detorsion.\",\"Venous and lymphatic obstruction occur first, so absent venous flow on Doppler supports the diagnosis, although normal flow does not exclude torsion. Urgent surgical detorsion is needed to preserve ovarian function and prevent necrosis.\"],\"tk\":\"Identify ovarian torsion and treat it surgically without delay.\",\"pr\":\"Ovarian torsion is a surgical emergency even if arterial flow is still present.\",\"tp\":\"Do not delay laparoscopy for repeated imaging when torsion is clinically likely.\",\"cn\":\"Ovarian torsion, suggested by sudden unilateral pain, vomiting and absent venous Doppler flow, needs urgent laparoscopic detorsion.\",\"sl\":\"Torsion presents with sudden sharp unilateral lower abdominal pain, with nausea and vomiting in 70%. Doppler commonly shows little or no intra-ovarian venous flow. Treatment is laparoscopy to uncoil the torted ovary, possibly oophoropexy.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[25,26,27]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":2,\"rs\":\"F303\"},{\"id\":\"M35\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":7,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Simple postmenopausal ovarian cyst\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A postmenopausal woman has an asymptomatic simple 4 cm ovarian cyst and undetectable cancer antigen 125 (CA-125). What is the most appropriate management?\",\"o\":[\"Immediate systemic antineoplastic treatment\",\"No documentation or follow-up\",\"Emergency oophorectomy tonight\",\"Annual CA-125 measurement only\",\"Follow-up ultrasound and CA-125 in 4-6 months\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Chemotherapy is reserved for confirmed malignant or trophoblastic disease and is not used for a benign simple cyst.\",\"A postmenopausal adnexal cyst should have documented risk assessment and follow-up rather than being ignored.\",\"Emergency oophorectomy is unnecessary for an asymptomatic simple postmenopausal cyst with reassuring tumor-marker assessment and no torsion.\",\"CA-125 can support ovarian-mass risk assessment, particularly after menopause, but is not a stand-alone diagnostic test.\",\"Low-risk postmenopausal simple cysts can be followed with repeat ultrasound and CA-125. A simple unilateral ovarian cyst smaller than 5 cm with normal CA-125 in a postmenopausal patient has a low risk of malignancy.\"],\"e\":[\"A simple unilateral ovarian cyst smaller than 5 cm with normal CA-125 in a postmenopausal patient has a low risk of malignancy.\",\"In this patient, low-risk postmenopausal simple cysts can be followed with repeat ultrasound and CA-125.\",\"Conservative management with repeat ultrasound and CA-125 in about 4-6 months is appropriate if the cyst has benign features and the patient is asymptomatic. Complex features or increasing size require referral.\"],\"tk\":\"Choose conservative follow-up for low-risk postmenopausal ovarian cysts.\",\"pr\":\"In postmenopause, simple cysts still need documented follow-up.\",\"tp\":\"Use CA-125 with ultrasound morphology, not as a standalone screening test.\",\"cn\":\"A postmenopausal simple cyst under 5 cm with normal cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) is followed with ultrasound and CA-125 in 4-6 months.\",\"sl\":\"A cyst in a postmenopausal woman is a tumour until proven otherwise; assess with history, examination including pelvic ultrasound and cancer antigen 125 (CA-125). The 4-6 month follow-up interval is not on the slides.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[20]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M35\"},{\"id\":\"M36\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":9,\"L\":40,\"ty\":[\"Ix\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Suspicious premenopausal adnexal mass\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 35-year-old woman has a multiloculated ovarian mass with a solid component on ultrasound. What is the most appropriate next evaluation?\",\"o\":[\"Reassurance only\",\"Bladder training\",\"Endometrial ablation\",\"Immediate bilateral oophorectomy without staging\",\"Pregnancy and germ-cell tumor markers including beta-hCG, alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), with specialist referral if suspicious\"],\"a\":4,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Reassurance or observation is appropriate only when the clinical and imaging findings indicate a low-risk condition that does not require urgent intervention.\",\"Bladder training is first-line behavioral treatment for urgency-predominant urinary incontinence and overactive bladder.\",\"Endometrial ablation treats selected heavy menstrual bleeding and does not evaluate a suspicious ovarian mass.\",\"Immediate bilateral oophorectomy without staging can compromise fertility and oncologic planning; suspicious masses require appropriate markers and specialist evaluation first.\",\"A complex solid ovarian mass in a young patient requires tumor-marker evaluation and specialist assessment.\"],\"e\":[\"A complex adnexal mass with multiloculation and solid components requires further evaluation rather than reassurance.\",\"In this patient, a complex solid ovarian mass in a young patient requires tumor-marker evaluation and specialist assessment.\",\"In a reproductive-age patient, pregnancy testing and tumor markers such as beta-hCG, AFP, LDH, and cancer antigen 125 (CA-125) help assess germ-cell and epithelial tumor risk, while suspicious morphology warrants specialist referral and surgical planning. Management depends on risk, symptoms, fertility goals, and imaging features.\"],\"tk\":\"Evaluate complex ovarian masses using age-appropriate tumor markers and imaging risk features.\",\"pr\":\"Solid components make an ovarian cyst complex until proven otherwise.\",\"tp\":\"Check pregnancy status and germ-cell markers in younger patients with complex adnexal masses.\",\"cn\":\"Under 40, a complex ovarian mass needs lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and beta human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) testing.\",\"sl\":\"Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) and beta human chorionic gonadotropin (bhCG) should be measured in all women under age 40 with a complex ovarian mass because of the possibility of germ cell tumours.\",\"s\":[[\"L40_0\",[12]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M36\"},{\"id\":\"M37\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Severe preeclampsia diagnosis\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"A 42-year-old primigravida with systemic lupus erythematosus presents with blood pressure 170/110 mmHg and epigastric pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?\",\"o\":[\"Gestational hypertension without severe features\",\"Normal pregnancy physiology\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum\",\"Preeclampsia with severe features\",\"Eclampsia\"],\"a\":3,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Gestational hypertension lacks the end-organ severe features that distinguish severe preeclampsia.\",\"Normal pregnancy physiology does not produce sustained blood pressure of 170/110 mmHg with epigastric pain.\",\"Hyperemesis gravidarum causes severe nausea, vomiting, dehydration, and weight loss rather than severe-range hypertension.\",\"Severe hypertension with epigastric pain is preeclampsia with severe features. Preeclampsia is new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ features.\",\"Eclampsia requires a generalized tonic-clonic seizure in a patient with preeclampsia; severe features without seizure remain preeclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Preeclampsia is new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks with proteinuria or end-organ features.\",\"In this patient, severe hypertension with epigastric pain is preeclampsia with severe features.\",\"Severe-range blood pressure and epigastric or right upper quadrant pain suggest severe features and risk of complications such as eclampsia, stroke, and haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets (HELLP) syndrome. SLE, advanced maternal age, and nulliparity increase preeclampsia risk.\"],\"tk\":\"Recognize severe features of preeclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"Epigastric pain in preeclampsia may reflect hepatic involvement.\",\"tp\":\"Treat severe-range blood pressure urgently while evaluating labs and fetal status.\",\"cn\":\"Blood pressure 160/110 mmHg or more with epigastric pain after 20 weeks indicates pre-eclampsia with severe features.\",\"sl\":\"Pre-eclampsia with severe features: persistent blood pressure of 160 systolic or 110 diastolic, or signs such as persistent epigastric or right upper quadrant pain and cerebral or visual disturbances. Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and age 40 years are risk factors.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[36,37,32]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M37\"},{\"id\":\"M38\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\",\"Mx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Acute severe hypertension treatment\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"In a pregnant patient with severe-range hypertension due to preeclampsia, which intravenous medication is an appropriate first-line antihypertensive?\",\"o\":[\"Intravenous furosemide as sole therapy\",\"Intravenous labetalol\",\"Intravenous enalapril\",\"Intravenous captopril\",\"Intravenous propranolol\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Furosemide is a loop diuretic used for volume overload rather than as primary therapy for overactive bladder or uncomplicated severe preeclampsia.\",\"IV labetalol is an accepted first-line treatment for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy. Acute severe hypertension in pregnancy requires urgent treatment to reduce the risk of maternal stroke.\",\"ACE inhibitors are fetotoxic during pregnancy and are avoided for antenatal blood-pressure treatment.\",\"ACE inhibitors are fetotoxic during pregnancy and are avoided for antenatal blood-pressure treatment.\",\"Propranolol is not a standard first-line agent for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy.\"],\"e\":[\"Acute severe hypertension in pregnancy requires urgent treatment to reduce the risk of maternal stroke.\",\"In this patient, iV labetalol is an accepted first-line treatment for acute severe hypertension in pregnancy.\",\"Intravenous labetalol and hydralazine are commonly used first-line agents, and oral immediate-release nifedipine is another accepted option. ACE inhibitors such as enalapril are contraindicated during pregnancy.\"],\"tk\":\"Select safe acute antihypertensive therapy in severe preeclampsia.\",\"pr\":\"The goal is to prevent stroke without causing sudden uteroplacental hypoperfusion.\",\"tp\":\"Recheck blood pressure promptly after each acute antihypertensive dose.\",\"cn\":\"Intravenous (IV) labetalol (or hydralazine) is first-line for severe hypertension in pre-eclampsia; angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors are contraindicated.\",\"sl\":\"Intravenous (IV) antihypertensive treatment: labetalol 20 mg IV bolus, then 40, 80 and 80 mg up to 220 mg at 10-minute intervals; hydralazine 5 mg IV boluses up to 15 mg; oral immediate-release nifedipine if no IV access.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[44]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M38\"},{\"id\":\"M39\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Risk\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Preeclampsia risk factors\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which factor is NOT a classic risk factor for preeclampsia in a 42-year-old primigravida with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)?\",\"o\":[\"Multifetal gestation\",\"Use of immunosuppressive medication\",\"Systemic lupus erythematosus\",\"Advanced maternal age\",\"Nulliparity\"],\"a\":1,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Multifetal gestation is a moderate risk factor for preeclampsia.\",\"Immunosuppressive therapy itself is not the classic risk factor; SLE is. Important preeclampsia risk factors include nulliparity, advanced maternal age, chronic hypertension, renal disease, autoimmune disease such as SLE, multifetal gestation, diabetes, and prior preeclampsia.\",\"Systemic lupus erythematosus is a high-risk condition for preeclampsia.\",\"Maternal age 40 years or older is a moderate risk factor for preeclampsia.\",\"Nulliparity is a moderate risk factor for preeclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Important preeclampsia risk factors include nulliparity, advanced maternal age, chronic hypertension, renal disease, autoimmune disease such as SLE, multifetal gestation, diabetes, and prior preeclampsia.\",\"In this patient, immunosuppressive therapy itself is not the classic risk factor; SLE is.\",\"Immunosuppressive medication use itself is not a classic independent risk factor, although it may indicate underlying autoimmune disease. The underlying disease is the relevant risk factor.\"],\"tk\":\"Distinguish disease-based preeclampsia risk factors from treatment exposures.\",\"pr\":\"SLE is a risk factor for preeclampsia even if the disease is controlled.\",\"tp\":\"When reviewing risk, separate the underlying condition from the medications used to treat it.\",\"cn\":\"Pre-eclampsia risk factors include nulliparity, multiple gestation, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and age 40 or more; immunosuppressant use is not.\",\"sl\":\"Risk factors: nulliparity, multiple gestation, obesity (body mass index [BMI] 35 or more), chronic hypertension, pregestational diabetes, chronic kidney disease, systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) or antiphospholipid syndrome (APLS), previous or family history, molar pregnancy, assisted reproduction and advanced maternal age (40 years).\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[32,34]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M39\"},{\"id\":\"M40\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":2,\"L\":1,\"ty\":[\"Pharm\"],\"lv\":3,\"ti\":\"Magnesium sulfate regimen\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Which magnesium sulfate regimen is commonly used for seizure prophylaxis or treatment in severe preeclampsia or eclampsia?\",\"o\":[\"20 mg intravenous bolus followed by no monitoring\",\"2 g intravenous loading dose without maintenance infusion\",\"4-6 g intravenous loading dose followed by 1-2 g/hour infusion\",\"10 g intravenous bolus every hour\",\"500 mg orally once daily\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"A single 20 mg intravenous bolus without monitoring does not provide the loading-plus-maintenance exposure used for eclampsia prevention.\",\"A 2 g loading dose without maintenance is below the standard loading dose and does not maintain therapeutic anticonvulsant exposure.\",\"The standard IV regimen uses a 4-6 g loading dose followed by 1-2 g/hour. Magnesium sulfate is the drug of choice for prevention and treatment of eclamptic seizures.\",\"Repeated 10 g intravenous boluses every hour would create an excessive magnesium load and a high risk of respiratory depression and cardiac toxicity.\",\"An oral 500 mg once-daily dose is not a therapeutic loading-and-maintenance anticonvulsant protocol for seizure prophylaxis in severe preeclampsia.\"],\"e\":[\"Magnesium sulfate is the drug of choice for prevention and treatment of eclamptic seizures.\",\"This presentation shows that the standard IV regimen uses a 4-6 g loading dose followed by 1-2 g/hour.\",\"A common regimen is a 4-6 g intravenous loading dose followed by a continuous infusion of 1-2 g/hour, with monitoring of respiratory rate, reflexes, urine output, and serum levels when indicated. Toxicity is treated with calcium gluconate.\"],\"tk\":\"Know magnesium sulfate dosing and toxicity monitoring.\",\"pr\":\"Magnesium prevents seizures; antihypertensives prevent stroke.\",\"tp\":\"Monitor reflexes, respirations, and urine output during magnesium infusion.\",\"cn\":\"Magnesium sulphate regimen: 4-6 g intravenous (IV) loading dose then 1-2 g/hour infusion, typically for 24 hours.\",\"sl\":\"Magnesium sulphate dose: 4 g bolus over 5-15 minutes, then 1 g/h infusion for 24 hours. In eclampsia seizure control with a 6 g intravenous (IV) bolus, with an additional 2 g if seizures recur.\",\"s\":[[\"L01_0\",[45,54]]],\"f\":\"\",\"n\":\"\",\"rp\":1,\"rs\":\"M40\"},{\"id\":\"M41\",\"k\":\"M\",\"b\":\"vein\",\"g\":3,\"L\":23,\"ty\":[\"Dx\"],\"lv\":2,\"ti\":\"Monochorionic diamniotic twins\",\"c\":\"\",\"q\":\"Ultrasound in a twin pregnancy shows two fetuses, one placenta, and a thin two-layer intertwin membrane. What is the chorionicity and amnionicity?\",\"o\":[\"Dichorionic monoamniotic\",\"Dichorionic diamniotic\",\"Monochorionic diamniotic\",\"Monoamniotic twin pregnancy with a dividing membrane\",\"Monochorionic monoamniotic\"],\"a\":2,\"oa\":null,\"ox\":[\"Dichorionic monoamniotic is not a viable chorionicity-amnionicity combination because separate chorions imply separate amniotic sacs.\",\"Dichorionic diamniotic twins usually have two placentas and a thicker intertwin membrane with a lambda sign.\",\"One plac";